const Phase5Data = {"source":"phase-5-note.md","sourceSha256":"caf906edbc8d80708bf29f40dea8a070db3b9559caa8cf5cf5a0f85c9616df4f","counts":{"sections":65,"tables":25,"codeBlocks":48,"diagrams":6,"equations":0,"inlineMath":0,"stepVisuals":30},"sections":[{"key":"overview","id":"overview","title":"Phase 5 — Web GIS and Mapping: From PostGIS to an Editable Web Map","anchor":"phase-5--web-gis-and-mapping-from-postgis-to-an-editable-web-map","depth":1,"start":0,"topics":["stack"],"end":226,"markdown":"# Phase 5 — Web GIS and Mapping: From PostGIS to an Editable Web Map\n\n> **Study note** · Level: developer who already knows CRS basics, data formats, GDAL/GeoPandas and PostGIS (Phases 1–4) · Estimated effort: 4–8 weeks\n\n---\n\n","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":6,"html":"<h2>Phase 5 — Web GIS and Mapping: From PostGIS to an Editable Web Map</h2>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Study note</strong> · Level: developer who already knows CRS basics, data formats, GDAL/GeoPandas and PostGIS (Phases 1–4) · Estimated effort: 4–8 weeks</p>\n</blockquote>\n<hr>\n","sha256":"e3f1f5095b274da4f1ab1fb090e83e0b109a1af1a5d1c26e7940fabe320f4bae"},{"key":"1","id":"1","title":"1. Title and learning objectives","anchor":"1-title-and-learning-objectives","depth":2,"start":226,"topics":["stack"],"end":263,"markdown":"## 1. Title and learning objectives\n\n","lineStart":7,"lineEnd":8,"html":"<h2>1. Title and learning objectives</h2>\n","sha256":"f0513840c10ab338fc76ddba5617a3d03f0537d0f14b4fe3d1a1c76ff53fb252"},{"key":"1.1","id":"1-1","title":"1.1 Scope","anchor":"11-scope","depth":3,"start":263,"topics":["stack"],"end":522,"markdown":"### 1.1 Scope\n\nThis note covers the complete **web GIS stack**: how spatial data stored in PostGIS is turned into tiles and features, published through standard APIs, rendered in a browser, edited by users, and protected by authentication and authorization.\n\n","lineStart":9,"lineEnd":12,"html":"<h2>1.1 Scope</h2>\n<p>This note covers the complete <strong>web GIS stack</strong>: how spatial data stored in PostGIS is turned into tiles and features, published through standard APIs, rendered in a browser, edited by users, and protected by authentication and authorization.</p>\n","sha256":"70f9b174e0e3b0df73ff605ac2deeb8b8de84b773730ec4775989606b7c1754b"},{"key":"1.2","id":"1-2","title":"1.2 Prerequisites","anchor":"12-prerequisites","depth":3,"start":522,"topics":["stack"],"end":1016,"markdown":"### 1.2 Prerequisites\n\n| Area | You should already be able to… |\n|---|---|\n| CRS | Explain EPSG:4326 vs EPSG:3857, axis order, and reproject with `ST_Transform` |\n| Formats | Distinguish GeoJSON, GeoPackage, MVT, PMTiles, XYZ vs TMS |\n| Standards | Describe WMS, WMTS, WFS and OGC API Features/Tiles at a high level |\n| Databases | Create geometry columns, GiST indexes, and write `ST_Intersects` / `&&` queries |\n| Web | Read JavaScript (ES modules), JSON, HTTP headers; run Docker Compose |\n\n","lineStart":13,"lineEnd":22,"html":"<h2>1.2 Prerequisites</h2>\n<div class=\"source-table\" role=\"region\" tabindex=\"0\" aria-label=\"Table in 1.2 Prerequisites\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Area</th>\n<th>You should already be able to…</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>CRS</td>\n<td>Explain EPSG:4326 vs EPSG:3857, axis order, and reproject with <code>ST_Transform</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Formats</td>\n<td>Distinguish GeoJSON, GeoPackage, MVT, PMTiles, XYZ vs TMS</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Standards</td>\n<td>Describe WMS, WMTS, WFS and OGC API Features/Tiles at a high level</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Databases</td>\n<td>Create geometry columns, GiST indexes, and write <code>ST_Intersects</code> / <code>&amp;&amp;</code> queries</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Web</td>\n<td>Read JavaScript (ES modules), JSON, HTTP headers; run Docker Compose</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>","sha256":"b1bb27344cef1880923f74c03bb716e5dc56e8ae3840e3df242a290562e86d81"},{"key":"1.3","id":"1-3","title":"1.3 Learning objectives","anchor":"13-learning-objectives","depth":3,"start":1016,"topics":["stack"],"end":2203,"markdown":"### 1.3 Learning objectives\n\nAfter working through this note you will be able to:\n\n1. **Choose** a map library (OpenLayers, Leaflet, MapLibre GL JS, CesiumJS, deck.gl) for a given requirement and justify the choice.\n2. **Compute** Web Mercator tile coordinates, tile bounds and ground resolution by hand and in code.\n3. **Generate** Mapbox Vector Tiles (MVT) from PostGIS (`ST_TileEnvelope`, `ST_AsMVTGeom`, `ST_AsMVT`) and pre-generate them with Tippecanoe.\n4. **Deploy** a tile server (pg_tileserv or Martin) and a feature server (pg_featureserv), and explain where GeoServer, MapServer, QGIS Server and GeoWebCache fit.\n5. **Style** vector tiles with MapLibre expressions, and add clustering, heatmaps and drawing/editing tools.\n6. **Optimise** delivery with simplification, level-of-detail (LOD) rules, tile-size budgets and caching.\n7. **Expose** data through an OGC API – Features–style interface and build an **editing workflow** with validation and optimistic concurrency.\n8. **Secure** spatial data with layer-level and row-level security (PostgreSQL RLS), and avoid tile-cache leaks.\n9. **Deliver** the capstone: PostGIS → tile/feature server → web client → editing workflow.\n\n","lineStart":23,"lineEnd":36,"html":"<h2>1.3 Learning objectives</h2>\n<p>After working through this note you will be able to:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Choose</strong> a map library (OpenLayers, Leaflet, MapLibre GL JS, CesiumJS, deck.gl) for a given requirement and justify the choice.</li>\n<li><strong>Compute</strong> Web Mercator tile coordinates, tile bounds and ground resolution by hand and in code.</li>\n<li><strong>Generate</strong> Mapbox Vector Tiles (MVT) from PostGIS (<code>ST_TileEnvelope</code>, <code>ST_AsMVTGeom</code>, <code>ST_AsMVT</code>) and pre-generate them with Tippecanoe.</li>\n<li><strong>Deploy</strong> a tile server (pg_tileserv or Martin) and a feature server (pg_featureserv), and explain where GeoServer, MapServer, QGIS Server and GeoWebCache fit.</li>\n<li><strong>Style</strong> vector tiles with MapLibre expressions, and add clustering, heatmaps and drawing/editing tools.</li>\n<li><strong>Optimise</strong> delivery with simplification, level-of-detail (LOD) rules, tile-size budgets and caching.</li>\n<li><strong>Expose</strong> data through an OGC API – Features–style interface and build an <strong>editing workflow</strong> with validation and optimistic concurrency.</li>\n<li><strong>Secure</strong> spatial data with layer-level and row-level security (PostgreSQL RLS), and avoid tile-cache leaks.</li>\n<li><strong>Deliver</strong> the capstone: PostGIS → tile/feature server → web client → editing workflow.</li>\n</ol>\n","sha256":"679192ac986efbd3b2a3c2cc93efe28780f955e91c4c0ba8d39cf7133f233677"},{"key":"1.4","id":"1-4","title":"1.4 Suggested 8-week plan (compress to 4 weeks by skipping the optional items)","anchor":"14-suggested-8-week-plan-compress-to-4-weeks-by-skipping-the-optional-items","depth":3,"start":2203,"topics":["stack"],"end":3036,"markdown":"### 1.4 Suggested 8-week plan (compress to 4 weeks by skipping the optional items)\n\n| Week | Focus | Output |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Tile math, Web Mercator, XYZ/TMS, OGC service families | `tile_math.py`, Exercise 1 |\n| 2 | Map libraries: MapLibre GL JS first, then OpenLayers and Leaflet side by side | Same dataset in 2 libraries |\n| 3 | MVT from PostGIS; pg_tileserv / Martin | Working vector-tile layer |\n| 4 | Styling, clustering, heatmaps; feature server + OGC API Features | Exercise 2 |\n| 5 | Performance: simplification, LOD, Tippecanoe, caching, GeoWebCache | Exercise 3 |\n| 6 | Drawing/editing tools; WFS-T vs custom REST; optimistic concurrency | Editing prototype |\n| 7 | AuthN/AuthZ, RLS, cache-safe security | Exercise 4 (part) |\n| 8 | Integration, tests, documentation (optional: Cesium 3D, deck.gl) | Capstone |\n\n---\n\n","lineStart":37,"lineEnd":51,"html":"<h2>1.4 Suggested 8-week plan (compress to 4 weeks by skipping the optional items)</h2>\n<div class=\"source-table\" role=\"region\" tabindex=\"0\" aria-label=\"Table in 1.4 Suggested 8-week plan (compress to 4 weeks by skipping the optional items)\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Week</th>\n<th>Focus</th>\n<th>Output</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Tile math, Web Mercator, XYZ/TMS, OGC service families</td>\n<td><code>tile_math.py</code>, Exercise 1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Map libraries: MapLibre GL JS first, then OpenLayers and Leaflet side by side</td>\n<td>Same dataset in 2 libraries</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>MVT from PostGIS; pg_tileserv / Martin</td>\n<td>Working vector-tile layer</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Styling, clustering, heatmaps; feature server + OGC API Features</td>\n<td>Exercise 2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>Performance: simplification, LOD, Tippecanoe, caching, GeoWebCache</td>\n<td>Exercise 3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>Drawing/editing tools; WFS-T vs custom REST; optimistic concurrency</td>\n<td>Editing prototype</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>AuthN/AuthZ, RLS, cache-safe security</td>\n<td>Exercise 4 (part)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>8</td>\n<td>Integration, tests, documentation (optional: Cesium 3D, deck.gl)</td>\n<td>Capstone</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div><hr>\n","sha256":"d4352b6503b6af2828874b29ef651259266a13d39a8144104869ac7cf2884f55"},{"key":"2","id":"2","title":"2. Conceptual foundations","anchor":"2-conceptual-foundations","depth":2,"start":3036,"topics":["stack"],"end":3066,"markdown":"## 2. Conceptual foundations\n\n","lineStart":52,"lineEnd":53,"html":"<h2>2. Conceptual foundations</h2>\n","sha256":"ff33d2c3293ab1c8f6586ef6073a7ba8c5982f51641dc8bc9f4a388b689de38e"},{"key":"2.1","id":"2-1","title":"2.1 What \"Web GIS\" means","anchor":"21-what-web-gis-means","depth":3,"start":3066,"topics":["stack"],"end":3681,"markdown":"### 2.1 What \"Web GIS\" means\n\nA **web GIS** is a GIS whose data, processing and presentation are delivered over HTTP. It is normally split into tiers:\n\n- **Data tier** — a spatial database (PostGIS) or files (GeoPackage, GeoTIFF).\n- **Service tier** — servers that translate database content into web-friendly responses: *tiles*, *features*, or *rendered map images*.\n- **Client tier** — a browser application that renders responses with a map library and lets users interact and edit.\n- **Cross-cutting concerns** — authentication (who are you?), authorization (what may you see/change?), caching, observability.\n\n","lineStart":54,"lineEnd":62,"html":"<h2>2.1 What &quot;Web GIS&quot; means</h2>\n<p>A <strong>web GIS</strong> is a GIS whose data, processing and presentation are delivered over HTTP. It is normally split into tiers:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Data tier</strong> — a spatial database (PostGIS) or files (GeoPackage, GeoTIFF).</li>\n<li><strong>Service tier</strong> — servers that translate database content into web-friendly responses: <em>tiles</em>, <em>features</em>, or <em>rendered map images</em>.</li>\n<li><strong>Client tier</strong> — a browser application that renders responses with a map library and lets users interact and edit.</li>\n<li><strong>Cross-cutting concerns</strong> — authentication (who are you?), authorization (what may you see/change?), caching, observability.</li>\n</ul>\n","sha256":"efedcc7843de42fc86fe681df360dbea84fb7ba8543026689becb2318d3eaf9b"},{"key":"2.2","id":"2-2","title":"2.2 Three ways to ship geometry to a browser","anchor":"22-three-ways-to-ship-geometry-to-a-browser","depth":3,"start":3681,"topics":["stack"],"end":4806,"markdown":"### 2.2 Three ways to ship geometry to a browser\n\n| Approach | What travels over the wire | Rendering happens… | Typical protocols | Strengths | Weaknesses |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **Rendered raster maps** | PNG/JPEG images of the map | On the server | WMS, WMTS, XYZ raster tiles | Server controls cartography; trivial client; huge datasets fine | Fixed styling; heavy server CPU; blurry on HiDPI/rotation |\n| **Vector tiles** | Compressed binary geometry + attributes per tile | In the client (WebGL/Canvas) | MVT over XYZ, OGC API – Tiles, WMTS | Client-side styling, smooth zoom/rotate, small payloads | Needs tile generation; geometry is simplified/clipped per zoom |\n| **Features (vector data)** | GeoJSON/GML/FlatGeobuf for features | In the client | WFS, OGC API – Features | Full geometry and attributes; query/edit friendly | Does not scale to millions of features without paging/filtering |\n\nRule of thumb: **tiles for display, features for query and edit.** Most production apps combine both: vector tiles draw the map; clicking an object calls a feature API to retrieve or modify the authoritative record.\n\n","lineStart":63,"lineEnd":72,"html":"<h2>2.2 Three ways to ship geometry to a browser</h2>\n<div class=\"source-table\" role=\"region\" tabindex=\"0\" aria-label=\"Table in 2.2 Three ways to ship geometry to a browser\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Approach</th>\n<th>What travels over the wire</th>\n<th>Rendering happens…</th>\n<th>Typical protocols</th>\n<th>Strengths</th>\n<th>Weaknesses</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Rendered raster maps</strong></td>\n<td>PNG/JPEG images of the map</td>\n<td>On the server</td>\n<td>WMS, WMTS, XYZ raster tiles</td>\n<td>Server controls cartography; trivial client; huge datasets fine</td>\n<td>Fixed styling; heavy server CPU; blurry on HiDPI/rotation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Vector tiles</strong></td>\n<td>Compressed binary geometry + attributes per tile</td>\n<td>In the client (WebGL/Canvas)</td>\n<td>MVT over XYZ, OGC API – Tiles, WMTS</td>\n<td>Client-side styling, smooth zoom/rotate, small payloads</td>\n<td>Needs tile generation; geometry is simplified/clipped per zoom</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Features (vector data)</strong></td>\n<td>GeoJSON/GML/FlatGeobuf for features</td>\n<td>In the client</td>\n<td>WFS, OGC API – Features</td>\n<td>Full geometry and attributes; query/edit friendly</td>\n<td>Does not scale to millions of features without paging/filtering</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div><p>Rule of thumb: <strong>tiles for display, features for query and edit.</strong> Most production apps combine both: vector tiles draw the map; clicking an object calls a feature API to retrieve or modify the authoritative record.</p>\n","sha256":"d0aff06ea70629fb664d0ddb4653ce2ac4425a040c71e04a9338352ce878d538"},{"key":"2.3","id":"2-3","title":"2.3 Key terminology","anchor":"23-key-terminology","depth":3,"start":4806,"topics":["stack"],"end":5988,"markdown":"### 2.3 Key terminology\n\n- **Slippy map / XYZ scheme** — a pyramid of square tiles addressed by zoom `z`, column `x` and row `y`; origin at the **top-left** (north-west), `y` increasing southwards.\n- **TMS (Tile Map Service) scheme** — same pyramid but `y` increases **northwards** (origin bottom-left). Mixing the two flips maps vertically: `y_tms = 2^z − 1 − y_xyz`.\n- **Web Mercator (EPSG:3857)** — a spherical Mercator projection used by nearly all web basemaps. Units are metres; valid latitude is about ±85.0511287798066°.\n- **MVT (Mapbox Vector Tile)** — a Protocol Buffers–encoded tile containing named layers of features with integer tile-local coordinates (default extent 4096).\n- **Level of detail (LOD)** — showing less detail (fewer features, simpler geometry) at low zoom.\n- **Clustering** — replacing nearby points by one aggregate marker with a count.\n- **WFS-T** — WFS with *Transaction* operations (Insert/Update/Delete).\n- **Row-level security (RLS)** — PostgreSQL policies that restrict which rows a role can read or write.\n- **Conformance class** — a testable group of requirements a server declares it supports (used by OGC API standards via `/conformance`).\n\n","lineStart":73,"lineEnd":84,"html":"<h2>2.3 Key terminology</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Slippy map / XYZ scheme</strong> — a pyramid of square tiles addressed by zoom <code>z</code>, column <code>x</code> and row <code>y</code>; origin at the <strong>top-left</strong> (north-west), <code>y</code> increasing southwards.</li>\n<li><strong>TMS (Tile Map Service) scheme</strong> — same pyramid but <code>y</code> increases <strong>northwards</strong> (origin bottom-left). Mixing the two flips maps vertically: <code>y_tms = 2^z − 1 − y_xyz</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Web Mercator (EPSG:3857)</strong> — a spherical Mercator projection used by nearly all web basemaps. Units are metres; valid latitude is about ±85.0511287798066°.</li>\n<li><strong>MVT (Mapbox Vector Tile)</strong> — a Protocol Buffers–encoded tile containing named layers of features with integer tile-local coordinates (default extent 4096).</li>\n<li><strong>Level of detail (LOD)</strong> — showing less detail (fewer features, simpler geometry) at low zoom.</li>\n<li><strong>Clustering</strong> — replacing nearby points by one aggregate marker with a count.</li>\n<li><strong>WFS-T</strong> — WFS with <em>Transaction</em> operations (Insert/Update/Delete).</li>\n<li><strong>Row-level security (RLS)</strong> — PostgreSQL policies that restrict which rows a role can read or write.</li>\n<li><strong>Conformance class</strong> — a testable group of requirements a server declares it supports (used by OGC API standards via <code>/conformance</code>).</li>\n</ul>\n","sha256":"7c4fcdfa569acf1448a641d8c243981121423a24822d546d6ead60eb7f4c42b5"},{"key":"2.4","id":"2-4","title":"2.4 The OGC service families at a glance","anchor":"24-the-ogc-service-families-at-a-glance","depth":3,"start":5988,"topics":["services"],"end":6929,"markdown":"### 2.4 The OGC service families at a glance\n\n| Family | Returns | Request style | Status / note |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **WMS** | Rendered map image | `GetMap`, `GetFeatureInfo`, `GetCapabilities` (KVP/XML) | Ubiquitous; legacy but still widely required |\n| **WMTS** | Pre-rendered tiles | KVP, RESTful URL templates | Tile-cache friendly |\n| **WFS** (+T) | Features (GML/GeoJSON), transactions | `GetFeature`, `Transaction` | Rich filtering; XML heavy |\n| **OGC API – Features** | Features as JSON/GeoJSON via REST | `/collections/{id}/items` | Part 1 (Core) and Part 2 (CRS by reference) are approved standards; Part 3 (Filtering, CQL2) has been adopted; Part 4 (Create, Replace, Update, Delete) has been developed as a draft/candidate standard — **verify its current status on ogc.org before relying on it** |\n| **OGC API – Tiles** | Tiles in any tile matrix set | `/collections/{id}/tiles/{tms}/{z}/{row}/{col}` | Part 1 Core approved |\n\n","lineStart":85,"lineEnd":94,"html":"<h2>2.4 The OGC service families at a glance</h2>\n<div class=\"source-table\" role=\"region\" tabindex=\"0\" aria-label=\"Table in 2.4 The OGC service families at a glance\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Family</th>\n<th>Returns</th>\n<th>Request style</th>\n<th>Status / note</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>WMS</strong></td>\n<td>Rendered map image</td>\n<td><code>GetMap</code>, <code>GetFeatureInfo</code>, <code>GetCapabilities</code> (KVP/XML)</td>\n<td>Ubiquitous; legacy but still widely required</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>WMTS</strong></td>\n<td>Pre-rendered tiles</td>\n<td>KVP, RESTful URL templates</td>\n<td>Tile-cache friendly</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>WFS</strong> (+T)</td>\n<td>Features (GML/GeoJSON), transactions</td>\n<td><code>GetFeature</code>, <code>Transaction</code></td>\n<td>Rich filtering; XML heavy</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>OGC API – Features</strong></td>\n<td>Features as JSON/GeoJSON via REST</td>\n<td><code>/collections/{id}/items</code></td>\n<td>Part 1 (Core) and Part 2 (CRS by reference) are approved standards; Part 3 (Filtering, CQL2) has been adopted; Part 4 (Create, Replace, Update, Delete) has been developed as a draft/candidate standard — <strong>verify its current status on ogc.org before relying on it</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>OGC API – Tiles</strong></td>\n<td>Tiles in any tile matrix set</td>\n<td><code>/collections/{id}/tiles/{tms}/{z}/{row}/{col}</code></td>\n<td>Part 1 Core approved</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>","sha256":"dfdb4f5f55f4c607fbad7d0fc8c6e84ec6a352e4e38e33766c6675cb271c137a"},{"key":"2.5","id":"2-5","title":"2.5 The map-library landscape","anchor":"25-the-map-library-landscape","depth":3,"start":6929,"topics":["services"],"end":7909,"markdown":"### 2.5 The map-library landscape\n\n| Library | Rendering | Sweet spot | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Leaflet** | DOM/Canvas (raster-centric) | Simple maps, quick prototypes, rich plugin ecosystem | Vector tiles and rotation need plugins; no native WebGL |\n| **OpenLayers** | Canvas / WebGL | Standards-heavy GIS apps: WMS/WMTS/WFS, many CRSs via proj4, editing interactions (`Draw`, `Modify`, `Snap`) | Steeper learning curve, extremely complete |\n| **MapLibre GL JS** | WebGL | Vector tiles, smooth 60 fps maps, data-driven styling via the MapLibre style spec | Open-source continuation of Mapbox GL JS v1; Web Mercator-centred (recent versions add a globe view — check release notes) |\n| **CesiumJS** | WebGL | 3D globe, terrain, 3D Tiles, time-dynamic data | WGS84 ellipsoid; heavier |\n| **deck.gl** | WebGL (GPU layers) | Large-scale analytical visualisation (millions of points, arcs, hexbins) | Usually an **overlay** on MapLibre/Google Maps; not a full GIS client |\n\n---\n\n","lineStart":95,"lineEnd":106,"html":"<h2>2.5 The map-library landscape</h2>\n<div class=\"source-table\" role=\"region\" tabindex=\"0\" aria-label=\"Table in 2.5 The map-library landscape\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Library</th>\n<th>Rendering</th>\n<th>Sweet spot</th>\n<th>Notes</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Leaflet</strong></td>\n<td>DOM/Canvas (raster-centric)</td>\n<td>Simple maps, quick prototypes, rich plugin ecosystem</td>\n<td>Vector tiles and rotation need plugins; no native WebGL</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>OpenLayers</strong></td>\n<td>Canvas / WebGL</td>\n<td>Standards-heavy GIS apps: WMS/WMTS/WFS, many CRSs via proj4, editing interactions (<code>Draw</code>, <code>Modify</code>, <code>Snap</code>)</td>\n<td>Steeper learning curve, extremely complete</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>MapLibre GL JS</strong></td>\n<td>WebGL</td>\n<td>Vector tiles, smooth 60 fps maps, data-driven styling via the MapLibre style spec</td>\n<td>Open-source continuation of Mapbox GL JS v1; Web Mercator-centred (recent versions add a globe view — check release notes)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>CesiumJS</strong></td>\n<td>WebGL</td>\n<td>3D globe, terrain, 3D Tiles, time-dynamic data</td>\n<td>WGS84 ellipsoid; heavier</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>deck.gl</strong></td>\n<td>WebGL (GPU layers)</td>\n<td>Large-scale analytical visualisation (millions of points, arcs, hexbins)</td>\n<td>Usually an <strong>overlay</strong> on MapLibre/Google Maps; not a full GIS client</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div><hr>\n","sha256":"fdf74922ec4e53e5599b6c7f6197fb439beb4f7c37a03ba3fd5327040cedaeac"},{"key":"3","id":"3","title":"3. Visual explanations","anchor":"3-visual-explanations","depth":2,"start":7909,"topics":["stack"],"end":7999,"markdown":"## 3. Visual explanations\n\n> These diagrams are **schematics**, not scale-accurate maps.\n\n","lineStart":107,"lineEnd":110,"html":"<h2>3. Visual explanations</h2>\n<blockquote>\n<p>These diagrams are <strong>schematics</strong>, not scale-accurate maps.</p>\n</blockquote>\n","sha256":"a10fd347dcb9d5a0afe6b6ac46ac05254c75834d534778510c57f18f2f2807b7"},{"key":"3.1","id":"3-1","title":"3.1 Conceptual overview of the stack","anchor":"31-conceptual-overview-of-the-stack","depth":3,"start":7999,"topics":["stack"],"end":9219,"markdown":"### 3.1 Conceptual overview of the stack\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n    subgraph SRC[\"Data sources\"]\n        S1[\"Field edits and apps\"]\n        S2[\"Open data: OSM, GeoPackage, GeoJSON\"]\n    end\n    subgraph DB[\"Data tier\"]\n        PG[(\"PostGIS\")]\n    end\n    subgraph SVC[\"Service tier\"]\n        TS[\"Tile server: pg_tileserv or Martin\"]\n        FS[\"Feature server: pg_featureserv\"]\n        MS[\"Map servers: GeoServer, MapServer, QGIS Server\"]\n        PRE[\"Pre-built tiles: Tippecanoe or tilemaker to MBTiles or PMTiles\"]\n        EDIT[\"Edit API: validation and transactions\"]\n    end\n    subgraph EDGE[\"Edge\"]\n        GW[\"Reverse proxy: auth, CORS, cache or CDN\"]\n    end\n    subgraph CLI[\"Client tier\"]\n        MAP[\"Web map: MapLibre, OpenLayers, Leaflet, Cesium, deck.gl\"]\n    end\n    S1 --> EDIT --> PG\n    S2 --> PG\n    S2 --> PRE\n    PG --> TS\n    PG --> FS\n    PG --> MS\n    GW --> TS\n    GW --> FS\n    GW --> MS\n    GW --> EDIT\n    PRE --> GW\n    MAP <--> GW\n```\n\n**How to read it:** reads flow from PostGIS through a server to the browser; writes flow through a dedicated *Edit API* so validation and authorization are enforced in one place. The gateway is where TLS, authentication, CORS and caching are applied.\n\n","lineStart":111,"lineEnd":150,"html":"<h2>3.1 Conceptual overview of the stack</h2>\n<figure class=\"source-diagram\"><a href=\"assets/figures/phase5-source-3-1.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/figures/phase5-source-3-1.svg\" alt=\"3.1 Conceptual overview of the stack — source diagram\" loading=\"lazy\"></a><figcaption>Source diagram · <a href=\"assets/figures/phase5-source-3-1.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Enlarge ↗</a></figcaption></figure><details class=\"source-code\"><summary>Mermaid definition</summary><pre><code class=\"language-mermaid\">flowchart LR\n    subgraph SRC[&quot;Data sources&quot;]\n        S1[&quot;Field edits and apps&quot;]\n        S2[&quot;Open data: OSM, GeoPackage, GeoJSON&quot;]\n    end\n    subgraph DB[&quot;Data tier&quot;]\n        PG[(&quot;PostGIS&quot;)]\n    end\n    subgraph SVC[&quot;Service tier&quot;]\n        TS[&quot;Tile server: pg_tileserv or Martin&quot;]\n        FS[&quot;Feature server: pg_featureserv&quot;]\n        MS[&quot;Map servers: GeoServer, MapServer, QGIS Server&quot;]\n        PRE[&quot;Pre-built tiles: Tippecanoe or tilemaker to MBTiles or PMTiles&quot;]\n        EDIT[&quot;Edit API: validation and transactions&quot;]\n    end\n    subgraph EDGE[&quot;Edge&quot;]\n        GW[&quot;Reverse proxy: auth, CORS, cache or CDN&quot;]\n    end\n    subgraph CLI[&quot;Client tier&quot;]\n        MAP[&quot;Web map: MapLibre, OpenLayers, Leaflet, Cesium, deck.gl&quot;]\n    end\n    S1 --&gt; EDIT --&gt; PG\n    S2 --&gt; PG\n    S2 --&gt; PRE\n    PG --&gt; TS\n    PG --&gt; FS\n    PG --&gt; MS\n    GW --&gt; TS\n    GW --&gt; FS\n    GW --&gt; MS\n    GW --&gt; EDIT\n    PRE --&gt; GW\n    MAP &lt;--&gt; GW</code></pre></details><p><strong>How to read it:</strong> reads flow from PostGIS through a server to the browser; writes flow through a dedicated <em>Edit API</em> so validation and authorization are enforced in one place. The gateway is where TLS, authentication, CORS and caching are applied.</p>\n","sha256":"233e8970019124ae8241a7f0ab4936e26e5dd73fbdf0439626ce12d6432b9b2b"},{"key":"3.2","id":"3-2","title":"3.2 Tile pyramid (ASCII schematic, XYZ scheme)","anchor":"32-tile-pyramid-ascii-schematic-xyz-scheme","depth":3,"start":9219,"topics":["tile-math"],"end":9842,"markdown":"### 3.2 Tile pyramid (ASCII schematic, XYZ scheme)\n\n```\nz=0   1 tile            z=1   4 tiles           z=2   16 tiles\n+-------+               +---+---+               +--+--+--+--+\n|       |               |0/0|1/0|   x →         |  |  |  |  |\n| 0/0/0 |               +---+---+               +--+--+--+--+\n|       |               |0/1|1/1|               |  |  |  |  |\n+-------+               +---+---+               +--+--+--+--+\n                          ↓ y increases south     ...\nTile count at zoom z = 4^z       Each tile splits into 4 children (quadtree)\nLabel format in this diagram: x/y     Full address: z/x/y\n```\n\n","lineStart":151,"lineEnd":164,"html":"<h2>3.2 Tile pyramid (ASCII schematic, XYZ scheme)</h2>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">z=0   1 tile            z=1   4 tiles           z=2   16 tiles\n+-------+               +---+---+               +--+--+--+--+\n|       |               |0/0|1/0|   x →         |  |  |  |  |\n| 0/0/0 |               +---+---+               +--+--+--+--+\n|       |               |0/1|1/1|               |  |  |  |  |\n+-------+               +---+---+               +--+--+--+--+\n                          ↓ y increases south     ...\nTile count at zoom z = 4^z       Each tile splits into 4 children (quadtree)\nLabel format in this diagram: x/y     Full address: z/x/y</code></pre>","sha256":"d091195c75c967066012e0f0b874110fe93826dc1a01dc85edb7f2929a6ed734"},{"key":"3.3","id":"3-3","title":"3.3 Sequence: lifecycle of a vector-tile request","anchor":"33-sequence-lifecycle-of-a-vector-tile-request","depth":3,"start":9842,"topics":["performance"],"end":10486,"markdown":"### 3.3 Sequence: lifecycle of a vector-tile request\n\n```mermaid\nsequenceDiagram\n    autonumber\n    participant B as Browser (MapLibre)\n    participant G as Gateway / CDN\n    participant T as Tile server\n    participant P as PostGIS\n    B->>G: GET /tiles/trees/12/2103/1346.pbf\n    alt cache hit\n        G-->>B: 200 MVT (Cache-Control, ETag)\n    else cache miss\n        G->>T: forward request (+ auth context)\n        T->>P: SELECT trees_mvt(12, 2103, 1346)\n        P-->>T: bytea (MVT)\n        T-->>G: 200 application/vnd.mapbox-vector-tile\n        G-->>B: 200 MVT (cached for next request)\n    end\n    B->>B: decode protobuf, style, draw\n```\n\n","lineStart":165,"lineEnd":186,"html":"<h2>3.3 Sequence: lifecycle of a vector-tile request</h2>\n<figure class=\"source-diagram\"><a href=\"assets/figures/phase5-source-3-3.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/figures/phase5-source-3-3.svg\" alt=\"3.3 Sequence: lifecycle of a vector-tile request — source diagram\" loading=\"lazy\"></a><figcaption>Source diagram · <a href=\"assets/figures/phase5-source-3-3.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Enlarge ↗</a></figcaption></figure><details class=\"source-code\"><summary>Mermaid definition</summary><pre><code class=\"language-mermaid\">sequenceDiagram\n    autonumber\n    participant B as Browser (MapLibre)\n    participant G as Gateway / CDN\n    participant T as Tile server\n    participant P as PostGIS\n    B-&gt;&gt;G: GET /tiles/trees/12/2103/1346.pbf\n    alt cache hit\n        G--&gt;&gt;B: 200 MVT (Cache-Control, ETag)\n    else cache miss\n        G-&gt;&gt;T: forward request (+ auth context)\n        T-&gt;&gt;P: SELECT trees_mvt(12, 2103, 1346)\n        P--&gt;&gt;T: bytea (MVT)\n        T--&gt;&gt;G: 200 application/vnd.mapbox-vector-tile\n        G--&gt;&gt;B: 200 MVT (cached for next request)\n    end\n    B-&gt;&gt;B: decode protobuf, style, draw</code></pre></details>","sha256":"5d1eb16fb753791abbc4c2136b1a1c81b6f8fdaff1e9405f46bf28ca02409685"},{"key":"3.4","id":"3-4","title":"3.4 Sequence: editing workflow with optimistic concurrency","anchor":"34-sequence-editing-workflow-with-optimistic-concurrency","depth":3,"start":10486,"topics":["editing"],"end":11292,"markdown":"### 3.4 Sequence: editing workflow with optimistic concurrency\n\n```mermaid\nsequenceDiagram\n    autonumber\n    participant U as User\n    participant C as Web client\n    participant A as Edit API\n    participant P as PostGIS (RLS on)\n    U->>C: select feature, drag point, edit attributes\n    C->>A: GET feature (id, version=3)\n    A->>P: SELECT ... WHERE id = ?\n    P-->>A: row (version 3)\n    A-->>C: GeoJSON Feature with version 3\n    U->>C: save\n    C->>A: PATCH feature (version=3, new geometry)\n    A->>P: UPDATE ... WHERE id=? AND version=3\n    alt rows updated = 1\n        P-->>A: version now 4\n        A-->>C: 200 OK (new version)\n        C->>C: refresh tile source\n    else rows updated = 0\n        A-->>C: 409 Conflict (someone else edited)\n        C->>U: show conflict, offer reload\n    end\n```\n\n","lineStart":187,"lineEnd":213,"html":"<h2>3.4 Sequence: editing workflow with optimistic concurrency</h2>\n<figure class=\"source-diagram\"><a href=\"assets/figures/phase5-source-3-4.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/figures/phase5-source-3-4.svg\" alt=\"3.4 Sequence: editing workflow with optimistic concurrency — source diagram\" loading=\"lazy\"></a><figcaption>Source diagram · <a href=\"assets/figures/phase5-source-3-4.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Enlarge ↗</a></figcaption></figure><details class=\"source-code\"><summary>Mermaid definition</summary><pre><code class=\"language-mermaid\">sequenceDiagram\n    autonumber\n    participant U as User\n    participant C as Web client\n    participant A as Edit API\n    participant P as PostGIS (RLS on)\n    U-&gt;&gt;C: select feature, drag point, edit attributes\n    C-&gt;&gt;A: GET feature (id, version=3)\n    A-&gt;&gt;P: SELECT ... WHERE id = ?\n    P--&gt;&gt;A: row (version 3)\n    A--&gt;&gt;C: GeoJSON Feature with version 3\n    U-&gt;&gt;C: save\n    C-&gt;&gt;A: PATCH feature (version=3, new geometry)\n    A-&gt;&gt;P: UPDATE ... WHERE id=? AND version=3\n    alt rows updated = 1\n        P--&gt;&gt;A: version now 4\n        A--&gt;&gt;C: 200 OK (new version)\n        C-&gt;&gt;C: refresh tile source\n    else rows updated = 0\n        A--&gt;&gt;C: 409 Conflict (someone else edited)\n        C-&gt;&gt;U: show conflict, offer reload\n    end</code></pre></details>","sha256":"a5d86a16166d792c3e1dbbcc7ac8ee64544ca3fba0709615a3f5ac870920a828"},{"key":"3.5","id":"3-5","title":"3.5 Data model for the capstone project","anchor":"35-data-model-for-the-capstone-project","depth":3,"start":11292,"topics":["security"],"end":12081,"markdown":"### 3.5 Data model for the capstone project\n\n```mermaid\nerDiagram\n    ORGANIZATION ||--o{ APP_USER : employs\n    ORGANIZATION ||--o{ TREE : owns\n    APP_USER ||--o{ EDIT_LOG : performs\n    TREE ||--o{ EDIT_LOG : \"is changed by\"\n    ORGANIZATION {\n        text code PK\n        text name\n    }\n    APP_USER {\n        bigint id PK\n        text email\n        text org_code FK\n        text role\n    }\n    TREE {\n        bigint id PK\n        text species\n        numeric height_m\n        text condition\n        text owner_org FK\n        boolean is_public\n        int version\n        timestamptz updated_at\n        geometry geom \"Point, EPSG:4326\"\n    }\n    EDIT_LOG {\n        bigint id PK\n        bigint tree_id FK\n        bigint user_id FK\n        text action\n        timestamptz at\n    }\n```\n\n","lineStart":214,"lineEnd":251,"html":"<h2>3.5 Data model for the capstone project</h2>\n<figure class=\"source-diagram\"><a href=\"assets/figures/phase5-source-3-5.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/figures/phase5-source-3-5.svg\" alt=\"3.5 Data model for the capstone project — source diagram\" loading=\"lazy\"></a><figcaption>Source diagram · <a href=\"assets/figures/phase5-source-3-5.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Enlarge ↗</a></figcaption></figure><details class=\"source-code\"><summary>Mermaid definition</summary><pre><code class=\"language-mermaid\">erDiagram\n    ORGANIZATION ||--o{ APP_USER : employs\n    ORGANIZATION ||--o{ TREE : owns\n    APP_USER ||--o{ EDIT_LOG : performs\n    TREE ||--o{ EDIT_LOG : &quot;is changed by&quot;\n    ORGANIZATION {\n        text code PK\n        text name\n    }\n    APP_USER {\n        bigint id PK\n        text email\n        text org_code FK\n        text role\n    }\n    TREE {\n        bigint id PK\n        text species\n        numeric height_m\n        text condition\n        text owner_org FK\n        boolean is_public\n        int version\n        timestamptz updated_at\n        geometry geom &quot;Point, EPSG:4326&quot;\n    }\n    EDIT_LOG {\n        bigint id PK\n        bigint tree_id FK\n        bigint user_id FK\n        text action\n        timestamptz at\n    }</code></pre></details>","sha256":"f11f105486210178f52ec21fd0103a30ad7a3a8f7d9ea4cfb53db7870cfc81b2"},{"key":"3.6","id":"3-6","title":"3.6 Decision flow: how should this layer be served?","anchor":"36-decision-flow-how-should-this-layer-be-served","depth":3,"start":12081,"topics":["services"],"end":12993,"markdown":"### 3.6 Decision flow: how should this layer be served?\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    Q[\"New layer to publish\"] --> A{\"Does the data change often?\"}\n    A -- \"Rarely: daily or less\" --> B{\"More than about 100k features?\"}\n    A -- \"Often: minutes or seconds\" --> C[\"Dynamic MVT from PostGIS with short cache TTL\"]\n    B -- \"Yes\" --> D[\"Pre-generate with Tippecanoe or tilemaker, serve MBTiles or PMTiles\"]\n    B -- \"No\" --> E{\"Need per-user filtering?\"}\n    E -- \"No\" --> F[\"GeoJSON or dynamic MVT plus CDN cache\"]\n    E -- \"Yes\" --> C\n    C --> G{\"Per-user data in tiles?\"}\n    G -- \"Yes\" --> H[\"Cache key must include role or skip shared cache\"]\n    G -- \"No\" --> I[\"Shared cache is safe\"]\n```\n\n**Interpretation:** the two questions that matter most are *how often does the data change* and *does the response depend on who is asking?* The second question is the one most often forgotten — see Section 6.\n\n---\n\n","lineStart":252,"lineEnd":271,"html":"<h2>3.6 Decision flow: how should this layer be served?</h2>\n<figure class=\"source-diagram\"><a href=\"assets/figures/phase5-source-3-6.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/figures/phase5-source-3-6.svg\" alt=\"3.6 Decision flow: how should this layer be served? — source diagram\" loading=\"lazy\"></a><figcaption>Source diagram · <a href=\"assets/figures/phase5-source-3-6.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Enlarge ↗</a></figcaption></figure><details class=\"source-code\"><summary>Mermaid definition</summary><pre><code class=\"language-mermaid\">flowchart TD\n    Q[&quot;New layer to publish&quot;] --&gt; A{&quot;Does the data change often?&quot;}\n    A -- &quot;Rarely: daily or less&quot; --&gt; B{&quot;More than about 100k features?&quot;}\n    A -- &quot;Often: minutes or seconds&quot; --&gt; C[&quot;Dynamic MVT from PostGIS with short cache TTL&quot;]\n    B -- &quot;Yes&quot; --&gt; D[&quot;Pre-generate with Tippecanoe or tilemaker, serve MBTiles or PMTiles&quot;]\n    B -- &quot;No&quot; --&gt; E{&quot;Need per-user filtering?&quot;}\n    E -- &quot;No&quot; --&gt; F[&quot;GeoJSON or dynamic MVT plus CDN cache&quot;]\n    E -- &quot;Yes&quot; --&gt; C\n    C --&gt; G{&quot;Per-user data in tiles?&quot;}\n    G -- &quot;Yes&quot; --&gt; H[&quot;Cache key must include role or skip shared cache&quot;]\n    G -- &quot;No&quot; --&gt; I[&quot;Shared cache is safe&quot;]</code></pre></details><p><strong>Interpretation:</strong> the two questions that matter most are <em>how often does the data change</em> and <em>does the response depend on who is asking?</em> The second question is the one most often forgotten — see Section 6.</p>\n<hr>\n","sha256":"ad2db2078465a08bfb0fdcf0b598464caa9400374f4b1990772c7ddee6368f3c"},{"key":"4","id":"4","title":"4. Technical deep dive","anchor":"4-technical-deep-dive","depth":2,"start":12993,"topics":["stack"],"end":13020,"markdown":"## 4. Technical deep dive\n\n","lineStart":272,"lineEnd":273,"html":"<h2>4. Technical deep dive</h2>\n","sha256":"068963b1f3f2c0bb9758365de0ce894bb698df75f29b865d9227d72ac8178a18"},{"key":"4.1","id":"4-1","title":"4.1 Web Mercator and tile mathematics","anchor":"41-web-mercator-and-tile-mathematics","depth":3,"start":13020,"topics":["tile-math"],"end":15226,"markdown":"### 4.1 Web Mercator and tile mathematics\n\nWeb Mercator (**EPSG:3857**) treats the Earth as a sphere of radius `R = 6 378 137 m`. Its square world extent is\n\n```\ncircumference C = 2·π·R = 40 075 016.685578 m\nX, Y ∈ [−20 037 508.342789 , +20 037 508.342789]   (metres, EPSG:3857)\nlatitude limit  φ_max = atan(sinh(π)) = 85.0511287798066°\n```\n\nA tile is addressed `(z, x, y)` with `n = 2^z` tiles per axis. Converting a WGS 84 coordinate `(λ, φ)` (longitude, latitude in **degrees**, EPSG:4326 / CRS:84 order lon,lat) to the XYZ tile that contains it:\n\n```\nn = 2^z\nx = floor( (λ + 180) / 360 · n )\ny = floor( (1 − asinh(tan φ) / π) / 2 · n )        # asinh(tan φ) = ln(tan φ + sec φ)\n```\n\nInverse (north-west corner of tile `x, y`):\n\n```\nλ = x / n · 360 − 180\nφ = atan( sinh( π · (1 − 2·y / n) ) )\n```\n\nGround resolution for 256-pixel tiles (metres per pixel; multiply by `cos φ` for true ground distance at latitude φ):\n\n```\nres(z) = C / (256 · 2^z)  =  156 543.03392804097 / 2^z       (at the equator)\n```\n\n**Reference table** (computed with the script in the solution to Exercise 1; \"res @52.37°\" = `res(z)·cos(52.37°)`):\n\n| z | Tiles (4^z) | Tile width (m, EPSG:3857) | res equator (m/px) | res @52.37° (m/px) |\n|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| 0 | 1 | 40 075 016.69 | 156 543.03 | 95 578.90 |\n| 5 | 1 024 | 1 252 344.27 | 4 891.97 | 2 986.84 |\n| 10 | 1 048 576 | 39 135.76 | 152.87 | 93.34 |\n| 12 | 16 777 216 | 9 783.94 | 38.22 | 23.33 |\n| 14 | 268 435 456 | 2 445.98 | 9.55 | 5.83 |\n| 18 | 68 719 476 736 | 152.87 | 0.60 | 0.36 |\n\n**Worked micro-example.** The point `(lon 4.9°, lat 52.37°)` at `z = 12` falls in tile **`12/2103/1346`**. That tile spans, in EPSG:4326, `W 4.833984°, S 52.321911°, E 4.921875°, N 52.375599°` and, in EPSG:3857, `minX 538 116.68, minY 6 858 541.67, maxX 547 900.62, maxY 6 868 325.61` (metres).\n\n**Retina / 512-px tiles.** MapLibre GL JS uses 512-px tiles, so its *zoom level* is one lower than the 256-px zoom that has the same ground resolution: `zoom_maplibre = zoom_256 − 1`. If your raster basemap looks half-sized or blurry, check `tileSize`.\n\n**XYZ vs TMS.** `y_tms = 2^z − 1 − y_xyz`. A map that renders but is mirrored north–south almost always has this mismatch.\n\n","lineStart":274,"lineEnd":321,"html":"<h2>4.1 Web Mercator and tile mathematics</h2>\n<p>Web Mercator (<strong>EPSG:3857</strong>) treats the Earth as a sphere of radius <code>R = 6 378 137 m</code>. Its square world extent is</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">circumference C = 2·π·R = 40 075 016.685578 m\nX, Y ∈ [−20 037 508.342789 , +20 037 508.342789]   (metres, EPSG:3857)\nlatitude limit  φ_max = atan(sinh(π)) = 85.0511287798066°</code></pre><p>A tile is addressed <code>(z, x, y)</code> with <code>n = 2^z</code> tiles per axis. Converting a WGS 84 coordinate <code>(λ, φ)</code> (longitude, latitude in <strong>degrees</strong>, EPSG:4326 / CRS:84 order lon,lat) to the XYZ tile that contains it:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">n = 2^z\nx = floor( (λ + 180) / 360 · n )\ny = floor( (1 − asinh(tan φ) / π) / 2 · n )        # asinh(tan φ) = ln(tan φ + sec φ)</code></pre><p>Inverse (north-west corner of tile <code>x, y</code>):</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">λ = x / n · 360 − 180\nφ = atan( sinh( π · (1 − 2·y / n) ) )</code></pre><p>Ground resolution for 256-pixel tiles (metres per pixel; multiply by <code>cos φ</code> for true ground distance at latitude φ):</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">res(z) = C / (256 · 2^z)  =  156 543.03392804097 / 2^z       (at the equator)</code></pre><p><strong>Reference table</strong> (computed with the script in the solution to Exercise 1; &quot;res @52.37°&quot; = <code>res(z)·cos(52.37°)</code>):</p>\n<div class=\"source-table\" role=\"region\" tabindex=\"0\" aria-label=\"Table in 4.1 Web Mercator and tile mathematics\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th align=\"right\">z</th>\n<th align=\"right\">Tiles (4^z)</th>\n<th align=\"right\">Tile width (m, EPSG:3857)</th>\n<th align=\"right\">res equator (m/px)</th>\n<th align=\"right\">res @52.37° (m/px)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"right\">0</td>\n<td align=\"right\">1</td>\n<td align=\"right\">40 075 016.69</td>\n<td align=\"right\">156 543.03</td>\n<td align=\"right\">95 578.90</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"right\">5</td>\n<td align=\"right\">1 024</td>\n<td align=\"right\">1 252 344.27</td>\n<td align=\"right\">4 891.97</td>\n<td align=\"right\">2 986.84</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"right\">10</td>\n<td align=\"right\">1 048 576</td>\n<td align=\"right\">39 135.76</td>\n<td align=\"right\">152.87</td>\n<td align=\"right\">93.34</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"right\">12</td>\n<td align=\"right\">16 777 216</td>\n<td align=\"right\">9 783.94</td>\n<td align=\"right\">38.22</td>\n<td align=\"right\">23.33</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"right\">14</td>\n<td align=\"right\">268 435 456</td>\n<td align=\"right\">2 445.98</td>\n<td align=\"right\">9.55</td>\n<td align=\"right\">5.83</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"right\">18</td>\n<td align=\"right\">68 719 476 736</td>\n<td align=\"right\">152.87</td>\n<td align=\"right\">0.60</td>\n<td align=\"right\">0.36</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div><p><strong>Worked micro-example.</strong> The point <code>(lon 4.9°, lat 52.37°)</code> at <code>z = 12</code> falls in tile <strong><code>12/2103/1346</code></strong>. That tile spans, in EPSG:4326, <code>W 4.833984°, S 52.321911°, E 4.921875°, N 52.375599°</code> and, in EPSG:3857, <code>minX 538 116.68, minY 6 858 541.67, maxX 547 900.62, maxY 6 868 325.61</code> (metres).</p>\n<p><strong>Retina / 512-px tiles.</strong> MapLibre GL JS uses 512-px tiles, so its <em>zoom level</em> is one lower than the 256-px zoom that has the same ground resolution: <code>zoom_maplibre = zoom_256 − 1</code>. If your raster basemap looks half-sized or blurry, check <code>tileSize</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>XYZ vs TMS.</strong> <code>y_tms = 2^z − 1 − y_xyz</code>. A map that renders but is mirrored north–south almost always has this mismatch.</p>\n","sha256":"cf4578316774c0f5c73284f644f68d1276e535914c08b024623d837fe853da86"},{"key":"4.2","id":"4-2","title":"4.2 The Mapbox Vector Tile (MVT) format","anchor":"42-the-mapbox-vector-tile-mvt-format","depth":3,"start":15226,"topics":["mvt"],"end":16708,"markdown":"### 4.2 The Mapbox Vector Tile (MVT) format\n\nAn MVT is a Protocol Buffers message (spec version 2.1) with this logical structure:\n\n```\nTile\n └─ Layer[]            name, version=2, extent (default 4096), keys[], values[]\n     └─ Feature[]      id (optional, unsigned int), type (POINT | LINESTRING | POLYGON),\n                       tags[] (pairs of indexes into keys/values), geometry[] (commands)\n```\n\nKey properties:\n\n- **Tile-local integer coordinates.** With `extent = 4096`, the tile is a 4096 × 4096 grid with the origin at the top-left and **y increasing downwards**. At z = 14 one unit ≈ `2 445.98 m / 4096 ≈ 0.597 m`, so coordinates are quantised to about 0.6 m at that zoom. This is why MVT geometry is not suitable as the authoritative store.\n- **Geometry commands.** A command integer is `(id & 0x7) | (count << 3)` with `MoveTo = 1`, `LineTo = 2`, `ClosePath = 7`. Parameters are **zig-zag** encoded deltas: `zigzag(n) = (n << 1) ^ (n >> 31)`. A single point at (25, 17) is encoded `[9, 50, 34]` (`9 = 1 | 1<<3`, `50 = zigzag(25)`, `34 = zigzag(17)`).\n- **Attributes are flat.** Values may be string, float, double, int, uint, sint or bool — **no nested objects or arrays**.\n- **Buffer.** Geometries are usually clipped to the tile plus a small buffer (e.g. 64 units) so that line joins and polygon outlines do not show seams at tile edges.\n- **Feature ids** must be unsigned integers if you want to use MapLibre `feature-state` (hover/selection) without `promoteId`.\n\n","lineStart":322,"lineEnd":340,"html":"<h2>4.2 The Mapbox Vector Tile (MVT) format</h2>\n<p>An MVT is a Protocol Buffers message (spec version 2.1) with this logical structure:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Tile\n └─ Layer[]            name, version=2, extent (default 4096), keys[], values[]\n     └─ Feature[]      id (optional, unsigned int), type (POINT | LINESTRING | POLYGON),\n                       tags[] (pairs of indexes into keys/values), geometry[] (commands)</code></pre><p>Key properties:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Tile-local integer coordinates.</strong> With <code>extent = 4096</code>, the tile is a 4096 × 4096 grid with the origin at the top-left and <strong>y increasing downwards</strong>. At z = 14 one unit ≈ <code>2 445.98 m / 4096 ≈ 0.597 m</code>, so coordinates are quantised to about 0.6 m at that zoom. This is why MVT geometry is not suitable as the authoritative store.</li>\n<li><strong>Geometry commands.</strong> A command integer is <code>(id &amp; 0x7) | (count &lt;&lt; 3)</code> with <code>MoveTo = 1</code>, <code>LineTo = 2</code>, <code>ClosePath = 7</code>. Parameters are <strong>zig-zag</strong> encoded deltas: <code>zigzag(n) = (n &lt;&lt; 1) ^ (n &gt;&gt; 31)</code>. A single point at (25, 17) is encoded <code>[9, 50, 34]</code> (<code>9 = 1 | 1&lt;&lt;3</code>, <code>50 = zigzag(25)</code>, <code>34 = zigzag(17)</code>).</li>\n<li><strong>Attributes are flat.</strong> Values may be string, float, double, int, uint, sint or bool — <strong>no nested objects or arrays</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Buffer.</strong> Geometries are usually clipped to the tile plus a small buffer (e.g. 64 units) so that line joins and polygon outlines do not show seams at tile edges.</li>\n<li><strong>Feature ids</strong> must be unsigned integers if you want to use MapLibre <code>feature-state</code> (hover/selection) without <code>promoteId</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n","sha256":"ce9785a826b817218ee1502bc8a386c92f4d388a45d31fbca31f57e1ecaf74f5"},{"key":"4.3","id":"4-3","title":"4.3 Generating MVT in PostGIS","anchor":"43-generating-mvt-in-postgis","depth":3,"start":16708,"topics":["mvt"],"end":20028,"markdown":"### 4.3 Generating MVT in PostGIS\n\nThree functions do the work (PostGIS ≥ 3.0 for `ST_TileEnvelope`):\n\n| Function | Purpose |\n|---|---|\n| `ST_TileEnvelope(z, x, y)` | Returns the tile's bounding rectangle in EPSG:3857 (SRID 3857 polygon) |\n| `ST_AsMVTGeom(geom, bounds, extent, buffer, clip_geom)` | Clips and snaps a geometry (already in the same SRS as `bounds`) to tile-local integer coordinates; returns `NULL` if the result is empty |\n| `ST_AsMVT(row, layer_name, extent, geom_column, id_column)` | Aggregates rows into one MVT `bytea` |\n\n**Points layer, stored in EPSG:4326**\n\n```sql\nCREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION postgisftw.trees_mvt(z integer, x integer, y integer)\nRETURNS bytea\nLANGUAGE sql STABLE PARALLEL SAFE\nAS $$\n  WITH env AS (\n    SELECT ST_TileEnvelope(z, x, y) AS g3857\n  ),\n  mvtgeom AS (\n    SELECT t.id,\n           t.species,\n           t.height_m,\n           t.condition,\n           ST_AsMVTGeom(ST_Transform(t.geom, 3857), env.g3857, 4096, 64, true) AS geom\n    FROM city.trees AS t, env\n    WHERE t.geom && ST_Transform(env.g3857, 4326)      -- index-assisted bbox filter\n  )\n  SELECT COALESCE(ST_AsMVT(mvtgeom.*, 'trees', 4096, 'geom', 'id'), ''::bytea)\n  FROM mvtgeom;\n$$;\n```\n\n- The **layer name** (`'trees'`) becomes `source-layer` in the client style.\n- The bbox filter is applied in EPSG:4326 so the GiST index on `geom` is used; transforming the *envelope* once is far cheaper than transforming every row.\n- `COALESCE(..., ''::bytea)` returns an empty (but valid, zero-byte) tile when nothing intersects; servers then answer `200`/`204` depending on implementation.\n- A rectangle in EPSG:3857 maps exactly to a rectangle in EPSG:4326 (meridians and parallels remain straight lines in both), so the transformed envelope is a safe filter.\n\n**Polygons with simplification and LOD** (assumes a `city.parcels` table with `geom geometry(MultiPolygon,4326)` and `area_m2 double precision`):\n\n```sql\nCREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION postgisftw.parcels_mvt(z integer, x integer, y integer)\nRETURNS bytea\nLANGUAGE sql STABLE PARALLEL SAFE\nAS $$\n  WITH env AS (\n    SELECT ST_TileEnvelope(z, x, y)              AS g3857,\n           156543.03392804097 / (2 ^ z)          AS res          -- metres per pixel, 256-px tiles\n  ),\n  mvtgeom AS (\n    SELECT p.id, p.land_use,\n           ST_AsMVTGeom(\n             ST_SimplifyPreserveTopology(ST_Transform(p.geom, 3857), env.res),   -- tolerance ≈ 1 px\n             env.g3857, 4096, 64, true) AS geom\n    FROM city.parcels AS p, env\n    WHERE p.geom && ST_Transform(env.g3857, 4326)\n      AND (z >= 14 OR p.area_m2 >= env.res * env.res * 16)       -- drop objects below ~16 px²\n  )\n  SELECT COALESCE(ST_AsMVT(mvtgeom.*, 'parcels', 4096, 'geom', 'id'), ''::bytea) FROM mvtgeom;\n$$;\n```\n\nDesign rationale: below one pixel of separation, vertices are invisible, so simplifying with a tolerance of about one pixel (`res`) removes bytes without a visible change. Note that `area_m2` here is a *projected* area for illustration; for global data use `ST_Area(geom::geography)`.\n\n> **Performance tip.** If tiles are the main workload, add a stored `geom_3857` column with its own GiST index and filter `geom_3857 && env.g3857`; this avoids `ST_Transform` entirely on the filter path. The trade-off is a second geometry copy to keep in sync (a generated column or trigger).\n\n","lineStart":341,"lineEnd":407,"html":"<h2>4.3 Generating MVT in PostGIS</h2>\n<p>Three functions do the work (PostGIS ≥ 3.0 for <code>ST_TileEnvelope</code>):</p>\n<div class=\"source-table\" role=\"region\" tabindex=\"0\" aria-label=\"Table in 4.3 Generating MVT in PostGIS\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Function</th>\n<th>Purpose</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>ST_TileEnvelope(z, x, y)</code></td>\n<td>Returns the tile&#39;s bounding rectangle in EPSG:3857 (SRID 3857 polygon)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>ST_AsMVTGeom(geom, bounds, extent, buffer, clip_geom)</code></td>\n<td>Clips and snaps a geometry (already in the same SRS as <code>bounds</code>) to tile-local integer coordinates; returns <code>NULL</code> if the result is empty</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>ST_AsMVT(row, layer_name, extent, geom_column, id_column)</code></td>\n<td>Aggregates rows into one MVT <code>bytea</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div><p><strong>Points layer, stored in EPSG:4326</strong></p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-sql\">CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION postgisftw.trees_mvt(z integer, x integer, y integer)\nRETURNS bytea\nLANGUAGE sql STABLE PARALLEL SAFE\nAS $$\n  WITH env AS (\n    SELECT ST_TileEnvelope(z, x, y) AS g3857\n  ),\n  mvtgeom AS (\n    SELECT t.id,\n           t.species,\n           t.height_m,\n           t.condition,\n           ST_AsMVTGeom(ST_Transform(t.geom, 3857), env.g3857, 4096, 64, true) AS geom\n    FROM city.trees AS t, env\n    WHERE t.geom &amp;&amp; ST_Transform(env.g3857, 4326)      -- index-assisted bbox filter\n  )\n  SELECT COALESCE(ST_AsMVT(mvtgeom.*, &#39;trees&#39;, 4096, &#39;geom&#39;, &#39;id&#39;), &#39;&#39;::bytea)\n  FROM mvtgeom;\n$$;</code></pre><ul>\n<li>The <strong>layer name</strong> (<code>&#39;trees&#39;</code>) becomes <code>source-layer</code> in the client style.</li>\n<li>The bbox filter is applied in EPSG:4326 so the GiST index on <code>geom</code> is used; transforming the <em>envelope</em> once is far cheaper than transforming every row.</li>\n<li><code>COALESCE(..., &#39;&#39;::bytea)</code> returns an empty (but valid, zero-byte) tile when nothing intersects; servers then answer <code>200</code>/<code>204</code> depending on implementation.</li>\n<li>A rectangle in EPSG:3857 maps exactly to a rectangle in EPSG:4326 (meridians and parallels remain straight lines in both), so the transformed envelope is a safe filter.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Polygons with simplification and LOD</strong> (assumes a <code>city.parcels</code> table with <code>geom geometry(MultiPolygon,4326)</code> and <code>area_m2 double precision</code>):</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-sql\">CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION postgisftw.parcels_mvt(z integer, x integer, y integer)\nRETURNS bytea\nLANGUAGE sql STABLE PARALLEL SAFE\nAS $$\n  WITH env AS (\n    SELECT ST_TileEnvelope(z, x, y)              AS g3857,\n           156543.03392804097 / (2 ^ z)          AS res          -- metres per pixel, 256-px tiles\n  ),\n  mvtgeom AS (\n    SELECT p.id, p.land_use,\n           ST_AsMVTGeom(\n             ST_SimplifyPreserveTopology(ST_Transform(p.geom, 3857), env.res),   -- tolerance ≈ 1 px\n             env.g3857, 4096, 64, true) AS geom\n    FROM city.parcels AS p, env\n    WHERE p.geom &amp;&amp; ST_Transform(env.g3857, 4326)\n      AND (z &gt;= 14 OR p.area_m2 &gt;= env.res * env.res * 16)       -- drop objects below ~16 px²\n  )\n  SELECT COALESCE(ST_AsMVT(mvtgeom.*, &#39;parcels&#39;, 4096, &#39;geom&#39;, &#39;id&#39;), &#39;&#39;::bytea) FROM mvtgeom;\n$$;</code></pre><p>Design rationale: below one pixel of separation, vertices are invisible, so simplifying with a tolerance of about one pixel (<code>res</code>) removes bytes without a visible change. Note that <code>area_m2</code> here is a <em>projected</em> area for illustration; for global data use <code>ST_Area(geom::geography)</code>.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Performance tip.</strong> If tiles are the main workload, add a stored <code>geom_3857</code> column with its own GiST index and filter <code>geom_3857 &amp;&amp; env.g3857</code>; this avoids <code>ST_Transform</code> entirely on the filter path. The trade-off is a second geometry copy to keep in sync (a generated column or trigger).</p>\n</blockquote>\n","sha256":"414e3b0568576b9198516e5220d2e2ecb4149e49ad1afb77e2a12bf4c2197dc1"},{"key":"4.4","id":"4-4","title":"4.4 Servers: what each one is for","anchor":"44-servers-what-each-one-is-for","depth":3,"start":20028,"topics":["services"],"end":22167,"markdown":"### 4.4 Servers: what each one is for\n\n| Server | Language | Serves | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **GeoServer** | Java | WMS, WMTS, WFS(-T), WCS, OGC API (Features, Tiles, others via modules), vector tiles (extension), embedded **GeoWebCache** | Full-featured, admin GUI + REST config; heavy but very capable; data security rules per workspace/layer |\n| **MapServer** | C | WMS, WFS, WCS, OGC API – Features, WMTS-like tiling | Config via *mapfile*; fast, minimal footprint |\n| **QGIS Server** | C++/Python | WMS, WFS, WMTS, WCS, OGC API – Features | Renders a **QGIS project** (`.qgs/.qgz`): your desktop styling becomes the web style |\n| **pg_tileserv** | Go | MVT for PostGIS **tables and SQL functions** | Function layers have signature `(z int, x int, y int [, params...]) → bytea`; URL `/{schema}.{function}/{z}/{x}/{y}.pbf` (default port 7800) |\n| **pg_featureserv** | Go | OGC API – Features for PostGIS tables/views/functions | **Read-oriented**: upstream pg_featureserv has no merged write support (write support exists only as an open pull request) and its maintainers recommend a read-only DB user when it is not used for updates |\n| **Martin** | Rust | MVT from PostGIS tables/functions, plus MBTiles and PMTiles files, sprites, fonts | Auto-discovers sources; `/catalog` lists them; function sources take `(z, x, y, query_params json)` |\n| **GeoWebCache** | Java | Tile cache/proxy in front of WMS (WMTS, TMS, XYZ endpoints) | Standalone or embedded in GeoServer; metatiling, seeding, truncation, disk quotas |\n\n> **pg_tileserv naming rule.** By default pg_tileserv publishes *function* layers only from the `postgisftw` schema (this is configurable), and *table* layers as `{schema}.{table}`. That is why the tile functions in this note live in `postgisftw`. Table layers return every attribute and apply no LOD logic, so prefer function layers for anything beyond a prototype.\n\nTypical split in this stack: **Martin or pg_tileserv** (tiles) + **pg_featureserv** (reads) + **a small edit service** (writes), with **GeoServer or QGIS Server** added when you need WMS/WFS interoperability with desktop GIS or legacy clients.\n\n","lineStart":408,"lineEnd":423,"html":"<h2>4.4 Servers: what each one is for</h2>\n<div class=\"source-table\" role=\"region\" tabindex=\"0\" aria-label=\"Table in 4.4 Servers: what each one is for\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Server</th>\n<th>Language</th>\n<th>Serves</th>\n<th>Notes</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>GeoServer</strong></td>\n<td>Java</td>\n<td>WMS, WMTS, WFS(-T), WCS, OGC API (Features, Tiles, others via modules), vector tiles (extension), embedded <strong>GeoWebCache</strong></td>\n<td>Full-featured, admin GUI + REST config; heavy but very capable; data security rules per workspace/layer</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>MapServer</strong></td>\n<td>C</td>\n<td>WMS, WFS, WCS, OGC API – Features, WMTS-like tiling</td>\n<td>Config via <em>mapfile</em>; fast, minimal footprint</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>QGIS Server</strong></td>\n<td>C++/Python</td>\n<td>WMS, WFS, WMTS, WCS, OGC API – Features</td>\n<td>Renders a <strong>QGIS project</strong> (<code>.qgs/.qgz</code>): your desktop styling becomes the web style</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>pg_tileserv</strong></td>\n<td>Go</td>\n<td>MVT for PostGIS <strong>tables and SQL functions</strong></td>\n<td>Function layers have signature <code>(z int, x int, y int [, params...]) → bytea</code>; URL <code>/{schema}.{function}/{z}/{x}/{y}.pbf</code> (default port 7800)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>pg_featureserv</strong></td>\n<td>Go</td>\n<td>OGC API – Features for PostGIS tables/views/functions</td>\n<td><strong>Read-oriented</strong>: upstream pg_featureserv has no merged write support (write support exists only as an open pull request) and its maintainers recommend a read-only DB user when it is not used for updates</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Martin</strong></td>\n<td>Rust</td>\n<td>MVT from PostGIS tables/functions, plus MBTiles and PMTiles files, sprites, fonts</td>\n<td>Auto-discovers sources; <code>/catalog</code> lists them; function sources take <code>(z, x, y, query_params json)</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>GeoWebCache</strong></td>\n<td>Java</td>\n<td>Tile cache/proxy in front of WMS (WMTS, TMS, XYZ endpoints)</td>\n<td>Standalone or embedded in GeoServer; metatiling, seeding, truncation, disk quotas</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div><blockquote>\n<p><strong>pg_tileserv naming rule.</strong> By default pg_tileserv publishes <em>function</em> layers only from the <code>postgisftw</code> schema (this is configurable), and <em>table</em> layers as <code>{schema}.{table}</code>. That is why the tile functions in this note live in <code>postgisftw</code>. Table layers return every attribute and apply no LOD logic, so prefer function layers for anything beyond a prototype.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Typical split in this stack: <strong>Martin or pg_tileserv</strong> (tiles) + <strong>pg_featureserv</strong> (reads) + <strong>a small edit service</strong> (writes), with <strong>GeoServer or QGIS Server</strong> added when you need WMS/WFS interoperability with desktop GIS or legacy clients.</p>\n","sha256":"49e757035dd108b08753d072bac1b40c70c60611730181469c584061c5924471"},{"key":"4.5","id":"4-5","title":"4.5 Pre-generated tiles: Tippecanoe, tilemaker, MBTiles, PMTiles","anchor":"45-pre-generated-tiles-tippecanoe-tilemaker-mbtiles-pmtiles","depth":3,"start":22167,"topics":["performance"],"end":24239,"markdown":"### 4.5 Pre-generated tiles: Tippecanoe, tilemaker, MBTiles, PMTiles\n\nDynamic tiles are flexible; pre-generated tiles are fast and cheap to host.\n\n- **Tippecanoe** builds tilesets from (line-delimited) GeoJSON, FlatGeobuf or CSV, choosing what to drop or merge so that tiles stay within size limits (default budget: 500 000 bytes per tile).\n- **tilemaker** converts **OpenStreetMap PBF** extracts into vector tiles using a JSON layer config and a Lua processing profile.\n- **MBTiles** is an SQLite container for tiles; **PMTiles** is a single-file archive designed for static hosting with HTTP **range requests** (no tile server required). Recent Tippecanoe releases can write PMTiles directly; otherwise convert with the `pmtiles` CLI.\n\n```bash\n# 1. Export from PostGIS to newline-delimited GeoJSON (EPSG:4326 required by Tippecanoe)\nogr2ogr -f GeoJSONSeq trees.geojsonl \\\n  PG:\"host=localhost dbname=gis user=postgres password=postgres\" city.trees\n\n# 2. Build tiles: guess a sensible max zoom, thin out dense areas instead of exceeding tile size\ntippecanoe -o trees.mbtiles -l trees -zg --drop-densest-as-needed --force trees.geojsonl\n\n# 3. Serve (Martin serves the file name as the source id)\nmartin trees.mbtiles          # tiles at http://localhost:3000/trees/{z}/{x}/{y}\n```\n\nUseful Tippecanoe options: `-Z/-z` (min/max zoom), `-zg` (guess max zoom), `--drop-densest-as-needed`, `--coalesce-densest-as-needed`, `--extend-zooms-if-still-dropping`, `-l` (layer name), `--force` (overwrite output). Check `tippecanoe --help` for your version.\n\n**GeoWebCache (GWC)** caches rendered tiles of WMS layers. Concepts you must know: *gridset* (tile matrix set; defaults include EPSG:900913/3857 and EPSG:4326), *metatiling* (render e.g. a 4×4 block at once so labels are not clipped at tile borders, then slice), *seeding* (pre-populate the cache), *truncation* (invalidate after data change), and *disk quota*. The WMTS endpoint of the embedded GWC is typically `/geoserver/gwc/service/wmts`. Beware the **TMS y-axis flip** (Section 4.1) when consuming GWC's TMS endpoint.\n\n","lineStart":424,"lineEnd":447,"html":"<h2>4.5 Pre-generated tiles: Tippecanoe, tilemaker, MBTiles, PMTiles</h2>\n<p>Dynamic tiles are flexible; pre-generated tiles are fast and cheap to host.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Tippecanoe</strong> builds tilesets from (line-delimited) GeoJSON, FlatGeobuf or CSV, choosing what to drop or merge so that tiles stay within size limits (default budget: 500 000 bytes per tile).</li>\n<li><strong>tilemaker</strong> converts <strong>OpenStreetMap PBF</strong> extracts into vector tiles using a JSON layer config and a Lua processing profile.</li>\n<li><strong>MBTiles</strong> is an SQLite container for tiles; <strong>PMTiles</strong> is a single-file archive designed for static hosting with HTTP <strong>range requests</strong> (no tile server required). Recent Tippecanoe releases can write PMTiles directly; otherwise convert with the <code>pmtiles</code> CLI.</li>\n</ul>\n<pre><code class=\"language-bash\"># 1. Export from PostGIS to newline-delimited GeoJSON (EPSG:4326 required by Tippecanoe)\nogr2ogr -f GeoJSONSeq trees.geojsonl \\\n  PG:&quot;host=localhost dbname=gis user=postgres password=postgres&quot; city.trees\n\n# 2. Build tiles: guess a sensible max zoom, thin out dense areas instead of exceeding tile size\ntippecanoe -o trees.mbtiles -l trees -zg --drop-densest-as-needed --force trees.geojsonl\n\n# 3. Serve (Martin serves the file name as the source id)\nmartin trees.mbtiles          # tiles at http://localhost:3000/trees/{z}/{x}/{y}</code></pre><p>Useful Tippecanoe options: <code>-Z/-z</code> (min/max zoom), <code>-zg</code> (guess max zoom), <code>--drop-densest-as-needed</code>, <code>--coalesce-densest-as-needed</code>, <code>--extend-zooms-if-still-dropping</code>, <code>-l</code> (layer name), <code>--force</code> (overwrite output). Check <code>tippecanoe --help</code> for your version.</p>\n<p><strong>GeoWebCache (GWC)</strong> caches rendered tiles of WMS layers. Concepts you must know: <em>gridset</em> (tile matrix set; defaults include EPSG:900913/3857 and EPSG:4326), <em>metatiling</em> (render e.g. a 4×4 block at once so labels are not clipped at tile borders, then slice), <em>seeding</em> (pre-populate the cache), <em>truncation</em> (invalidate after data change), and <em>disk quota</em>. The WMTS endpoint of the embedded GWC is typically <code>/geoserver/gwc/service/wmts</code>. Beware the <strong>TMS y-axis flip</strong> (Section 4.1) when consuming GWC&#39;s TMS endpoint.</p>\n","sha256":"948ab29c80820a2f3b57f2ea80574cba1449ede4994488d88c708f2aa4676f08"},{"key":"4.6","id":"4-6","title":"4.6 OGC API – Features in practice","anchor":"46-ogc-api--features-in-practice","depth":3,"start":24239,"topics":["services"],"end":27481,"markdown":"### 4.6 OGC API – Features in practice\n\nOGC API – Features is a resource-oriented REST API. Mandatory-style resources (Part 1, Core):\n\n| Path | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| `/` | Landing page with links |\n| `/conformance` | Conformance classes the server implements |\n| `/collections` | List of feature collections |\n| `/collections/{collectionId}` | Collection metadata (extent, CRSs, links) |\n| `/collections/{collectionId}/items` | The features (GeoJSON `FeatureCollection`) |\n| `/collections/{collectionId}/items/{featureId}` | A single feature |\n\nCore query parameters: `limit` (page size), `bbox` (`minLon,minLat,maxLon,maxLat` in **CRS84** = lon,lat order, unless `bbox-crs` is supplied via Part 2), `datetime`. Servers page results with `links` containing `rel=\"next\"`. Part 3 adds a `filter` parameter with **CQL2** (`filter-lang=cql2-text`), e.g. `filter=condition='poor' AND height_m > 10`, where supported.\n\nIllustrative response (abbreviated; exact members vary by server):\n\n```http\nGET /collections/city.trees/items?limit=2&bbox=4.85,52.34,4.95,52.40 HTTP/1.1\nAccept: application/geo+json\n```\n\n```json\n{\n  \"type\": \"FeatureCollection\",\n  \"features\": [\n    { \"type\": \"Feature\", \"id\": \"1\",\n      \"geometry\": { \"type\": \"Point\", \"coordinates\": [4.8952, 52.3702] },\n      \"properties\": { \"species\": \"Oak\", \"height_m\": 14.2, \"condition\": \"good\" } },\n    { \"type\": \"Feature\", \"id\": \"2\",\n      \"geometry\": { \"type\": \"Point\", \"coordinates\": [4.9041, 52.3676] },\n      \"properties\": { \"species\": \"Linden\", \"height_m\": 11.0, \"condition\": \"fair\" } }\n  ],\n  \"numberReturned\": 2,\n  \"links\": [ { \"rel\": \"self\", \"type\": \"application/geo+json\", \"href\": \"…/items?limit=2&bbox=…\" },\n             { \"rel\": \"next\", \"type\": \"application/geo+json\", \"href\": \"…/items?limit=2&offset=2&bbox=…\" } ]\n}\n```\n\n**Write operations.** OGC API – Features – **Part 4** defines `POST /collections/{id}/items` (create), `PUT …/items/{fid}` (replace), `PATCH …/items/{fid}` (update) and `DELETE …/items/{fid}`, and includes optimistic-locking semantics. At the time of writing it is a draft/candidate specification, and servers differ in what they implement (GeoServer's documentation describes this as a direction rather than an implemented feature). In this note we therefore build the write path as a **small custom REST service whose shape follows Part 4**, which keeps the client portable if you later swap in a standard implementation. If you need standards-based editing *today*, **WFS-T** (supported by GeoServer and others) is the mature option.\n\n**WFS-T sketch** (XML body POSTed to the WFS endpoint; schematic, namespaces abbreviated):\n\n```xml\n<wfs:Transaction service=\"WFS\" version=\"2.0.0\"\n    xmlns:wfs=\"http://www.opengis.net/wfs/2.0\" xmlns:gml=\"http://www.opengis.net/gml/3.2\"\n    xmlns:city=\"http://example.com/city\">\n  <wfs:Insert>\n    <city:trees>\n      <city:species>Oak</city:species>\n      <city:geom><gml:Point srsName=\"urn:ogc:def:crs:EPSG::4326\">\n        <!-- EPSG:4326 in URN form is lat lon order! -->\n        <gml:pos>52.3702 4.8952</gml:pos></gml:Point></city:geom>\n    </city:trees>\n  </wfs:Insert>\n</wfs:Transaction>\n```\n\nNote the axis-order trap: with the URN form `urn:ogc:def:crs:EPSG::4326` the coordinate order is **latitude, longitude**.\n\n","lineStart":448,"lineEnd":507,"html":"<h2>4.6 OGC API – Features in practice</h2>\n<p>OGC API – Features is a resource-oriented REST API. Mandatory-style resources (Part 1, Core):</p>\n<div class=\"source-table\" role=\"region\" tabindex=\"0\" aria-label=\"Table in 4.6 OGC API – Features in practice\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Path</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>/</code></td>\n<td>Landing page with links</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>/conformance</code></td>\n<td>Conformance classes the server implements</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>/collections</code></td>\n<td>List of feature collections</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>/collections/{collectionId}</code></td>\n<td>Collection metadata (extent, CRSs, links)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>/collections/{collectionId}/items</code></td>\n<td>The features (GeoJSON <code>FeatureCollection</code>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>/collections/{collectionId}/items/{featureId}</code></td>\n<td>A single feature</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div><p>Core query parameters: <code>limit</code> (page size), <code>bbox</code> (<code>minLon,minLat,maxLon,maxLat</code> in <strong>CRS84</strong> = lon,lat order, unless <code>bbox-crs</code> is supplied via Part 2), <code>datetime</code>. Servers page results with <code>links</code> containing <code>rel=&quot;next&quot;</code>. Part 3 adds a <code>filter</code> parameter with <strong>CQL2</strong> (<code>filter-lang=cql2-text</code>), e.g. <code>filter=condition=&#39;poor&#39; AND height_m &gt; 10</code>, where supported.</p>\n<p>Illustrative response (abbreviated; exact members vary by server):</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-http\">GET /collections/city.trees/items?limit=2&amp;bbox=4.85,52.34,4.95,52.40 HTTP/1.1\nAccept: application/geo+json</code></pre><pre><code class=\"language-json\">{\n  &quot;type&quot;: &quot;FeatureCollection&quot;,\n  &quot;features&quot;: [\n    { &quot;type&quot;: &quot;Feature&quot;, &quot;id&quot;: &quot;1&quot;,\n      &quot;geometry&quot;: { &quot;type&quot;: &quot;Point&quot;, &quot;coordinates&quot;: [4.8952, 52.3702] },\n      &quot;properties&quot;: { &quot;species&quot;: &quot;Oak&quot;, &quot;height_m&quot;: 14.2, &quot;condition&quot;: &quot;good&quot; } },\n    { &quot;type&quot;: &quot;Feature&quot;, &quot;id&quot;: &quot;2&quot;,\n      &quot;geometry&quot;: { &quot;type&quot;: &quot;Point&quot;, &quot;coordinates&quot;: [4.9041, 52.3676] },\n      &quot;properties&quot;: { &quot;species&quot;: &quot;Linden&quot;, &quot;height_m&quot;: 11.0, &quot;condition&quot;: &quot;fair&quot; } }\n  ],\n  &quot;numberReturned&quot;: 2,\n  &quot;links&quot;: [ { &quot;rel&quot;: &quot;self&quot;, &quot;type&quot;: &quot;application/geo+json&quot;, &quot;href&quot;: &quot;…/items?limit=2&amp;bbox=…&quot; },\n             { &quot;rel&quot;: &quot;next&quot;, &quot;type&quot;: &quot;application/geo+json&quot;, &quot;href&quot;: &quot;…/items?limit=2&amp;offset=2&amp;bbox=…&quot; } ]\n}</code></pre><p><strong>Write operations.</strong> OGC API – Features – <strong>Part 4</strong> defines <code>POST /collections/{id}/items</code> (create), <code>PUT …/items/{fid}</code> (replace), <code>PATCH …/items/{fid}</code> (update) and <code>DELETE …/items/{fid}</code>, and includes optimistic-locking semantics. At the time of writing it is a draft/candidate specification, and servers differ in what they implement (GeoServer&#39;s documentation describes this as a direction rather than an implemented feature). In this note we therefore build the write path as a <strong>small custom REST service whose shape follows Part 4</strong>, which keeps the client portable if you later swap in a standard implementation. If you need standards-based editing <em>today</em>, <strong>WFS-T</strong> (supported by GeoServer and others) is the mature option.</p>\n<p><strong>WFS-T sketch</strong> (XML body POSTed to the WFS endpoint; schematic, namespaces abbreviated):</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-xml\">&lt;wfs:Transaction service=&quot;WFS&quot; version=&quot;2.0.0&quot;\n    xmlns:wfs=&quot;http://www.opengis.net/wfs/2.0&quot; xmlns:gml=&quot;http://www.opengis.net/gml/3.2&quot;\n    xmlns:city=&quot;http://example.com/city&quot;&gt;\n  &lt;wfs:Insert&gt;\n    &lt;city:trees&gt;\n      &lt;city:species&gt;Oak&lt;/city:species&gt;\n      &lt;city:geom&gt;&lt;gml:Point srsName=&quot;urn:ogc:def:crs:EPSG::4326&quot;&gt;\n        &lt;!-- EPSG:4326 in URN form is lat lon order! --&gt;\n        &lt;gml:pos&gt;52.3702 4.8952&lt;/gml:pos&gt;&lt;/gml:Point&gt;&lt;/city:geom&gt;\n    &lt;/city:trees&gt;\n  &lt;/wfs:Insert&gt;\n&lt;/wfs:Transaction&gt;</code></pre><p>Note the axis-order trap: with the URN form <code>urn:ogc:def:crs:EPSG::4326</code> the coordinate order is <strong>latitude, longitude</strong>.</p>\n","sha256":"701865453604d6687c8e0ca9d479ab0c8434ef5b413c9ba6b87d0c79e7d9412c"},{"key":"4.7","id":"4-7","title":"4.7 Frontend techniques","anchor":"47-frontend-techniques","depth":3,"start":27481,"topics":["styling"],"end":27510,"markdown":"### 4.7 Frontend techniques\n\n","lineStart":508,"lineEnd":509,"html":"<h2>4.7 Frontend techniques</h2>\n","sha256":"7d36904afaf46bcff9251267f83d9ca9580acd7f91cd152fce52330e21858f6d"},{"key":"4.7.1","id":"4-7-1","title":"4.7.1 MapLibre GL JS: vector-tile source and data-driven styling","anchor":"471-maplibre-gl-js-vector-tile-source-and-data-driven-styling","depth":4,"start":27510,"topics":["styling"],"end":29034,"markdown":"#### 4.7.1 MapLibre GL JS: vector-tile source and data-driven styling\n\n```html\n<link href=\"https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@5/dist/maplibre-gl.css\" rel=\"stylesheet\" />\n<script src=\"https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@5/dist/maplibre-gl.js\"></script>\n<div id=\"map\" style=\"height:100vh\"></div>\n<script>\n  const map = new maplibregl.Map({\n    container: 'map',\n    style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json',   // demo basemap; use your own in production\n    center: [4.9, 52.37],                                   // [lon, lat]\n    zoom: 12\n  });\n\n  map.on('load', () => {\n    map.addSource('trees', {\n      type: 'vector',\n      tiles: ['http://localhost:7800/postgisftw.trees_mvt/{z}/{x}/{y}.pbf'],\n      minzoom: 8, maxzoom: 16            // beyond maxzoom the client over-zooms the z16 tiles\n    });\n    map.addLayer({\n      id: 'trees-circles', type: 'circle', source: 'trees', 'source-layer': 'trees',\n      paint: {\n        'circle-radius': ['interpolate', ['linear'], ['zoom'], 10, 2, 16, 8],\n        'circle-color': ['match', ['get', 'condition'],\n                         'good', '#2e7d32', 'fair', '#f9a825', 'poor', '#c62828', '#757575'],\n        'circle-stroke-width': 1, 'circle-stroke-color': '#ffffff'\n      }\n    });\n  });\n</script>\n```\n\nExpressions (`['get', …]`, `['match', …]`, `['interpolate', …]`, `['step', …]`, `['case', …]`) are evaluated on the GPU/client per feature, so changing style never requires regenerating tiles. Pin the library version in production rather than using a floating major tag.\n\n","lineStart":510,"lineEnd":544,"html":"<h2>4.7.1 MapLibre GL JS: vector-tile source and data-driven styling</h2>\n<pre><code class=\"language-html\">&lt;link href=&quot;https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@5/dist/maplibre-gl.css&quot; rel=&quot;stylesheet&quot; /&gt;\n&lt;script src=&quot;https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@5/dist/maplibre-gl.js&quot;&gt;&lt;/script&gt;\n&lt;div id=&quot;map&quot; style=&quot;height:100vh&quot;&gt;&lt;/div&gt;\n&lt;script&gt;\n  const map = new maplibregl.Map({\n    container: &#39;map&#39;,\n    style: &#39;https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json&#39;,   // demo basemap; use your own in production\n    center: [4.9, 52.37],                                   // [lon, lat]\n    zoom: 12\n  });\n\n  map.on(&#39;load&#39;, () =&gt; {\n    map.addSource(&#39;trees&#39;, {\n      type: &#39;vector&#39;,\n      tiles: [&#39;http://localhost:7800/postgisftw.trees_mvt/{z}/{x}/{y}.pbf&#39;],\n      minzoom: 8, maxzoom: 16            // beyond maxzoom the client over-zooms the z16 tiles\n    });\n    map.addLayer({\n      id: &#39;trees-circles&#39;, type: &#39;circle&#39;, source: &#39;trees&#39;, &#39;source-layer&#39;: &#39;trees&#39;,\n      paint: {\n        &#39;circle-radius&#39;: [&#39;interpolate&#39;, [&#39;linear&#39;], [&#39;zoom&#39;], 10, 2, 16, 8],\n        &#39;circle-color&#39;: [&#39;match&#39;, [&#39;get&#39;, &#39;condition&#39;],\n                         &#39;good&#39;, &#39;#2e7d32&#39;, &#39;fair&#39;, &#39;#f9a825&#39;, &#39;poor&#39;, &#39;#c62828&#39;, &#39;#757575&#39;],\n        &#39;circle-stroke-width&#39;: 1, &#39;circle-stroke-color&#39;: &#39;#ffffff&#39;\n      }\n    });\n  });\n&lt;/script&gt;</code></pre><p>Expressions (<code>[&#39;get&#39;, …]</code>, <code>[&#39;match&#39;, …]</code>, <code>[&#39;interpolate&#39;, …]</code>, <code>[&#39;step&#39;, …]</code>, <code>[&#39;case&#39;, …]</code>) are evaluated on the GPU/client per feature, so changing style never requires regenerating tiles. Pin the library version in production rather than using a floating major tag.</p>\n","sha256":"a06b801a7b5dec05db691bfb20d5d0e0fb099c08b03204d436d87a7c16302170"},{"key":"4.7.2","id":"4-7-2","title":"4.7.2 Clustering (client-side, GeoJSON source)","anchor":"472-clustering-client-side-geojson-source","depth":4,"start":29034,"topics":["styling"],"end":30682,"markdown":"#### 4.7.2 Clustering (client-side, GeoJSON source)\n\n```js\nmap.addSource('trees-geojson', {\n  type: 'geojson',\n  data: 'http://localhost:9000/collections/city.trees/items?limit=1000',  // server caps `limit`\n  cluster: true, clusterRadius: 50, clusterMaxZoom: 14\n});\nmap.addLayer({ id: 'clusters', type: 'circle', source: 'trees-geojson',\n  filter: ['has', 'point_count'],\n  paint: { 'circle-color': ['step', ['get', 'point_count'], '#51bbd6', 100, '#f1f075', 750, '#f28cb1'],\n           'circle-radius': ['step', ['get', 'point_count'], 15, 100, 20, 750, 25] } });\nmap.addLayer({ id: 'cluster-count', type: 'symbol', source: 'trees-geojson',\n  filter: ['has', 'point_count'],\n  layout: { 'text-field': '{point_count_abbreviated}',\n            'text-font': ['Open Sans Semibold'],   // must exist in the style's `glyphs` source\n            'text-size': 12 } });\nmap.addLayer({ id: 'unclustered', type: 'circle', source: 'trees-geojson',\n  filter: ['!', ['has', 'point_count']],\n  paint: { 'circle-color': '#2e7d32', 'circle-radius': 5 } });\n\nmap.on('click', 'clusters', async (e) => {\n  const f = map.queryRenderedFeatures(e.point, { layers: ['clusters'] })[0];\n  const zoom = await map.getSource('trees-geojson').getClusterExpansionZoom(f.properties.cluster_id);\n  map.easeTo({ center: f.geometry.coordinates, zoom });\n});\n```\n\nClient-side clustering loads **all points** into the browser, so it suits up to roughly tens of thousands of points. For larger data, cluster **server-side** (e.g. grid aggregation with `ST_SnapToGrid` or `ST_ClusterDBSCAN` in the tile function, or Tippecanoe's `--cluster-distance`) and ship the aggregates as tiles.\n\n","lineStart":545,"lineEnd":574,"html":"<h2>4.7.2 Clustering (client-side, GeoJSON source)</h2>\n<pre><code class=\"language-js\">map.addSource(&#39;trees-geojson&#39;, {\n  type: &#39;geojson&#39;,\n  data: &#39;http://localhost:9000/collections/city.trees/items?limit=1000&#39;,  // server caps `limit`\n  cluster: true, clusterRadius: 50, clusterMaxZoom: 14\n});\nmap.addLayer({ id: &#39;clusters&#39;, type: &#39;circle&#39;, source: &#39;trees-geojson&#39;,\n  filter: [&#39;has&#39;, &#39;point_count&#39;],\n  paint: { &#39;circle-color&#39;: [&#39;step&#39;, [&#39;get&#39;, &#39;point_count&#39;], &#39;#51bbd6&#39;, 100, &#39;#f1f075&#39;, 750, &#39;#f28cb1&#39;],\n           &#39;circle-radius&#39;: [&#39;step&#39;, [&#39;get&#39;, &#39;point_count&#39;], 15, 100, 20, 750, 25] } });\nmap.addLayer({ id: &#39;cluster-count&#39;, type: &#39;symbol&#39;, source: &#39;trees-geojson&#39;,\n  filter: [&#39;has&#39;, &#39;point_count&#39;],\n  layout: { &#39;text-field&#39;: &#39;{point_count_abbreviated}&#39;,\n            &#39;text-font&#39;: [&#39;Open Sans Semibold&#39;],   // must exist in the style&#39;s `glyphs` source\n            &#39;text-size&#39;: 12 } });\nmap.addLayer({ id: &#39;unclustered&#39;, type: &#39;circle&#39;, source: &#39;trees-geojson&#39;,\n  filter: [&#39;!&#39;, [&#39;has&#39;, &#39;point_count&#39;]],\n  paint: { &#39;circle-color&#39;: &#39;#2e7d32&#39;, &#39;circle-radius&#39;: 5 } });\n\nmap.on(&#39;click&#39;, &#39;clusters&#39;, async (e) =&gt; {\n  const f = map.queryRenderedFeatures(e.point, { layers: [&#39;clusters&#39;] })[0];\n  const zoom = await map.getSource(&#39;trees-geojson&#39;).getClusterExpansionZoom(f.properties.cluster_id);\n  map.easeTo({ center: f.geometry.coordinates, zoom });\n});</code></pre><p>Client-side clustering loads <strong>all points</strong> into the browser, so it suits up to roughly tens of thousands of points. For larger data, cluster <strong>server-side</strong> (e.g. grid aggregation with <code>ST_SnapToGrid</code> or <code>ST_ClusterDBSCAN</code> in the tile function, or Tippecanoe&#39;s <code>--cluster-distance</code>) and ship the aggregates as tiles.</p>\n","sha256":"c16bbdcd09ee20215e3824f29c218d14690e4a76258f8bb5394720aa9f108a37"},{"key":"4.7.3","id":"4-7-3","title":"4.7.3 Heatmap","anchor":"473-heatmap","depth":4,"start":30682,"topics":["styling"],"end":31574,"markdown":"#### 4.7.3 Heatmap\n\n```js\nmap.addLayer({\n  id: 'trees-heat', type: 'heatmap', source: 'trees', 'source-layer': 'trees', maxzoom: 15,\n  paint: {\n    'heatmap-weight': ['interpolate', ['linear'], ['coalesce', ['get', 'height_m'], 1], 0, 0, 30, 1],\n    'heatmap-intensity': ['interpolate', ['linear'], ['zoom'], 0, 1, 15, 3],\n    'heatmap-radius': ['interpolate', ['linear'], ['zoom'], 0, 2, 15, 20],\n    'heatmap-color': ['interpolate', ['linear'], ['heatmap-density'],\n      0, 'rgba(33,102,172,0)', 0.2, 'rgb(103,169,207)', 0.4, 'rgb(209,229,240)',\n      0.6, 'rgb(253,219,199)', 0.8, 'rgb(239,138,98)', 1, 'rgb(178,24,43)'],\n    'heatmap-opacity': 0.8\n  }\n});\n```\n\nA heatmap is a *kernel density* rendering of point weights; it is a visual summary, not a statistical estimate. Use it on an **un-clustered** source, and fade it out (`maxzoom`) in favour of individual points when zoomed in.\n\n","lineStart":575,"lineEnd":593,"html":"<h2>4.7.3 Heatmap</h2>\n<pre><code class=\"language-js\">map.addLayer({\n  id: &#39;trees-heat&#39;, type: &#39;heatmap&#39;, source: &#39;trees&#39;, &#39;source-layer&#39;: &#39;trees&#39;, maxzoom: 15,\n  paint: {\n    &#39;heatmap-weight&#39;: [&#39;interpolate&#39;, [&#39;linear&#39;], [&#39;coalesce&#39;, [&#39;get&#39;, &#39;height_m&#39;], 1], 0, 0, 30, 1],\n    &#39;heatmap-intensity&#39;: [&#39;interpolate&#39;, [&#39;linear&#39;], [&#39;zoom&#39;], 0, 1, 15, 3],\n    &#39;heatmap-radius&#39;: [&#39;interpolate&#39;, [&#39;linear&#39;], [&#39;zoom&#39;], 0, 2, 15, 20],\n    &#39;heatmap-color&#39;: [&#39;interpolate&#39;, [&#39;linear&#39;], [&#39;heatmap-density&#39;],\n      0, &#39;rgba(33,102,172,0)&#39;, 0.2, &#39;rgb(103,169,207)&#39;, 0.4, &#39;rgb(209,229,240)&#39;,\n      0.6, &#39;rgb(253,219,199)&#39;, 0.8, &#39;rgb(239,138,98)&#39;, 1, &#39;rgb(178,24,43)&#39;],\n    &#39;heatmap-opacity&#39;: 0.8\n  }\n});</code></pre><p>A heatmap is a <em>kernel density</em> rendering of point weights; it is a visual summary, not a statistical estimate. Use it on an <strong>un-clustered</strong> source, and fade it out (<code>maxzoom</code>) in favour of individual points when zoomed in.</p>\n","sha256":"04f46ab738c869a1dc38837ab7cc128a250c31f55b0e8176f1abba5e8f86872c"},{"key":"4.7.4","id":"4-7-4","title":"4.7.4 OpenLayers: drawing, modifying, snapping","anchor":"474-openlayers-drawing-modifying-snapping","depth":4,"start":31574,"topics":["styling"],"end":32779,"markdown":"#### 4.7.4 OpenLayers: drawing, modifying, snapping\n\n```js\nimport Map from 'ol/Map.js';\nimport View from 'ol/View.js';\nimport TileLayer from 'ol/layer/Tile.js';\nimport OSM from 'ol/source/OSM.js';\nimport VectorLayer from 'ol/layer/Vector.js';\nimport VectorSource from 'ol/source/Vector.js';\nimport { Draw, Modify, Snap } from 'ol/interaction.js';\nimport { fromLonLat } from 'ol/proj.js';\n\nconst source = new VectorSource();\nconst map = new Map({\n  target: 'map',\n  layers: [new TileLayer({ source: new OSM() }), new VectorLayer({ source })],\n  view: new View({ center: fromLonLat([4.9, 52.37]), zoom: 12 })   // view CRS is EPSG:3857\n});\nmap.addInteraction(new Modify({ source }));\nmap.addInteraction(new Draw({ source, type: 'Point' }));\nmap.addInteraction(new Snap({ source }));      // Snap must be added LAST\n```\n\nOpenLayers features live in the **view projection** (EPSG:3857 by default). Convert to EPSG:4326 before sending to an API: `new GeoJSON().writeFeatureObject(feature, { dataProjection: 'EPSG:4326', featureProjection: 'EPSG:3857' })`. For WFS-T, `ol/format/WFS#writeTransaction(inserts, updates, deletes, { featureNS, featurePrefix, featureType, srsName })` builds the XML shown earlier.\n\n","lineStart":594,"lineEnd":618,"html":"<h2>4.7.4 OpenLayers: drawing, modifying, snapping</h2>\n<pre><code class=\"language-js\">import Map from &#39;ol/Map.js&#39;;\nimport View from &#39;ol/View.js&#39;;\nimport TileLayer from &#39;ol/layer/Tile.js&#39;;\nimport OSM from &#39;ol/source/OSM.js&#39;;\nimport VectorLayer from &#39;ol/layer/Vector.js&#39;;\nimport VectorSource from &#39;ol/source/Vector.js&#39;;\nimport { Draw, Modify, Snap } from &#39;ol/interaction.js&#39;;\nimport { fromLonLat } from &#39;ol/proj.js&#39;;\n\nconst source = new VectorSource();\nconst map = new Map({\n  target: &#39;map&#39;,\n  layers: [new TileLayer({ source: new OSM() }), new VectorLayer({ source })],\n  view: new View({ center: fromLonLat([4.9, 52.37]), zoom: 12 })   // view CRS is EPSG:3857\n});\nmap.addInteraction(new Modify({ source }));\nmap.addInteraction(new Draw({ source, type: &#39;Point&#39; }));\nmap.addInteraction(new Snap({ source }));      // Snap must be added LAST</code></pre><p>OpenLayers features live in the <strong>view projection</strong> (EPSG:3857 by default). Convert to EPSG:4326 before sending to an API: <code>new GeoJSON().writeFeatureObject(feature, { dataProjection: &#39;EPSG:4326&#39;, featureProjection: &#39;EPSG:3857&#39; })</code>. For WFS-T, <code>ol/format/WFS#writeTransaction(inserts, updates, deletes, { featureNS, featurePrefix, featureType, srsName })</code> builds the XML shown earlier.</p>\n","sha256":"b56a35d9282bd2ccd9d3cabf07db4829634b7acb1be9fbf938eed023fad723cd"},{"key":"4.7.5","id":"4-7-5","title":"4.7.5 Leaflet, CesiumJS and deck.gl in one glance","anchor":"475-leaflet-cesiumjs-and-deckgl-in-one-glance","depth":4,"start":32779,"topics":["services"],"end":34090,"markdown":"#### 4.7.5 Leaflet, CesiumJS and deck.gl in one glance\n\n```js\n// Leaflet: minimal GeoJSON overlay (EPSG:3857 raster basemap, data in lon/lat; Leaflet takes [lat, lon] for LatLng)\nconst lmap = L.map('map').setView([52.37, 4.9], 12);\nL.tileLayer('https://tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png', { attribution: '© OpenStreetMap contributors' }).addTo(lmap);\nfetch('/collections/city.trees/items?limit=500').then(r => r.json()).then(gj => L.geoJSON(gj).addTo(lmap));\n```\n\n> Public OpenStreetMap tile servers have a usage policy and are not meant for production traffic; use a commercial/self-hosted basemap for real deployments and always show attribution.\n\n```js\n// deck.gl as an overlay on MapLibre: GPU-rendered points\nimport { MapboxOverlay } from '@deck.gl/mapbox';\nimport { ScatterplotLayer } from '@deck.gl/layers';\nmap.addControl(new MapboxOverlay({\n  interleaved: true,\n  layers: [new ScatterplotLayer({ id: 'pts', data: points,           // [{coordinates:[lon,lat]}, …]\n    getPosition: d => d.coordinates, getRadius: 4, radiusUnits: 'pixels', getFillColor: [200, 30, 30] })]\n}));\n```\n\nCesiumJS is chosen when you need a **3D globe**, terrain or **3D Tiles** (buildings, point clouds); its coordinates are Cartesian positions on the WGS 84 ellipsoid (`Cesium.Cartesian3.fromDegrees(lon, lat, height)`).\n\n","lineStart":619,"lineEnd":642,"html":"<h2>4.7.5 Leaflet, CesiumJS and deck.gl in one glance</h2>\n<pre><code class=\"language-js\">// Leaflet: minimal GeoJSON overlay (EPSG:3857 raster basemap, data in lon/lat; Leaflet takes [lat, lon] for LatLng)\nconst lmap = L.map(&#39;map&#39;).setView([52.37, 4.9], 12);\nL.tileLayer(&#39;https://tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png&#39;, { attribution: &#39;© OpenStreetMap contributors&#39; }).addTo(lmap);\nfetch(&#39;/collections/city.trees/items?limit=500&#39;).then(r =&gt; r.json()).then(gj =&gt; L.geoJSON(gj).addTo(lmap));</code></pre><blockquote>\n<p>Public OpenStreetMap tile servers have a usage policy and are not meant for production traffic; use a commercial/self-hosted basemap for real deployments and always show attribution.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<pre><code class=\"language-js\">// deck.gl as an overlay on MapLibre: GPU-rendered points\nimport { MapboxOverlay } from &#39;@deck.gl/mapbox&#39;;\nimport { ScatterplotLayer } from &#39;@deck.gl/layers&#39;;\nmap.addControl(new MapboxOverlay({\n  interleaved: true,\n  layers: [new ScatterplotLayer({ id: &#39;pts&#39;, data: points,           // [{coordinates:[lon,lat]}, …]\n    getPosition: d =&gt; d.coordinates, getRadius: 4, radiusUnits: &#39;pixels&#39;, getFillColor: [200, 30, 30] })]\n}));</code></pre><p>CesiumJS is chosen when you need a <strong>3D globe</strong>, terrain or <strong>3D Tiles</strong> (buildings, point clouds); its coordinates are Cartesian positions on the WGS 84 ellipsoid (<code>Cesium.Cartesian3.fromDegrees(lon, lat, height)</code>).</p>\n","sha256":"36af9c8e189df36f69450ce136155f411c379d3fb717436a639847c254bd3ceb"},{"key":"4.8","id":"4-8","title":"4.8 Performance engineering","anchor":"48-performance-engineering","depth":3,"start":34090,"topics":["performance"],"end":35815,"markdown":"### 4.8 Performance engineering\n\n| Lever | Technique | Why it works |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Tile budget** | Keep tiles well below ~500 KB (many teams target 100–200 KB) and a few thousand features | Download + decode + GPU upload dominate perceived latency |\n| **Simplification** | Tolerance ≈ one pixel: `ε(z) = res(z)`; use topology-preserving simplification for polygons that share borders | Sub-pixel vertices are invisible |\n| **LOD rules** | Per-zoom attribute filters (e.g. only major roads at z < 8), area/length thresholds, `minzoom`/`maxzoom` on layers | Fewer, more meaningful features at small scale |\n| **Aggregation** | Cluster/grid/hexbin at low zoom; show individuals at high zoom | Avoids drawing 10⁶ overlapping points |\n| **Indexing** | GiST on geometry (store a 3857 copy if tile-heavy), `&&` before exact predicates, `EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)` | Avoids sequential scans per tile |\n| **Pre-generation** | Tippecanoe/tilemaker for static or slow-changing data; PMTiles on object storage + CDN | Zero database load at read time |\n| **HTTP caching** | `Cache-Control: public, max-age=…`, `ETag`, gzip/brotli; CDN in front | Most tile requests never reach your origin |\n| **Invalidation** | After an edit, purge only tiles intersecting the changed bbox for each affected zoom (use the tile math from 4.1) | Cheap, targeted cache freshness |\n| **Client** | Source `minzoom/maxzoom`, `maxTileCacheSize`, avoid hundreds of layers, prefer expressions over many filtered layers | Less style evaluation per frame |\n\n**Level-of-detail rule of thumb.** A feature is worth drawing if its on-screen size is at least a few pixels. For polygons: `area_m2 ≥ k · res(z)²` with `k ≈ 16`; for lines: `length_m ≥ ~8 · res(z)`.\n\n","lineStart":643,"lineEnd":658,"html":"<h2>4.8 Performance engineering</h2>\n<div class=\"source-table\" role=\"region\" tabindex=\"0\" aria-label=\"Table in 4.8 Performance engineering\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Lever</th>\n<th>Technique</th>\n<th>Why it works</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Tile budget</strong></td>\n<td>Keep tiles well below ~500 KB (many teams target 100–200 KB) and a few thousand features</td>\n<td>Download + decode + GPU upload dominate perceived latency</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Simplification</strong></td>\n<td>Tolerance ≈ one pixel: <code>ε(z) = res(z)</code>; use topology-preserving simplification for polygons that share borders</td>\n<td>Sub-pixel vertices are invisible</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>LOD rules</strong></td>\n<td>Per-zoom attribute filters (e.g. only major roads at z &lt; 8), area/length thresholds, <code>minzoom</code>/<code>maxzoom</code> on layers</td>\n<td>Fewer, more meaningful features at small scale</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Aggregation</strong></td>\n<td>Cluster/grid/hexbin at low zoom; show individuals at high zoom</td>\n<td>Avoids drawing 10⁶ overlapping points</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Indexing</strong></td>\n<td>GiST on geometry (store a 3857 copy if tile-heavy), <code>&amp;&amp;</code> before exact predicates, <code>EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)</code></td>\n<td>Avoids sequential scans per tile</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Pre-generation</strong></td>\n<td>Tippecanoe/tilemaker for static or slow-changing data; PMTiles on object storage + CDN</td>\n<td>Zero database load at read time</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>HTTP caching</strong></td>\n<td><code>Cache-Control: public, max-age=…</code>, <code>ETag</code>, gzip/brotli; CDN in front</td>\n<td>Most tile requests never reach your origin</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Invalidation</strong></td>\n<td>After an edit, purge only tiles intersecting the changed bbox for each affected zoom (use the tile math from 4.1)</td>\n<td>Cheap, targeted cache freshness</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Client</strong></td>\n<td>Source <code>minzoom/maxzoom</code>, <code>maxTileCacheSize</code>, avoid hundreds of layers, prefer expressions over many filtered layers</td>\n<td>Less style evaluation per frame</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div><p><strong>Level-of-detail rule of thumb.</strong> A feature is worth drawing if its on-screen size is at least a few pixels. For polygons: <code>area_m2 ≥ k · res(z)²</code> with <code>k ≈ 16</code>; for lines: <code>length_m ≥ ~8 · res(z)</code>.</p>\n","sha256":"d33d8ba23ed3fcaaad6bfd73d381db867809d69c343597a416b4e8873144b07f"},{"key":"4.9","id":"4-9","title":"4.9 Authentication and authorization for spatial data","anchor":"49-authentication-and-authorization-for-spatial-data","depth":3,"start":35815,"topics":["security"],"end":38694,"markdown":"### 4.9 Authentication and authorization for spatial data\n\nSeparate three questions: **Who are you?** (authentication: OIDC/OAuth 2.0 → JWT or session cookie) · **Which layers may you open?** (layer-level authorization) · **Which rows within a layer may you see or change?** (row-level authorization).\n\n| Level | Where to enforce | Mechanisms |\n|---|---|---|\n| Layer | Gateway and/or server | Path-based rules in a reverse proxy (`/tiles/internal/*` needs role `staff`); GeoServer data security rules (workspace/layer vs role); separate server instances or DB roles per tier; `GRANT SELECT` on the table/function |\n| Row | **Database** (authoritative) | PostgreSQL **RLS** policies; GeoServer CQL read filters per role (via its security extensions) for WMS/WFS |\n| Column | Database/API | Column-level `GRANT`, views that omit sensitive columns |\n| Write | Edit API + DB | Role-checked endpoints, `WITH CHECK` policies, validation constraints |\n\n**RLS essentials**\n\n```sql\nALTER TABLE city.trees ENABLE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;\nALTER TABLE city.trees FORCE  ROW LEVEL SECURITY;     -- also applies to the table owner\n\n-- anonymous map readers: public rows only\nCREATE POLICY read_public ON city.trees FOR SELECT TO tile_reader\n  USING (is_public);\n\n-- editors: only their organisation's rows (org passed per transaction by the edit API)\nCREATE POLICY org_rw ON city.trees FOR ALL TO edit_api\n  USING      (owner_org = current_setting('app.org', true))\n  WITH CHECK (owner_org = current_setting('app.org', true));\n```\n\nPolicies for the same command are **OR-ed** (permissive by default); `USING` filters existing rows, `WITH CHECK` validates new/changed rows. Superusers and roles with `BYPASSRLS` ignore policies — never connect a web service with such a role.\n\n**Client side.** Attach credentials to tile and feature requests without placing tokens in URLs (URLs end up in logs):\n\n```js\nconst map = new maplibregl.Map({\n  /* … */\n  transformRequest: (url, resourceType) =>\n    url.startsWith('https://api.example.org/')\n      ? { url, headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${getAccessToken()}` } }\n      : { url }\n});\n```\n\n**The cache-leak trap.** Tiles are cached by URL. If a tile's content depends on the user, a shared cache or CDN may serve user A's tile to user B. Either (a) serve user-specific tiles with `Cache-Control: private` (or `no-store`) and `Vary: Authorization`, (b) include the access tier in the cache key (`/tiles/{tier}/{z}/{x}/{y}`), or (c) keep tiles public-only and fetch protected attributes through the authenticated feature API.\n\n**Single-role servers.** Out of the box, pg_tileserv, pg_featureserv and Martin typically connect with *one* database role, so per-user RLS requires either one server instance and DB role per access tier behind the gateway, or a custom endpoint that sets a per-request session variable (as the edit API in Section 5 does).\n\n---\n\n","lineStart":659,"lineEnd":705,"html":"<h2>4.9 Authentication and authorization for spatial data</h2>\n<p>Separate three questions: <strong>Who are you?</strong> (authentication: OIDC/OAuth 2.0 → JWT or session cookie) · <strong>Which layers may you open?</strong> (layer-level authorization) · <strong>Which rows within a layer may you see or change?</strong> (row-level authorization).</p>\n<div class=\"source-table\" role=\"region\" tabindex=\"0\" aria-label=\"Table in 4.9 Authentication and authorization for spatial data\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Level</th>\n<th>Where to enforce</th>\n<th>Mechanisms</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Layer</td>\n<td>Gateway and/or server</td>\n<td>Path-based rules in a reverse proxy (<code>/tiles/internal/*</code> needs role <code>staff</code>); GeoServer data security rules (workspace/layer vs role); separate server instances or DB roles per tier; <code>GRANT SELECT</code> on the table/function</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Row</td>\n<td><strong>Database</strong> (authoritative)</td>\n<td>PostgreSQL <strong>RLS</strong> policies; GeoServer CQL read filters per role (via its security extensions) for WMS/WFS</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Column</td>\n<td>Database/API</td>\n<td>Column-level <code>GRANT</code>, views that omit sensitive columns</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Write</td>\n<td>Edit API + DB</td>\n<td>Role-checked endpoints, <code>WITH CHECK</code> policies, validation constraints</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div><p><strong>RLS essentials</strong></p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-sql\">ALTER TABLE city.trees ENABLE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;\nALTER TABLE city.trees FORCE  ROW LEVEL SECURITY;     -- also applies to the table owner\n\n-- anonymous map readers: public rows only\nCREATE POLICY read_public ON city.trees FOR SELECT TO tile_reader\n  USING (is_public);\n\n-- editors: only their organisation&#39;s rows (org passed per transaction by the edit API)\nCREATE POLICY org_rw ON city.trees FOR ALL TO edit_api\n  USING      (owner_org = current_setting(&#39;app.org&#39;, true))\n  WITH CHECK (owner_org = current_setting(&#39;app.org&#39;, true));</code></pre><p>Policies for the same command are <strong>OR-ed</strong> (permissive by default); <code>USING</code> filters existing rows, <code>WITH CHECK</code> validates new/changed rows. Superusers and roles with <code>BYPASSRLS</code> ignore policies — never connect a web service with such a role.</p>\n<p><strong>Client side.</strong> Attach credentials to tile and feature requests without placing tokens in URLs (URLs end up in logs):</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-js\">const map = new maplibregl.Map({\n  /* … */\n  transformRequest: (url, resourceType) =&gt;\n    url.startsWith(&#39;https://api.example.org/&#39;)\n      ? { url, headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${getAccessToken()}` } }\n      : { url }\n});</code></pre><p><strong>The cache-leak trap.</strong> Tiles are cached by URL. If a tile&#39;s content depends on the user, a shared cache or CDN may serve user A&#39;s tile to user B. Either (a) serve user-specific tiles with <code>Cache-Control: private</code> (or <code>no-store</code>) and <code>Vary: Authorization</code>, (b) include the access tier in the cache key (<code>/tiles/{tier}/{z}/{x}/{y}</code>), or (c) keep tiles public-only and fetch protected attributes through the authenticated feature API.</p>\n<p><strong>Single-role servers.</strong> Out of the box, pg_tileserv, pg_featureserv and Martin typically connect with <em>one</em> database role, so per-user RLS requires either one server instance and DB role per access tier behind the gateway, or a custom endpoint that sets a per-request session variable (as the edit API in Section 5 does).</p>\n<hr>\n","sha256":"82e93d64bfbed5cfb96b755700b300a524062c43519864601de373505f5d9fc2"},{"key":"5","id":"5","title":"5. Worked GIS example — an editable street-tree inventory","anchor":"5-worked-gis-example--an-editable-street-tree-inventory","depth":2,"start":38694,"topics":["inventory"],"end":38756,"markdown":"## 5. Worked GIS example — an editable street-tree inventory\n\n","lineStart":706,"lineEnd":707,"html":"<h2>5. Worked GIS example — an editable street-tree inventory</h2>\n","sha256":"eefea8ea52a9662bc8427e5f483f378196fc920fc3381035a413a2b43824ee29"},{"key":"5.1","id":"5-1","title":"5.1 Scenario","anchor":"51-scenario","depth":3,"start":38756,"topics":["inventory"],"end":39972,"markdown":"### 5.1 Scenario\n\nTwo organisations, **`parks`** and **`harbor`**, maintain street trees in the same database. The public may see all trees flagged `is_public`. Staff of each organisation may additionally see, create, edit and delete **their own** trees (including non-public ones) through an authenticated API. The stack:\n\n```\nPostGIS 16 ──► pg_tileserv  (public vector tiles, role tile_reader)\n          ├──► pg_featureserv (public OGC API – Features, role tile_reader)\n          └──► Edit API (FastAPI, role edit_api, JWT → org, RLS enforced)\n                                  ▲\n                    MapLibre web client (tiles + editor layer)\n```\n\nThe sample coordinates are **illustrative** (WGS 84 lon/lat, EPSG:4326) and were chosen so that tile results can be verified by the tile math in Section 4.1.\n\n| id | species | org | public | lon | lat | tile @ z12 |\n|---:|---|---|:---:|---:|---:|---|\n| 1 | Oak | parks | yes | 4.8952 | 52.3702 | 12/2103/1346 |\n| 2 | Linden | parks | yes | 4.9041 | 52.3676 | 12/2103/1346 |\n| 3 | Maple | parks | **no** | 4.8841 | 52.3731 | 12/2103/1346 |\n| 4 | Plane | harbor | yes | 4.9122 | 52.3601 | 12/2103/1346 |\n| 5 | Birch | harbor | yes | 4.9320 | 52.3790 | 12/2104/1345 |\n\n","lineStart":708,"lineEnd":729,"html":"<h2>5.1 Scenario</h2>\n<p>Two organisations, <strong><code>parks</code></strong> and <strong><code>harbor</code></strong>, maintain street trees in the same database. The public may see all trees flagged <code>is_public</code>. Staff of each organisation may additionally see, create, edit and delete <strong>their own</strong> trees (including non-public ones) through an authenticated API. The stack:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">PostGIS 16 ──► pg_tileserv  (public vector tiles, role tile_reader)\n          ├──► pg_featureserv (public OGC API – Features, role tile_reader)\n          └──► Edit API (FastAPI, role edit_api, JWT → org, RLS enforced)\n                                  ▲\n                    MapLibre web client (tiles + editor layer)</code></pre><p>The sample coordinates are <strong>illustrative</strong> (WGS 84 lon/lat, EPSG:4326) and were chosen so that tile results can be verified by the tile math in Section 4.1.</p>\n<div class=\"source-table\" role=\"region\" tabindex=\"0\" aria-label=\"Table in 5.1 Scenario\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th align=\"right\">id</th>\n<th>species</th>\n<th>org</th>\n<th align=\"center\">public</th>\n<th align=\"right\">lon</th>\n<th align=\"right\">lat</th>\n<th>tile @ z12</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"right\">1</td>\n<td>Oak</td>\n<td>parks</td>\n<td align=\"center\">yes</td>\n<td align=\"right\">4.8952</td>\n<td align=\"right\">52.3702</td>\n<td>12/2103/1346</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"right\">2</td>\n<td>Linden</td>\n<td>parks</td>\n<td align=\"center\">yes</td>\n<td align=\"right\">4.9041</td>\n<td align=\"right\">52.3676</td>\n<td>12/2103/1346</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"right\">3</td>\n<td>Maple</td>\n<td>parks</td>\n<td align=\"center\"><strong>no</strong></td>\n<td align=\"right\">4.8841</td>\n<td align=\"right\">52.3731</td>\n<td>12/2103/1346</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"right\">4</td>\n<td>Plane</td>\n<td>harbor</td>\n<td align=\"center\">yes</td>\n<td align=\"right\">4.9122</td>\n<td align=\"right\">52.3601</td>\n<td>12/2103/1346</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"right\">5</td>\n<td>Birch</td>\n<td>harbor</td>\n<td align=\"center\">yes</td>\n<td align=\"right\">4.9320</td>\n<td align=\"right\">52.3790</td>\n<td>12/2104/1345</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>","sha256":"e218a664fe7ba74316d31be8a2727d9f1126aee3a4605d4fa28f462cd82e6fb5"},{"key":"5.2","id":"5-2","title":"5.2 Step 1 — Run the database and servers","anchor":"52-step-1--run-the-database-and-servers","depth":3,"start":39972,"topics":["inventory"],"end":40699,"markdown":"### 5.2 Step 1 — Run the database and servers\n\n`docker-compose.yml` (pin exact image tags in real projects instead of `latest`):\n\n```yaml\nservices:\n  db:\n    image: postgis/postgis:16-3.4\n    environment: { POSTGRES_PASSWORD: postgres, POSTGRES_DB: gis }\n    ports: [\"5432:5432\"]\n    volumes: [\"./initdb:/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d:ro\"]   # *.sql run once, alphabetically\n  tiles:\n    image: pramsey/pg_tileserv:latest\n    environment: { DATABASE_URL: \"postgresql://tile_reader:reader_pw@db/gis\" }\n    ports: [\"7800:7800\"]\n    depends_on: [db]\n  features:\n    image: pramsey/pg_featureserv:latest\n    environment: { DATABASE_URL: \"postgresql://tile_reader:reader_pw@db/gis\" }\n    ports: [\"9000:9000\"]\n    depends_on: [db]\n```\n\n","lineStart":730,"lineEnd":752,"html":"<h2>5.2 Step 1 — Run the database and servers</h2>\n<p><code>docker-compose.yml</code> (pin exact image tags in real projects instead of <code>latest</code>):</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-yaml\">services:\n  db:\n    image: postgis/postgis:16-3.4\n    environment: { POSTGRES_PASSWORD: postgres, POSTGRES_DB: gis }\n    ports: [&quot;5432:5432&quot;]\n    volumes: [&quot;./initdb:/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d:ro&quot;]   # *.sql run once, alphabetically\n  tiles:\n    image: pramsey/pg_tileserv:latest\n    environment: { DATABASE_URL: &quot;postgresql://tile_reader:reader_pw@db/gis&quot; }\n    ports: [&quot;7800:7800&quot;]\n    depends_on: [db]\n  features:\n    image: pramsey/pg_featureserv:latest\n    environment: { DATABASE_URL: &quot;postgresql://tile_reader:reader_pw@db/gis&quot; }\n    ports: [&quot;9000:9000&quot;]\n    depends_on: [db]</code></pre>","sha256":"769772b9ed7832e60996caf612506be95e7a720932eadc92d14fbcda6edb739a"},{"key":"5.3","id":"5-3","title":"5.3 Step 2 — Schema, roles, security, data (`initdb/01_schema.sql`)","anchor":"53-step-2--schema-roles-security-data-initdb01_schemasql","depth":3,"start":40699,"topics":["inventory"],"end":43973,"markdown":"### 5.3 Step 2 — Schema, roles, security, data (`initdb/01_schema.sql`)\n\n```sql\nCREATE EXTENSION IF NOT EXISTS postgis;\nCREATE SCHEMA city;\nCREATE SCHEMA postgisftw;\n\nCREATE TABLE city.trees (\n  id         bigint GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY PRIMARY KEY,\n  species    text        NOT NULL,\n  height_m   numeric(5,2) CHECK (height_m >= 0),\n  condition  text        NOT NULL CHECK (condition IN ('good','fair','poor')),\n  owner_org  text        NOT NULL,\n  is_public  boolean     NOT NULL DEFAULT true,\n  version    integer     NOT NULL DEFAULT 1,\n  updated_at timestamptz NOT NULL DEFAULT now(),\n  geom       geometry(Point, 4326) NOT NULL\n);\nCREATE INDEX trees_geom_gix ON city.trees USING gist (geom);\n\nCREATE TABLE city.edit_log (\n  id       bigint GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY PRIMARY KEY,\n  tree_id  bigint,\n  user_sub text,\n  action   text NOT NULL,\n  at       timestamptz NOT NULL DEFAULT now()\n);\n\n-- Roles: one for anonymous map readers, one for the edit API (neither is a superuser)\nCREATE ROLE tile_reader LOGIN PASSWORD 'reader_pw';\nCREATE ROLE edit_api    LOGIN PASSWORD 'edit_pw';\nGRANT USAGE ON SCHEMA city, postgisftw TO tile_reader, edit_api;\nGRANT SELECT ON city.trees TO tile_reader;\nGRANT SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE ON city.trees TO edit_api;\nGRANT INSERT ON city.edit_log TO edit_api;\nGRANT SELECT ON spatial_ref_sys TO tile_reader, edit_api;   -- ST_Transform reads it (usually already open to PUBLIC; shown for clarity)\n\n-- Row-level security\nALTER TABLE city.trees ENABLE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;\nALTER TABLE city.trees FORCE  ROW LEVEL SECURITY;\nCREATE POLICY read_public ON city.trees FOR SELECT TO tile_reader USING (is_public);\nCREATE POLICY org_rw      ON city.trees FOR ALL    TO edit_api\n  USING      (owner_org = current_setting('app.org', true))\n  WITH CHECK (owner_org = current_setting('app.org', true));\n\n-- Audit trigger (user identity arrives through a transaction-local setting)\nCREATE FUNCTION city.log_tree_edit() RETURNS trigger LANGUAGE plpgsql AS $$\nBEGIN\n  IF TG_OP = 'DELETE' THEN\n    INSERT INTO city.edit_log(tree_id, user_sub, action) VALUES (OLD.id, current_setting('app.user', true), TG_OP);\n    RETURN OLD;\n  ELSE\n    INSERT INTO city.edit_log(tree_id, user_sub, action) VALUES (NEW.id, current_setting('app.user', true), TG_OP);\n    RETURN NEW;\n  END IF;\nEND $$;\nCREATE TRIGGER trees_log AFTER INSERT OR UPDATE OR DELETE ON city.trees\n  FOR EACH ROW EXECUTE FUNCTION city.log_tree_edit();\n\n-- Seed data (loaded as the superuser during init, which bypasses RLS)\nINSERT INTO city.trees (species, height_m, condition, owner_org, is_public, geom) VALUES\n ('Oak',    14.2, 'good', 'parks',  true,  ST_SetSRID(ST_MakePoint(4.8952, 52.3702), 4326)),\n ('Linden', 11.0, 'fair', 'parks',  true,  ST_SetSRID(ST_MakePoint(4.9041, 52.3676), 4326)),\n ('Maple',   9.5, 'poor', 'parks',  false, ST_SetSRID(ST_MakePoint(4.8841, 52.3731), 4326)),\n ('Plane',  18.7, 'good', 'harbor', true,  ST_SetSRID(ST_MakePoint(4.9122, 52.3601), 4326)),\n ('Birch',   8.1, 'fair', 'harbor', true,  ST_SetSRID(ST_MakePoint(4.9320, 52.3790), 4326));\n```\n\n> The ER diagram in Section 3.5 shows the *logical* model. In this lab the identity provider owns organisations and users, so `owner_org` and `user_sub` are plain text columns rather than foreign keys.\n\n","lineStart":753,"lineEnd":822,"html":"<h2>5.3 Step 2 — Schema, roles, security, data (<code>initdb/01_schema.sql</code>)</h2>\n<pre><code class=\"language-sql\">CREATE EXTENSION IF NOT EXISTS postgis;\nCREATE SCHEMA city;\nCREATE SCHEMA postgisftw;\n\nCREATE TABLE city.trees (\n  id         bigint GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY PRIMARY KEY,\n  species    text        NOT NULL,\n  height_m   numeric(5,2) CHECK (height_m &gt;= 0),\n  condition  text        NOT NULL CHECK (condition IN (&#39;good&#39;,&#39;fair&#39;,&#39;poor&#39;)),\n  owner_org  text        NOT NULL,\n  is_public  boolean     NOT NULL DEFAULT true,\n  version    integer     NOT NULL DEFAULT 1,\n  updated_at timestamptz NOT NULL DEFAULT now(),\n  geom       geometry(Point, 4326) NOT NULL\n);\nCREATE INDEX trees_geom_gix ON city.trees USING gist (geom);\n\nCREATE TABLE city.edit_log (\n  id       bigint GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY PRIMARY KEY,\n  tree_id  bigint,\n  user_sub text,\n  action   text NOT NULL,\n  at       timestamptz NOT NULL DEFAULT now()\n);\n\n-- Roles: one for anonymous map readers, one for the edit API (neither is a superuser)\nCREATE ROLE tile_reader LOGIN PASSWORD &#39;reader_pw&#39;;\nCREATE ROLE edit_api    LOGIN PASSWORD &#39;edit_pw&#39;;\nGRANT USAGE ON SCHEMA city, postgisftw TO tile_reader, edit_api;\nGRANT SELECT ON city.trees TO tile_reader;\nGRANT SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE ON city.trees TO edit_api;\nGRANT INSERT ON city.edit_log TO edit_api;\nGRANT SELECT ON spatial_ref_sys TO tile_reader, edit_api;   -- ST_Transform reads it (usually already open to PUBLIC; shown for clarity)\n\n-- Row-level security\nALTER TABLE city.trees ENABLE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;\nALTER TABLE city.trees FORCE  ROW LEVEL SECURITY;\nCREATE POLICY read_public ON city.trees FOR SELECT TO tile_reader USING (is_public);\nCREATE POLICY org_rw      ON city.trees FOR ALL    TO edit_api\n  USING      (owner_org = current_setting(&#39;app.org&#39;, true))\n  WITH CHECK (owner_org = current_setting(&#39;app.org&#39;, true));\n\n-- Audit trigger (user identity arrives through a transaction-local setting)\nCREATE FUNCTION city.log_tree_edit() RETURNS trigger LANGUAGE plpgsql AS $$\nBEGIN\n  IF TG_OP = &#39;DELETE&#39; THEN\n    INSERT INTO city.edit_log(tree_id, user_sub, action) VALUES (OLD.id, current_setting(&#39;app.user&#39;, true), TG_OP);\n    RETURN OLD;\n  ELSE\n    INSERT INTO city.edit_log(tree_id, user_sub, action) VALUES (NEW.id, current_setting(&#39;app.user&#39;, true), TG_OP);\n    RETURN NEW;\n  END IF;\nEND $$;\nCREATE TRIGGER trees_log AFTER INSERT OR UPDATE OR DELETE ON city.trees\n  FOR EACH ROW EXECUTE FUNCTION city.log_tree_edit();\n\n-- Seed data (loaded as the superuser during init, which bypasses RLS)\nINSERT INTO city.trees (species, height_m, condition, owner_org, is_public, geom) VALUES\n (&#39;Oak&#39;,    14.2, &#39;good&#39;, &#39;parks&#39;,  true,  ST_SetSRID(ST_MakePoint(4.8952, 52.3702), 4326)),\n (&#39;Linden&#39;, 11.0, &#39;fair&#39;, &#39;parks&#39;,  true,  ST_SetSRID(ST_MakePoint(4.9041, 52.3676), 4326)),\n (&#39;Maple&#39;,   9.5, &#39;poor&#39;, &#39;parks&#39;,  false, ST_SetSRID(ST_MakePoint(4.8841, 52.3731), 4326)),\n (&#39;Plane&#39;,  18.7, &#39;good&#39;, &#39;harbor&#39;, true,  ST_SetSRID(ST_MakePoint(4.9122, 52.3601), 4326)),\n (&#39;Birch&#39;,   8.1, &#39;fair&#39;, &#39;harbor&#39;, true,  ST_SetSRID(ST_MakePoint(4.9320, 52.3790), 4326));</code></pre><blockquote>\n<p>The ER diagram in Section 3.5 shows the <em>logical</em> model. In this lab the identity provider owns organisations and users, so <code>owner_org</code> and <code>user_sub</code> are plain text columns rather than foreign keys.</p>\n</blockquote>\n","sha256":"dc05a00484e53daeb7993710487c7c0928a5378c744758632e8763e51776d16b"},{"key":"5.4","id":"5-4","title":"5.4 Step 3 — Tile function (`initdb/02_tiles.sql`)","anchor":"54-step-3--tile-function-initdb02_tilessql","depth":3,"start":43973,"topics":["mvt"],"end":44385,"markdown":"### 5.4 Step 3 — Tile function (`initdb/02_tiles.sql`)\n\nUse the `postgisftw.trees_mvt(z, x, y)` function from Section 4.3, then:\n\n```sql\nGRANT EXECUTE ON FUNCTION postgisftw.trees_mvt(integer, integer, integer) TO tile_reader;\n```\n\nThe function runs with the caller's rights (`SECURITY INVOKER`, the default), so **RLS applies to tile_reader inside the tile query**: non-public trees never enter a public tile.\n\n","lineStart":823,"lineEnd":832,"html":"<h2>5.4 Step 3 — Tile function (<code>initdb/02_tiles.sql</code>)</h2>\n<p>Use the <code>postgisftw.trees_mvt(z, x, y)</code> function from Section 4.3, then:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-sql\">GRANT EXECUTE ON FUNCTION postgisftw.trees_mvt(integer, integer, integer) TO tile_reader;</code></pre><p>The function runs with the caller&#39;s rights (<code>SECURITY INVOKER</code>, the default), so <strong>RLS applies to tile_reader inside the tile query</strong>: non-public trees never enter a public tile.</p>\n","sha256":"65af06dbf161d4b42a332bb3c4326f0591813a72e4d7f126b6e4763ecda4edbd"},{"key":"5.5","id":"5-5","title":"5.5 Step 4 — Verify the read path","anchor":"55-step-4--verify-the-read-path","depth":3,"start":44385,"topics":["inventory"],"end":45905,"markdown":"### 5.5 Step 4 — Verify the read path\n\n```bash\ndocker compose up -d\n\n# (a) tile server answers with a vector tile\ncurl -s -o /dev/null -w \"%{http_code} %{content_type} %{size_download}\\n\" \\\n  http://localhost:7800/postgisftw.trees_mvt/12/2103/1346.pbf\n# expected: 200, an MVT content type (typically application/vnd.mapbox-vector-tile), size > 0\n\n# (b) OGC API Features through pg_featureserv\ncurl -s \"http://localhost:9000/collections/city.trees/items?limit=10\" | python3 -c \\\n  \"import sys,json; d=json.load(sys.stdin); print(len(d['features']), sorted(f['properties']['species'] for f in d['features']))\"\n# expected: 4 ['Birch', 'Linden', 'Oak', 'Plane']     (Maple is hidden by RLS)\n```\n\nDecode the tile and count features (`pip install requests mapbox-vector-tile`):\n\n```python\nimport requests, mapbox_vector_tile\n\ndef trees_in(z, x, y):\n    r = requests.get(f\"http://localhost:7800/postgisftw.trees_mvt/{z}/{x}/{y}.pbf\", timeout=10)\n    r.raise_for_status()\n    tile = mapbox_vector_tile.decode(r.content)           # {'trees': {'extent': 4096, 'features': [...]}}\n    return sorted(f[\"properties\"][\"species\"] for f in tile.get(\"trees\", {}).get(\"features\", []))\n\nprint(trees_in(12, 2103, 1346))   # expected ['Linden', 'Oak', 'Plane']   (Maple hidden by RLS)\nprint(trees_in(12, 2104, 1345))   # expected ['Birch']\n```\n\n**Why these results?** The tile math places trees 1–4 in `12/2103/1346` and tree 5 in `12/2104/1345`. RLS removes the non-public Maple for `tile_reader`, so the first tile holds three features.\n\n","lineStart":833,"lineEnd":865,"html":"<h2>5.5 Step 4 — Verify the read path</h2>\n<pre><code class=\"language-bash\">docker compose up -d\n\n# (a) tile server answers with a vector tile\ncurl -s -o /dev/null -w &quot;%{http_code} %{content_type} %{size_download}\\n&quot; \\\n  http://localhost:7800/postgisftw.trees_mvt/12/2103/1346.pbf\n# expected: 200, an MVT content type (typically application/vnd.mapbox-vector-tile), size &gt; 0\n\n# (b) OGC API Features through pg_featureserv\ncurl -s &quot;http://localhost:9000/collections/city.trees/items?limit=10&quot; | python3 -c \\\n  &quot;import sys,json; d=json.load(sys.stdin); print(len(d[&#39;features&#39;]), sorted(f[&#39;properties&#39;][&#39;species&#39;] for f in d[&#39;features&#39;]))&quot;\n# expected: 4 [&#39;Birch&#39;, &#39;Linden&#39;, &#39;Oak&#39;, &#39;Plane&#39;]     (Maple is hidden by RLS)</code></pre><p>Decode the tile and count features (<code>pip install requests mapbox-vector-tile</code>):</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">import requests, mapbox_vector_tile\n\ndef trees_in(z, x, y):\n    r = requests.get(f&quot;http://localhost:7800/postgisftw.trees_mvt/{z}/{x}/{y}.pbf&quot;, timeout=10)\n    r.raise_for_status()\n    tile = mapbox_vector_tile.decode(r.content)           # {&#39;trees&#39;: {&#39;extent&#39;: 4096, &#39;features&#39;: [...]}}\n    return sorted(f[&quot;properties&quot;][&quot;species&quot;] for f in tile.get(&quot;trees&quot;, {}).get(&quot;features&quot;, []))\n\nprint(trees_in(12, 2103, 1346))   # expected [&#39;Linden&#39;, &#39;Oak&#39;, &#39;Plane&#39;]   (Maple hidden by RLS)\nprint(trees_in(12, 2104, 1345))   # expected [&#39;Birch&#39;]</code></pre><p><strong>Why these results?</strong> The tile math places trees 1–4 in <code>12/2103/1346</code> and tree 5 in <code>12/2104/1345</code>. RLS removes the non-public Maple for <code>tile_reader</code>, so the first tile holds three features.</p>\n","sha256":"41600e8b628e13889496cadf1d533c55bd5dbc7e33f5ade1c9c65aa5cf92e497"},{"key":"5.6","id":"5-6","title":"5.6 Step 5 — The edit API (`edit_api/main.py`)","anchor":"56-step-5--the-edit-api-edit_apimainpy","depth":3,"start":45905,"topics":["editing"],"end":52747,"markdown":"### 5.6 Step 5 — The edit API (`edit_api/main.py`)\n\n```python\nimport os\nfrom contextlib import contextmanager\nfrom typing import Literal, Optional\n\nimport jwt                                  # PyJWT\nimport psycopg\nfrom fastapi import Depends, FastAPI, Header, HTTPException\nfrom fastapi.middleware.cors import CORSMiddleware\nfrom psycopg.rows import dict_row\nfrom pydantic import BaseModel, Field\n\nDSN = os.environ[\"EDIT_DSN\"]               # connects as role edit_api (no BYPASSRLS)\nJWT_SECRET = os.environ[\"JWT_SECRET\"]      # dev only: in production use asymmetric keys (OIDC/JWKS)\n\napp = FastAPI(title=\"Tree edit API\")\napp.add_middleware(CORSMiddleware, allow_origins=[\"http://localhost:8080\"],\n                   allow_methods=[\"GET\", \"POST\", \"PATCH\", \"DELETE\"], allow_headers=[\"Authorization\", \"Content-Type\"])\n\nclass Claims(BaseModel):\n    sub: str\n    org: str\n\ndef claims(authorization: str = Header(...)) -> Claims:\n    scheme, _, token = authorization.partition(\" \")\n    try:\n        if scheme.lower() != \"bearer\":\n            raise ValueError(\"scheme\")\n        data = jwt.decode(token, JWT_SECRET, algorithms=[\"HS256\"])      # checks signature and exp\n        return Claims(sub=data[\"sub\"], org=data[\"org\"])\n    except Exception:\n        raise HTTPException(status_code=401, detail=\"invalid or missing token\")\n\n@contextmanager\ndef session(c: Claims):\n    \"\"\"One transaction per request; identity is visible to RLS policies and the audit trigger.\"\"\"\n    with psycopg.connect(DSN, row_factory=dict_row) as conn:\n        conn.execute(\"SELECT set_config('app.org',  %s, true), set_config('app.user', %s, true)\", (c.org, c.sub))\n        yield conn                          # commit on success, rollback on exception\n\nclass TreeIn(BaseModel):\n    species: str = Field(min_length=1, max_length=80)\n    height_m: Optional[float] = Field(None, ge=0, le=150)\n    condition: Literal[\"good\", \"fair\", \"poor\"]\n    lon: float = Field(ge=-180, le=180)     # note the order: longitude first\n    lat: float = Field(ge=-90, le=90)\n    is_public: bool = True\n\nclass TreePatch(BaseModel):\n    version: int                            # version the client last saw (optimistic lock)\n    species: Optional[str] = Field(None, min_length=1, max_length=80)\n    height_m: Optional[float] = Field(None, ge=0, le=150)\n    condition: Optional[Literal[\"good\", \"fair\", \"poor\"]] = None\n    lon: Optional[float] = Field(None, ge=-180, le=180)\n    lat: Optional[float] = Field(None, ge=-90, le=90)\n\n@app.get(\"/trees\")\ndef list_trees(c: Claims = Depends(claims)):\n    with session(c) as conn:\n        rows = conn.execute(\"\"\"SELECT id, version, species, height_m, condition, is_public,\n                                      ST_AsGeoJSON(geom)::json AS geometry\n                               FROM city.trees ORDER BY id\"\"\").fetchall()\n    return {\"type\": \"FeatureCollection\", \"features\": [\n        {\"type\": \"Feature\", \"id\": r[\"id\"], \"geometry\": r[\"geometry\"],\n         \"properties\": {k: (float(v) if k == \"height_m\" and v is not None else v)\n                        for k, v in r.items() if k not in (\"id\", \"geometry\")}} for r in rows]}\n\n@app.post(\"/trees\", status_code=201)\ndef create_tree(t: TreeIn, c: Claims = Depends(claims)):\n    with session(c) as conn:                # owner_org comes from the token, never from the request body\n        return conn.execute(\"\"\"INSERT INTO city.trees (species, height_m, condition, owner_org, is_public, geom)\n                               VALUES (%s, %s, %s, %s, %s, ST_SetSRID(ST_MakePoint(%s, %s), 4326))\n                               RETURNING id, version\"\"\",\n                            (t.species, t.height_m, t.condition, c.org, t.is_public, t.lon, t.lat)).fetchone()\n\n@app.patch(\"/trees/{tree_id}\")\ndef update_tree(tree_id: int, p: TreePatch, c: Claims = Depends(claims)):\n    params = {**p.model_dump(), \"id\": tree_id}\n    with session(c) as conn:\n        row = conn.execute(\"\"\"UPDATE city.trees SET\n                 species   = COALESCE(%(species)s,   species),\n                 height_m  = COALESCE(%(height_m)s,  height_m),\n                 condition = COALESCE(%(condition)s, condition),\n                 geom      = CASE WHEN %(lon)s::float8 IS NULL OR %(lat)s::float8 IS NULL THEN geom\n                                  ELSE ST_SetSRID(ST_MakePoint(%(lon)s, %(lat)s), 4326) END,\n                 version   = version + 1, updated_at = now()\n               WHERE id = %(id)s AND version = %(version)s\n               RETURNING id, version\"\"\", params).fetchone()\n        if row:\n            return row\n        exists = conn.execute(\"SELECT 1 FROM city.trees WHERE id = %s\", (tree_id,)).fetchone()\n    # Rows hidden by RLS look identical to missing rows: do not leak their existence.\n    raise HTTPException(409 if exists else 404, \"version conflict\" if exists else \"not found\")\n\n@app.delete(\"/trees/{tree_id}\", status_code=204)\ndef delete_tree(tree_id: int, c: Claims = Depends(claims)):\n    with session(c) as conn:\n        if conn.execute(\"DELETE FROM city.trees WHERE id = %s RETURNING id\", (tree_id,)).fetchone() is None:\n            raise HTTPException(404, \"not found\")\n```\n\nRun it and mint development tokens:\n\n```bash\npip install fastapi uvicorn \"psycopg[binary]\" pyjwt\nEDIT_DSN=\"postgresql://edit_api:edit_pw@localhost:5432/gis\" JWT_SECRET=\"dev-only-change-me\" \\\n  uvicorn main:app --port 8000\n\npython3 -c \"import jwt,time; print(jwt.encode({'sub':'ana','org':'parks','exp':int(time.time())+3600}, 'dev-only-change-me', algorithm='HS256'))\"\n```\n\nVerify the write path and security (replace `$T_PARKS` / `$T_HARBOR` by tokens for `org=parks` / `org=harbor`):\n\n```bash\ncurl -s -H \"Authorization: Bearer $T_PARKS\"  localhost:8000/trees | python3 -c \"import sys,json; print([f['properties']['species'] for f in json.load(sys.stdin)['features']])\"\n# expected: ['Oak', 'Linden', 'Maple']      (includes the non-public Maple)\ncurl -s -H \"Authorization: Bearer $T_HARBOR\" localhost:8000/trees | python3 -c \"import sys,json; print([f['properties']['species'] for f in json.load(sys.stdin)['features']])\"\n# expected: ['Plane', 'Birch']\n\n# harbor tries to change a parks tree (id 1): RLS hides it → 404, nothing changes\ncurl -s -o /dev/null -w \"%{http_code}\\n\" -X PATCH -H \"Authorization: Bearer $T_HARBOR\" \\\n  -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" -d '{\"version\":1,\"condition\":\"poor\"}' localhost:8000/trees/1      # expected 404\n\n# parks edits tree 1 twice with the same version: first succeeds, second conflicts\ncurl -s -X PATCH -H \"Authorization: Bearer $T_PARKS\" -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\\n  -d '{\"version\":1,\"condition\":\"fair\"}' localhost:8000/trees/1      # expected {\"id\":1,\"version\":2}\ncurl -s -o /dev/null -w \"%{http_code}\\n\" -X PATCH -H \"Authorization: Bearer $T_PARKS\" \\\n  -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" -d '{\"version\":1,\"condition\":\"poor\"}' localhost:8000/trees/1      # expected 409\n```\n\n","lineStart":866,"lineEnd":997,"html":"<h2>5.6 Step 5 — The edit API (<code>edit_api/main.py</code>)</h2>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">import os\nfrom contextlib import contextmanager\nfrom typing import Literal, Optional\n\nimport jwt                                  # PyJWT\nimport psycopg\nfrom fastapi import Depends, FastAPI, Header, HTTPException\nfrom fastapi.middleware.cors import CORSMiddleware\nfrom psycopg.rows import dict_row\nfrom pydantic import BaseModel, Field\n\nDSN = os.environ[&quot;EDIT_DSN&quot;]               # connects as role edit_api (no BYPASSRLS)\nJWT_SECRET = os.environ[&quot;JWT_SECRET&quot;]      # dev only: in production use asymmetric keys (OIDC/JWKS)\n\napp = FastAPI(title=&quot;Tree edit API&quot;)\napp.add_middleware(CORSMiddleware, allow_origins=[&quot;http://localhost:8080&quot;],\n                   allow_methods=[&quot;GET&quot;, &quot;POST&quot;, &quot;PATCH&quot;, &quot;DELETE&quot;], allow_headers=[&quot;Authorization&quot;, &quot;Content-Type&quot;])\n\nclass Claims(BaseModel):\n    sub: str\n    org: str\n\ndef claims(authorization: str = Header(...)) -&gt; Claims:\n    scheme, _, token = authorization.partition(&quot; &quot;)\n    try:\n        if scheme.lower() != &quot;bearer&quot;:\n            raise ValueError(&quot;scheme&quot;)\n        data = jwt.decode(token, JWT_SECRET, algorithms=[&quot;HS256&quot;])      # checks signature and exp\n        return Claims(sub=data[&quot;sub&quot;], org=data[&quot;org&quot;])\n    except Exception:\n        raise HTTPException(status_code=401, detail=&quot;invalid or missing token&quot;)\n\n@contextmanager\ndef session(c: Claims):\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;One transaction per request; identity is visible to RLS policies and the audit trigger.&quot;&quot;&quot;\n    with psycopg.connect(DSN, row_factory=dict_row) as conn:\n        conn.execute(&quot;SELECT set_config(&#39;app.org&#39;,  %s, true), set_config(&#39;app.user&#39;, %s, true)&quot;, (c.org, c.sub))\n        yield conn                          # commit on success, rollback on exception\n\nclass TreeIn(BaseModel):\n    species: str = Field(min_length=1, max_length=80)\n    height_m: Optional[float] = Field(None, ge=0, le=150)\n    condition: Literal[&quot;good&quot;, &quot;fair&quot;, &quot;poor&quot;]\n    lon: float = Field(ge=-180, le=180)     # note the order: longitude first\n    lat: float = Field(ge=-90, le=90)\n    is_public: bool = True\n\nclass TreePatch(BaseModel):\n    version: int                            # version the client last saw (optimistic lock)\n    species: Optional[str] = Field(None, min_length=1, max_length=80)\n    height_m: Optional[float] = Field(None, ge=0, le=150)\n    condition: Optional[Literal[&quot;good&quot;, &quot;fair&quot;, &quot;poor&quot;]] = None\n    lon: Optional[float] = Field(None, ge=-180, le=180)\n    lat: Optional[float] = Field(None, ge=-90, le=90)\n\n@app.get(&quot;/trees&quot;)\ndef list_trees(c: Claims = Depends(claims)):\n    with session(c) as conn:\n        rows = conn.execute(&quot;&quot;&quot;SELECT id, version, species, height_m, condition, is_public,\n                                      ST_AsGeoJSON(geom)::json AS geometry\n                               FROM city.trees ORDER BY id&quot;&quot;&quot;).fetchall()\n    return {&quot;type&quot;: &quot;FeatureCollection&quot;, &quot;features&quot;: [\n        {&quot;type&quot;: &quot;Feature&quot;, &quot;id&quot;: r[&quot;id&quot;], &quot;geometry&quot;: r[&quot;geometry&quot;],\n         &quot;properties&quot;: {k: (float(v) if k == &quot;height_m&quot; and v is not None else v)\n                        for k, v in r.items() if k not in (&quot;id&quot;, &quot;geometry&quot;)}} for r in rows]}\n\n@app.post(&quot;/trees&quot;, status_code=201)\ndef create_tree(t: TreeIn, c: Claims = Depends(claims)):\n    with session(c) as conn:                # owner_org comes from the token, never from the request body\n        return conn.execute(&quot;&quot;&quot;INSERT INTO city.trees (species, height_m, condition, owner_org, is_public, geom)\n                               VALUES (%s, %s, %s, %s, %s, ST_SetSRID(ST_MakePoint(%s, %s), 4326))\n                               RETURNING id, version&quot;&quot;&quot;,\n                            (t.species, t.height_m, t.condition, c.org, t.is_public, t.lon, t.lat)).fetchone()\n\n@app.patch(&quot;/trees/{tree_id}&quot;)\ndef update_tree(tree_id: int, p: TreePatch, c: Claims = Depends(claims)):\n    params = {**p.model_dump(), &quot;id&quot;: tree_id}\n    with session(c) as conn:\n        row = conn.execute(&quot;&quot;&quot;UPDATE city.trees SET\n                 species   = COALESCE(%(species)s,   species),\n                 height_m  = COALESCE(%(height_m)s,  height_m),\n                 condition = COALESCE(%(condition)s, condition),\n                 geom      = CASE WHEN %(lon)s::float8 IS NULL OR %(lat)s::float8 IS NULL THEN geom\n                                  ELSE ST_SetSRID(ST_MakePoint(%(lon)s, %(lat)s), 4326) END,\n                 version   = version + 1, updated_at = now()\n               WHERE id = %(id)s AND version = %(version)s\n               RETURNING id, version&quot;&quot;&quot;, params).fetchone()\n        if row:\n            return row\n        exists = conn.execute(&quot;SELECT 1 FROM city.trees WHERE id = %s&quot;, (tree_id,)).fetchone()\n    # Rows hidden by RLS look identical to missing rows: do not leak their existence.\n    raise HTTPException(409 if exists else 404, &quot;version conflict&quot; if exists else &quot;not found&quot;)\n\n@app.delete(&quot;/trees/{tree_id}&quot;, status_code=204)\ndef delete_tree(tree_id: int, c: Claims = Depends(claims)):\n    with session(c) as conn:\n        if conn.execute(&quot;DELETE FROM city.trees WHERE id = %s RETURNING id&quot;, (tree_id,)).fetchone() is None:\n            raise HTTPException(404, &quot;not found&quot;)</code></pre><p>Run it and mint development tokens:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-bash\">pip install fastapi uvicorn &quot;psycopg[binary]&quot; pyjwt\nEDIT_DSN=&quot;postgresql://edit_api:edit_pw@localhost:5432/gis&quot; JWT_SECRET=&quot;dev-only-change-me&quot; \\\n  uvicorn main:app --port 8000\n\npython3 -c &quot;import jwt,time; print(jwt.encode({&#39;sub&#39;:&#39;ana&#39;,&#39;org&#39;:&#39;parks&#39;,&#39;exp&#39;:int(time.time())+3600}, &#39;dev-only-change-me&#39;, algorithm=&#39;HS256&#39;))&quot;</code></pre><p>Verify the write path and security (replace <code>$T_PARKS</code> / <code>$T_HARBOR</code> by tokens for <code>org=parks</code> / <code>org=harbor</code>):</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-bash\">curl -s -H &quot;Authorization: Bearer $T_PARKS&quot;  localhost:8000/trees | python3 -c &quot;import sys,json; print([f[&#39;properties&#39;][&#39;species&#39;] for f in json.load(sys.stdin)[&#39;features&#39;]])&quot;\n# expected: [&#39;Oak&#39;, &#39;Linden&#39;, &#39;Maple&#39;]      (includes the non-public Maple)\ncurl -s -H &quot;Authorization: Bearer $T_HARBOR&quot; localhost:8000/trees | python3 -c &quot;import sys,json; print([f[&#39;properties&#39;][&#39;species&#39;] for f in json.load(sys.stdin)[&#39;features&#39;]])&quot;\n# expected: [&#39;Plane&#39;, &#39;Birch&#39;]\n\n# harbor tries to change a parks tree (id 1): RLS hides it → 404, nothing changes\ncurl -s -o /dev/null -w &quot;%{http_code}\\n&quot; -X PATCH -H &quot;Authorization: Bearer $T_HARBOR&quot; \\\n  -H &quot;Content-Type: application/json&quot; -d &#39;{&quot;version&quot;:1,&quot;condition&quot;:&quot;poor&quot;}&#39; localhost:8000/trees/1      # expected 404\n\n# parks edits tree 1 twice with the same version: first succeeds, second conflicts\ncurl -s -X PATCH -H &quot;Authorization: Bearer $T_PARKS&quot; -H &quot;Content-Type: application/json&quot; \\\n  -d &#39;{&quot;version&quot;:1,&quot;condition&quot;:&quot;fair&quot;}&#39; localhost:8000/trees/1      # expected {&quot;id&quot;:1,&quot;version&quot;:2}\ncurl -s -o /dev/null -w &quot;%{http_code}\\n&quot; -X PATCH -H &quot;Authorization: Bearer $T_PARKS&quot; \\\n  -H &quot;Content-Type: application/json&quot; -d &#39;{&quot;version&quot;:1,&quot;condition&quot;:&quot;poor&quot;}&#39; localhost:8000/trees/1      # expected 409</code></pre>","sha256":"a31675a36b724324f0f5cb4790a4ddc27c1254c56de54b78511cf561a884e94b"},{"key":"5.7","id":"5-7","title":"5.7 Step 6 — Web client (`index.html`, serve with `python3 -m http.server 8080`)","anchor":"57-step-6--web-client-indexhtml-serve-with-python3--m-httpserver-8080","depth":3,"start":52747,"topics":["editing"],"end":56326,"markdown":"### 5.7 Step 6 — Web client (`index.html`, serve with `python3 -m http.server 8080`)\n\n```html\n<!doctype html><meta charset=\"utf-8\" /><title>Tree inventory</title>\n<link href=\"https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@5/dist/maplibre-gl.css\" rel=\"stylesheet\" />\n<script src=\"https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@5/dist/maplibre-gl.js\"></script>\n<style>html,body,#map{height:100%;margin:0} #bar{position:absolute;z-index:2;margin:8px;padding:6px;background:#fff}</style>\n<div id=\"bar\"><label><input id=\"edit\" type=\"checkbox\" /> add trees on click</label></div>\n<div id=\"map\"></div>\n<script>\nconst TILES = 'http://localhost:7800/postgisftw.trees_mvt/{z}/{x}/{y}.pbf';\nconst API   = 'http://localhost:8000';\nconst TOKEN = prompt('Paste a development JWT');                    // lab only\nconst auth  = { Authorization: `Bearer ${TOKEN}`, 'Content-Type': 'application/json' };\n\nconst map = new maplibregl.Map({ container: 'map', style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json',\n                                 center: [4.9, 52.37], zoom: 13 });\n\nasync function loadMine() {                                         // authenticated, org-scoped features\n  const r = await fetch(`${API}/trees`, { headers: auth });\n  return r.ok ? r.json() : { type: 'FeatureCollection', features: [] };\n}\nfunction refreshPublicTiles() {                                     // cache-bust the public vector tiles\n  map.getSource('trees').setTiles([`${TILES}?v=${Date.now()}`]);\n}\n\nmap.on('load', async () => {\n  map.addSource('trees', { type: 'vector', tiles: [TILES], minzoom: 8, maxzoom: 16 });\n  map.addLayer({ id: 'public', type: 'circle', source: 'trees', 'source-layer': 'trees',\n    paint: { 'circle-radius': 5, 'circle-color': ['match', ['get', 'condition'],\n             'good', '#2e7d32', 'fair', '#f9a825', 'poor', '#c62828', '#757575'] } });\n\n  map.addSource('mine', { type: 'geojson', data: await loadMine() });\n  map.addLayer({ id: 'mine', type: 'circle', source: 'mine',        // editor layer: ring around my trees\n    paint: { 'circle-radius': 9, 'circle-color': 'rgba(0,0,0,0)', 'circle-stroke-width': 2, 'circle-stroke-color': '#1565c0' } });\n\n  map.on('click', async (e) => {\n    const hit = map.queryRenderedFeatures(e.point, { layers: ['mine'] })[0];\n    if (hit) {                                                      // EDIT an existing tree\n      const condition = prompt('New condition (good/fair/poor)?', hit.properties.condition);\n      if (!condition) return;\n      const r = await fetch(`${API}/trees/${hit.id}`, { method: 'PATCH', headers: auth,\n        body: JSON.stringify({ version: hit.properties.version, condition }) });\n      if (r.status === 409) alert('Someone else changed this tree. Reloading.');\n    } else if (document.getElementById('edit').checked) {           // CREATE a new tree\n      const species = prompt('Species?'); if (!species) return;\n      await fetch(`${API}/trees`, { method: 'POST', headers: auth,\n        body: JSON.stringify({ species, condition: 'good', lon: e.lngLat.lng, lat: e.lngLat.lat }) });\n    } else return;\n    map.getSource('mine').setData(await loadMine());\n    refreshPublicTiles();\n  });\n});\n</script>\n```\n\n**What this demonstrates:** public, cacheable, anonymous-safe data is drawn from **vector tiles**; the authenticated, editable, possibly non-public data comes from the **API as GeoJSON** and is drawn as a separate layer. After each edit the client refreshes its own layer and cache-busts the tile source (`setTiles` is available on vector sources in current MapLibre releases; if your version lacks it, remove and re-add the source).\n\n","lineStart":998,"lineEnd":1055,"html":"<h2>5.7 Step 6 — Web client (<code>index.html</code>, serve with <code>python3 -m http.server 8080</code>)</h2>\n<pre><code class=\"language-html\">&lt;!doctype html&gt;&lt;meta charset=&quot;utf-8&quot; /&gt;&lt;title&gt;Tree inventory&lt;/title&gt;\n&lt;link href=&quot;https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@5/dist/maplibre-gl.css&quot; rel=&quot;stylesheet&quot; /&gt;\n&lt;script src=&quot;https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@5/dist/maplibre-gl.js&quot;&gt;&lt;/script&gt;\n&lt;style&gt;html,body,#map{height:100%;margin:0} #bar{position:absolute;z-index:2;margin:8px;padding:6px;background:#fff}&lt;/style&gt;\n&lt;div id=&quot;bar&quot;&gt;&lt;label&gt;&lt;input id=&quot;edit&quot; type=&quot;checkbox&quot; /&gt; add trees on click&lt;/label&gt;&lt;/div&gt;\n&lt;div id=&quot;map&quot;&gt;&lt;/div&gt;\n&lt;script&gt;\nconst TILES = &#39;http://localhost:7800/postgisftw.trees_mvt/{z}/{x}/{y}.pbf&#39;;\nconst API   = &#39;http://localhost:8000&#39;;\nconst TOKEN = prompt(&#39;Paste a development JWT&#39;);                    // lab only\nconst auth  = { Authorization: `Bearer ${TOKEN}`, &#39;Content-Type&#39;: &#39;application/json&#39; };\n\nconst map = new maplibregl.Map({ container: &#39;map&#39;, style: &#39;https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json&#39;,\n                                 center: [4.9, 52.37], zoom: 13 });\n\nasync function loadMine() {                                         // authenticated, org-scoped features\n  const r = await fetch(`${API}/trees`, { headers: auth });\n  return r.ok ? r.json() : { type: &#39;FeatureCollection&#39;, features: [] };\n}\nfunction refreshPublicTiles() {                                     // cache-bust the public vector tiles\n  map.getSource(&#39;trees&#39;).setTiles([`${TILES}?v=${Date.now()}`]);\n}\n\nmap.on(&#39;load&#39;, async () =&gt; {\n  map.addSource(&#39;trees&#39;, { type: &#39;vector&#39;, tiles: [TILES], minzoom: 8, maxzoom: 16 });\n  map.addLayer({ id: &#39;public&#39;, type: &#39;circle&#39;, source: &#39;trees&#39;, &#39;source-layer&#39;: &#39;trees&#39;,\n    paint: { &#39;circle-radius&#39;: 5, &#39;circle-color&#39;: [&#39;match&#39;, [&#39;get&#39;, &#39;condition&#39;],\n             &#39;good&#39;, &#39;#2e7d32&#39;, &#39;fair&#39;, &#39;#f9a825&#39;, &#39;poor&#39;, &#39;#c62828&#39;, &#39;#757575&#39;] } });\n\n  map.addSource(&#39;mine&#39;, { type: &#39;geojson&#39;, data: await loadMine() });\n  map.addLayer({ id: &#39;mine&#39;, type: &#39;circle&#39;, source: &#39;mine&#39;,        // editor layer: ring around my trees\n    paint: { &#39;circle-radius&#39;: 9, &#39;circle-color&#39;: &#39;rgba(0,0,0,0)&#39;, &#39;circle-stroke-width&#39;: 2, &#39;circle-stroke-color&#39;: &#39;#1565c0&#39; } });\n\n  map.on(&#39;click&#39;, async (e) =&gt; {\n    const hit = map.queryRenderedFeatures(e.point, { layers: [&#39;mine&#39;] })[0];\n    if (hit) {                                                      // EDIT an existing tree\n      const condition = prompt(&#39;New condition (good/fair/poor)?&#39;, hit.properties.condition);\n      if (!condition) return;\n      const r = await fetch(`${API}/trees/${hit.id}`, { method: &#39;PATCH&#39;, headers: auth,\n        body: JSON.stringify({ version: hit.properties.version, condition }) });\n      if (r.status === 409) alert(&#39;Someone else changed this tree. Reloading.&#39;);\n    } else if (document.getElementById(&#39;edit&#39;).checked) {           // CREATE a new tree\n      const species = prompt(&#39;Species?&#39;); if (!species) return;\n      await fetch(`${API}/trees`, { method: &#39;POST&#39;, headers: auth,\n        body: JSON.stringify({ species, condition: &#39;good&#39;, lon: e.lngLat.lng, lat: e.lngLat.lat }) });\n    } else return;\n    map.getSource(&#39;mine&#39;).setData(await loadMine());\n    refreshPublicTiles();\n  });\n});\n&lt;/script&gt;</code></pre><p><strong>What this demonstrates:</strong> public, cacheable, anonymous-safe data is drawn from <strong>vector tiles</strong>; the authenticated, editable, possibly non-public data comes from the <strong>API as GeoJSON</strong> and is drawn as a separate layer. After each edit the client refreshes its own layer and cache-busts the tile source (<code>setTiles</code> is available on vector sources in current MapLibre releases; if your version lacks it, remove and re-add the source).</p>\n","sha256":"b2f8ccd8dbebc9c7e95745f3a411e615d9d7a1af9131246cfd08f660ddc2230e"},{"key":"5.8","id":"5-8","title":"5.8 Verification checklist for the worked example","anchor":"58-verification-checklist-for-the-worked-example","depth":3,"start":56326,"topics":["inventory"],"end":56883,"markdown":"### 5.8 Verification checklist for the worked example\n\n| Check | Expected |\n|---|---|\n| Tile `12/2103/1346` for `tile_reader` | 3 features: Linden, Oak, Plane |\n| Tile `12/2104/1345` | 1 feature: Birch |\n| pg_featureserv `items` | 4 features (no Maple) |\n| `GET /trees` as parks / harbor | 3 / 2 features |\n| harbor `PATCH` on a parks tree | 404, row unchanged |\n| Two `PATCH`es with the same `version` | first 200 (`version` → 2), second 409 |\n| `SELECT action FROM city.edit_log` after the edits | contains an `UPDATE` row with `user_sub = 'ana'` |\n\n---\n\n","lineStart":1056,"lineEnd":1069,"html":"<h2>5.8 Verification checklist for the worked example</h2>\n<div class=\"source-table\" role=\"region\" tabindex=\"0\" aria-label=\"Table in 5.8 Verification checklist for the worked example\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Check</th>\n<th>Expected</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Tile <code>12/2103/1346</code> for <code>tile_reader</code></td>\n<td>3 features: Linden, Oak, Plane</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Tile <code>12/2104/1345</code></td>\n<td>1 feature: Birch</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>pg_featureserv <code>items</code></td>\n<td>4 features (no Maple)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>GET /trees</code> as parks / harbor</td>\n<td>3 / 2 features</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>harbor <code>PATCH</code> on a parks tree</td>\n<td>404, row unchanged</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Two <code>PATCH</code>es with the same <code>version</code></td>\n<td>first 200 (<code>version</code> → 2), second 409</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>SELECT action FROM city.edit_log</code> after the edits</td>\n<td>contains an <code>UPDATE</code> row with <code>user_sub = &#39;ana&#39;</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div><hr>\n","sha256":"a952bafa8d9da7ad983dff5866aa5028c1119ebf31744faf5c4adbc0edf5664e"},{"key":"6","id":"6","title":"6. Common pitfalls and best practices","anchor":"6-common-pitfalls-and-best-practices","depth":2,"start":56883,"topics":["security"],"end":56925,"markdown":"## 6. Common pitfalls and best practices\n\n","lineStart":1070,"lineEnd":1071,"html":"<h2>6. Common pitfalls and best practices</h2>\n","sha256":"d50c3fc6309a6ce26a5448b38053ae90fe2e0572c65049f1cba76c1059e832fd"},{"key":"6.1","id":"6-1","title":"6.1 Coordinate systems and geometry","anchor":"61-coordinate-systems-and-geometry","depth":3,"start":56925,"topics":["tile-math"],"end":58210,"markdown":"### 6.1 Coordinate systems and geometry\n\n| # | Pitfall | Symptom | Prevention |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | **Axis-order confusion.** GeoJSON and CRS:84 are `lon,lat`; WMS 1.3.0 with `EPSG:4326` and GML URNs are `lat,lon`; Leaflet's `LatLng` is `[lat, lon]` | Data appears in the ocean or mirrored across the diagonal | Use `CRS:84` for WMS 1.3.0 `bbox`; always name the order in variable names (`lon`, `lat`); test with a known point |\n| 2 | **Measuring in EPSG:3857.** Scale grows as `1/cos φ` | At 52.37° N lengths are inflated ≈ 1.64×, areas ≈ 2.7× | Compute lengths/areas with `geography` or a local projected CRS; use 3857 only for display |\n| 3 | **Latitudes beyond ±85.0511°** | Tile math returns `NaN`/out-of-range rows | Clamp latitude (as in the Exercise 1 solution) |\n| 4 | **Antimeridian** (±180°) | Polygons stretch across the whole map | Split geometries at ±180° or shift longitudes; prefer data prepared for web use |\n| 5 | **XYZ vs TMS and 256 vs 512 px** | Mirrored map, or everything one zoom off | `y_tms = 2^z − 1 − y`; set `tileSize` explicitly; remember MapLibre zoom = 256-px zoom − 1 |\n| 6 | **Invalid geometries** | `ST_AsMVTGeom`/`ST_Simplify` errors or missing polygons | Constrain with `CHECK (ST_IsValid(geom))` or repair once with `ST_MakeValid` on load |\n\n","lineStart":1072,"lineEnd":1082,"html":"<h2>6.1 Coordinate systems and geometry</h2>\n<div class=\"source-table\" role=\"region\" tabindex=\"0\" aria-label=\"Table in 6.1 Coordinate systems and geometry\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>#</th>\n<th>Pitfall</th>\n<th>Symptom</th>\n<th>Prevention</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td><strong>Axis-order confusion.</strong> GeoJSON and CRS:84 are <code>lon,lat</code>; WMS 1.3.0 with <code>EPSG:4326</code> and GML URNs are <code>lat,lon</code>; Leaflet&#39;s <code>LatLng</code> is <code>[lat, lon]</code></td>\n<td>Data appears in the ocean or mirrored across the diagonal</td>\n<td>Use <code>CRS:84</code> for WMS 1.3.0 <code>bbox</code>; always name the order in variable names (<code>lon</code>, <code>lat</code>); test with a known point</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td><strong>Measuring in EPSG:3857.</strong> Scale grows as <code>1/cos φ</code></td>\n<td>At 52.37° N lengths are inflated ≈ 1.64×, areas ≈ 2.7×</td>\n<td>Compute lengths/areas with <code>geography</code> or a local projected CRS; use 3857 only for display</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td><strong>Latitudes beyond ±85.0511°</strong></td>\n<td>Tile math returns <code>NaN</code>/out-of-range rows</td>\n<td>Clamp latitude (as in the Exercise 1 solution)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td><strong>Antimeridian</strong> (±180°)</td>\n<td>Polygons stretch across the whole map</td>\n<td>Split geometries at ±180° or shift longitudes; prefer data prepared for web use</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td><strong>XYZ vs TMS and 256 vs 512 px</strong></td>\n<td>Mirrored map, or everything one zoom off</td>\n<td><code>y_tms = 2^z − 1 − y</code>; set <code>tileSize</code> explicitly; remember MapLibre zoom = 256-px zoom − 1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td><strong>Invalid geometries</strong></td>\n<td><code>ST_AsMVTGeom</code>/<code>ST_Simplify</code> errors or missing polygons</td>\n<td>Constrain with <code>CHECK (ST_IsValid(geom))</code> or repair once with <code>ST_MakeValid</code> on load</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>","sha256":"ac6f67406052af8ba3cb3dbb157f54b6b13a8ff8c7dac2de1a5761775e92f701"},{"key":"6.2","id":"6-2","title":"6.2 Tiles and rendering","anchor":"62-tiles-and-rendering","depth":3,"start":58210,"topics":["mvt"],"end":59424,"markdown":"### 6.2 Tiles and rendering\n\n| # | Pitfall | Symptom | Prevention |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 7 | Wrong `source-layer` name | Blank map, no errors | The name equals the layer name in `ST_AsMVT(..., 'name')`; inspect a decoded tile |\n| 8 | Oversized tiles | Slow panning, dropped features | Budget bytes and feature counts per tile; simplify; aggregate; use Tippecanoe `--drop-densest-as-needed` |\n| 9 | MVT quantisation treated as data | Coordinates off by up to ~0.6 m at z14 | Tiles are for **display**; fetch authoritative geometry from the feature API |\n| 10 | Over-zoom mismatch | Blocky geometry or missing data at high zoom | Set source `maxzoom` to the highest generated zoom; client over-zooms beyond |\n| 11 | Tile seams / clipped outlines | Hairlines at tile borders | Use a buffer (e.g. 64 units) and `clip_geom = true` |\n| 12 | Cluster counts split by tile edges | A grid cell shows two partial counts | Aggregate on a global grid and accept it, or aggregate with a buffer/union client-side; for exact counts use server-side queries |\n| 13 | Basemap licensing | Blocked tile requests or legal exposure | Read the provider's policy; public OSM tile servers are not for production traffic; always attribute |\n\n","lineStart":1083,"lineEnd":1094,"html":"<h2>6.2 Tiles and rendering</h2>\n<div class=\"source-table\" role=\"region\" tabindex=\"0\" aria-label=\"Table in 6.2 Tiles and rendering\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>#</th>\n<th>Pitfall</th>\n<th>Symptom</th>\n<th>Prevention</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>Wrong <code>source-layer</code> name</td>\n<td>Blank map, no errors</td>\n<td>The name equals the layer name in <code>ST_AsMVT(..., &#39;name&#39;)</code>; inspect a decoded tile</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>8</td>\n<td>Oversized tiles</td>\n<td>Slow panning, dropped features</td>\n<td>Budget bytes and feature counts per tile; simplify; aggregate; use Tippecanoe <code>--drop-densest-as-needed</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>9</td>\n<td>MVT quantisation treated as data</td>\n<td>Coordinates off by up to ~0.6 m at z14</td>\n<td>Tiles are for <strong>display</strong>; fetch authoritative geometry from the feature API</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>Over-zoom mismatch</td>\n<td>Blocky geometry or missing data at high zoom</td>\n<td>Set source <code>maxzoom</code> to the highest generated zoom; client over-zooms beyond</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>11</td>\n<td>Tile seams / clipped outlines</td>\n<td>Hairlines at tile borders</td>\n<td>Use a buffer (e.g. 64 units) and <code>clip_geom = true</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>12</td>\n<td>Cluster counts split by tile edges</td>\n<td>A grid cell shows two partial counts</td>\n<td>Aggregate on a global grid and accept it, or aggregate with a buffer/union client-side; for exact counts use server-side queries</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>13</td>\n<td>Basemap licensing</td>\n<td>Blocked tile requests or legal exposure</td>\n<td>Read the provider&#39;s policy; public OSM tile servers are not for production traffic; always attribute</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>","sha256":"b1e22cfa32c3e65ae4bce66a6286f398f5e9b0a76de19607b5e8c88615f2f01a"},{"key":"6.3","id":"6-3","title":"6.3 Database and API performance","anchor":"63-database-and-api-performance","depth":3,"start":59424,"topics":["performance"],"end":60501,"markdown":"### 6.3 Database and API performance\n\n| # | Pitfall | Symptom | Prevention |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 14 | `ST_Transform(col, 3857) && env` in `WHERE` | Seq scan on every tile | Filter in the column's SRID (`col && ST_Transform(env, 4326)`) or index a 3857 copy |\n| 15 | Missing GiST index or stale statistics | Slow tiles after bulk load | `CREATE INDEX … USING gist`, then `ANALYZE`; inspect with `EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)` |\n| 16 | One viewport = many parallel tile requests | Connection-pool exhaustion | Size pools for bursts (a viewport commonly needs several to a dozen tiles); cache; put a CDN in front |\n| 17 | Unbounded feature requests | Browser freezes, server OOM | Enforce server-side page limits; page with `next` links; use keyset pagination for large offsets |\n| 18 | SQL built by string concatenation in tile/feature functions | SQL injection through query parameters | Use typed function parameters and bind variables only |\n| 19 | Large GeoJSON loaded wholesale | Multi-second load, high memory | Switch to vector tiles beyond tens of thousands of features |\n\n","lineStart":1095,"lineEnd":1105,"html":"<h2>6.3 Database and API performance</h2>\n<div class=\"source-table\" role=\"region\" tabindex=\"0\" aria-label=\"Table in 6.3 Database and API performance\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>#</th>\n<th>Pitfall</th>\n<th>Symptom</th>\n<th>Prevention</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>14</td>\n<td><code>ST_Transform(col, 3857) &amp;&amp; env</code> in <code>WHERE</code></td>\n<td>Seq scan on every tile</td>\n<td>Filter in the column&#39;s SRID (<code>col &amp;&amp; ST_Transform(env, 4326)</code>) or index a 3857 copy</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>15</td>\n<td>Missing GiST index or stale statistics</td>\n<td>Slow tiles after bulk load</td>\n<td><code>CREATE INDEX … USING gist</code>, then <code>ANALYZE</code>; inspect with <code>EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>16</td>\n<td>One viewport = many parallel tile requests</td>\n<td>Connection-pool exhaustion</td>\n<td>Size pools for bursts (a viewport commonly needs several to a dozen tiles); cache; put a CDN in front</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>17</td>\n<td>Unbounded feature requests</td>\n<td>Browser freezes, server OOM</td>\n<td>Enforce server-side page limits; page with <code>next</code> links; use keyset pagination for large offsets</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>18</td>\n<td>SQL built by string concatenation in tile/feature functions</td>\n<td>SQL injection through query parameters</td>\n<td>Use typed function parameters and bind variables only</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>19</td>\n<td>Large GeoJSON loaded wholesale</td>\n<td>Multi-second load, high memory</td>\n<td>Switch to vector tiles beyond tens of thousands of features</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>","sha256":"eb6ec72934d073fafb110919e90555c588b71cc23e25cf8527e4e32224a2f5dc"},{"key":"6.4","id":"6-4","title":"6.4 Security","anchor":"64-security","depth":3,"start":60501,"topics":["security"],"end":61574,"markdown":"### 6.4 Security\n\n| # | Pitfall | Symptom | Prevention |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 20 | **Cache leaks** | User A receives user B's tile | `Cache-Control: private`, `Vary: Authorization`, or a tier in the cache key; keep shared tiles public-only |\n| 21 | RLS silently bypassed | Rows visible to everyone | Never use superuser/`BYPASSRLS` roles; use `FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY` for owner-run code; for views use `WITH (security_invoker = true)` (PostgreSQL 15+) so the caller's rights apply |\n| 22 | Trusting client-supplied ownership | Users create/modify other orgs' rows | Derive `owner_org` from the verified token; enforce again in `WITH CHECK` |\n| 23 | Tokens in URLs | Credentials in logs and referrers | Send an `Authorization` header (`transformRequest`) or use same-site cookies through the gateway |\n| 24 | Over-permissive CORS | Any site can call your API with the user's credentials | Allow-list origins; never combine `*` with credentialed requests |\n| 25 | Existence leaks | `403` vs `404` reveals hidden rows | Return the same response for \"hidden\" and \"missing\" |\n\n","lineStart":1106,"lineEnd":1116,"html":"<h2>6.4 Security</h2>\n<div class=\"source-table\" role=\"region\" tabindex=\"0\" aria-label=\"Table in 6.4 Security\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>#</th>\n<th>Pitfall</th>\n<th>Symptom</th>\n<th>Prevention</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>20</td>\n<td><strong>Cache leaks</strong></td>\n<td>User A receives user B&#39;s tile</td>\n<td><code>Cache-Control: private</code>, <code>Vary: Authorization</code>, or a tier in the cache key; keep shared tiles public-only</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>21</td>\n<td>RLS silently bypassed</td>\n<td>Rows visible to everyone</td>\n<td>Never use superuser/<code>BYPASSRLS</code> roles; use <code>FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY</code> for owner-run code; for views use <code>WITH (security_invoker = true)</code> (PostgreSQL 15+) so the caller&#39;s rights apply</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>22</td>\n<td>Trusting client-supplied ownership</td>\n<td>Users create/modify other orgs&#39; rows</td>\n<td>Derive <code>owner_org</code> from the verified token; enforce again in <code>WITH CHECK</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>23</td>\n<td>Tokens in URLs</td>\n<td>Credentials in logs and referrers</td>\n<td>Send an <code>Authorization</code> header (<code>transformRequest</code>) or use same-site cookies through the gateway</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>24</td>\n<td>Over-permissive CORS</td>\n<td>Any site can call your API with the user&#39;s credentials</td>\n<td>Allow-list origins; never combine <code>*</code> with credentialed requests</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>25</td>\n<td>Existence leaks</td>\n<td><code>403</code> vs <code>404</code> reveals hidden rows</td>\n<td>Return the same response for &quot;hidden&quot; and &quot;missing&quot;</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>","sha256":"a08c2055003101d7b6d8abf489b1e8cd12f61813c1254a9a162bb279bac456c2"},{"key":"6.5","id":"6-5","title":"6.5 Editing","anchor":"65-editing","depth":3,"start":61574,"topics":["editing"],"end":62270,"markdown":"### 6.5 Editing\n\n| # | Pitfall | Symptom | Prevention |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 26 | Last-write-wins | Silent overwrite of a colleague's edit | Version column (or `ETag`/`If-Match`), `409 Conflict` |\n| 27 | Forgetting to convert projection | Features saved at the wrong place | Convert view CRS → storage CRS at the boundary (`dataProjection`/`featureProjection` in OpenLayers) |\n| 28 | Saving invalid or silly geometry | Self-intersections, empty geometries, out-of-range coordinates | Validate in the API and in the database (`CHECK`, bounds, types) |\n| 29 | Stale tiles after edits | Users do not see their change | Refresh/cache-bust the source, short TTLs, targeted invalidation (Exercise 3) |\n\n","lineStart":1117,"lineEnd":1125,"html":"<h2>6.5 Editing</h2>\n<div class=\"source-table\" role=\"region\" tabindex=\"0\" aria-label=\"Table in 6.5 Editing\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>#</th>\n<th>Pitfall</th>\n<th>Symptom</th>\n<th>Prevention</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>26</td>\n<td>Last-write-wins</td>\n<td>Silent overwrite of a colleague&#39;s edit</td>\n<td>Version column (or <code>ETag</code>/<code>If-Match</code>), <code>409 Conflict</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>27</td>\n<td>Forgetting to convert projection</td>\n<td>Features saved at the wrong place</td>\n<td>Convert view CRS → storage CRS at the boundary (<code>dataProjection</code>/<code>featureProjection</code> in OpenLayers)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>28</td>\n<td>Saving invalid or silly geometry</td>\n<td>Self-intersections, empty geometries, out-of-range coordinates</td>\n<td>Validate in the API and in the database (<code>CHECK</code>, bounds, types)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>29</td>\n<td>Stale tiles after edits</td>\n<td>Users do not see their change</td>\n<td>Refresh/cache-bust the source, short TTLs, targeted invalidation (Exercise 3)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>","sha256":"1b8acc18819918c8d6995d67d88b36fea24609a7c17e921d182629ce9a4fc4bf"},{"key":"6.6","id":"6-6","title":"6.6 Best-practice checklist","anchor":"66-best-practice-checklist","depth":3,"start":62270,"topics":["security"],"end":62955,"markdown":"### 6.6 Best-practice checklist\n\n1. Tiles for display, features for query and edit; keep one authoritative copy of the data (PostGIS).\n2. Store in EPSG:4326 (or a local CRS); display in EPSG:3857; convert at the edges.\n3. Set explicit budgets: bytes per tile, features per tile, latency per tile.\n4. Put every rule that protects data **in the database** (RLS, constraints) and make the API a second line of defence.\n5. Measure before optimising: `EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)`, browser network tab, and a repeatable benchmark.\n6. Pin library and image versions; read release notes before upgrading MapLibre, OpenLayers, or the servers.\n7. Treat every cache as a security boundary.\n\n---\n\n","lineStart":1126,"lineEnd":1137,"html":"<h2>6.6 Best-practice checklist</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Tiles for display, features for query and edit; keep one authoritative copy of the data (PostGIS).</li>\n<li>Store in EPSG:4326 (or a local CRS); display in EPSG:3857; convert at the edges.</li>\n<li>Set explicit budgets: bytes per tile, features per tile, latency per tile.</li>\n<li>Put every rule that protects data <strong>in the database</strong> (RLS, constraints) and make the API a second line of defence.</li>\n<li>Measure before optimising: <code>EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)</code>, browser network tab, and a repeatable benchmark.</li>\n<li>Pin library and image versions; read release notes before upgrading MapLibre, OpenLayers, or the servers.</li>\n<li>Treat every cache as a security boundary.</li>\n</ol>\n<hr>\n","sha256":"7ee0c2587ba7ae7783e0e75bfbfcdfe791b371f00d959882bc87a51049918c97"},{"key":"7","id":"7","title":"7. Progressive exercises","anchor":"7-progressive-exercises","depth":2,"start":62955,"topics":["tile-math"],"end":63253,"markdown":"## 7. Progressive exercises\n\nThe four exercises build on each other: **Exercise 1**'s tile-math module is reused in 2, 3 and 4; **Exercise 2**'s stack is the base of 3; **Exercise 3**'s data, tiles and tooling feed the capstone in 4. Solutions are in Section 8 — try each exercise before looking.\n\n","lineStart":1138,"lineEnd":1141,"html":"<h2>7. Progressive exercises</h2>\n<p>The four exercises build on each other: <strong>Exercise 1</strong>&#39;s tile-math module is reused in 2, 3 and 4; <strong>Exercise 2</strong>&#39;s stack is the base of 3; <strong>Exercise 3</strong>&#39;s data, tiles and tooling feed the capstone in 4. Solutions are in Section 8 — try each exercise before looking.</p>\n","sha256":"76fc9571b5d3d7d18a6028e677282dd37695cad18cb8eb84be48087a4bd7897e"},{"key":"exercise-1","id":"exercise-1","title":"Exercise 1 — Beginner: build a tile-math toolkit","anchor":"exercise-1--beginner-build-a-tile-math-toolkit","depth":3,"start":63253,"topics":["tile-math"],"end":64582,"markdown":"### Exercise 1 — Beginner: build a tile-math toolkit\n\n**Task.** Implement a small, dependency-free Python module with: `lonlat_to_tile(lon, lat, z)`, `tile_bounds_3857(z, x, y)`, `tile_bounds_4326(z, x, y)`, `resolution(z, lat)`, `tms_y(z, y)` and `tiles_for_bbox(west, south, east, north, z)`.\n\n**Prerequisites.** Section 4.1; basic Python.\n\n**Sample input.**\n\n| Call | Inputs |\n|---|---|\n| `lonlat_to_tile` | `(4.9, 52.37, 12)`, `(4.9, 52.37, 14)`, `(-74.0060, 40.7128, 10)`, `(139.6917, 35.6895, 16)`, `(0, 0, 1)`, `(180, 90, 3)` |\n| `tile_bounds_*` | tile `12/2103/1346` |\n| `tiles_for_bbox` | bbox `(4.85, 52.34, 4.95, 52.40)` for `z = 10 … 14` |\n| `tms_y` | `z = 12`, `y = 1346` |\n\n**Expected deliverable.** `tile_math.py` with `assert`-based self-tests, plus a short table of the results above.\n\n**Success criteria.**\n- All six `lonlat_to_tile` cases return in-range indices (including the clamped one).\n- The point `(4.9, 52.37)` lies inside the bounds you compute for its own tile at z = 12.\n- `resolution(0)` equals 156 543.034 m/px to at least 6 significant digits.\n- `tms_y` applied twice returns the original `y`.\n- You can predict, before running, that the bbox needs more tiles at each deeper zoom.\n\n**Hint.** `asinh(tan φ)` is numerically friendlier than `ln(tan φ + sec φ)`; clamp the results to `0 … 2^z − 1`.\n\n","lineStart":1142,"lineEnd":1167,"html":"<h2>Exercise 1 — Beginner: build a tile-math toolkit</h2>\n<p><strong>Task.</strong> Implement a small, dependency-free Python module with: <code>lonlat_to_tile(lon, lat, z)</code>, <code>tile_bounds_3857(z, x, y)</code>, <code>tile_bounds_4326(z, x, y)</code>, <code>resolution(z, lat)</code>, <code>tms_y(z, y)</code> and <code>tiles_for_bbox(west, south, east, north, z)</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites.</strong> Section 4.1; basic Python.</p>\n<p><strong>Sample input.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"source-table\" role=\"region\" tabindex=\"0\" aria-label=\"Table in Exercise 1 — Beginner: build a tile-math toolkit\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Call</th>\n<th>Inputs</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>lonlat_to_tile</code></td>\n<td><code>(4.9, 52.37, 12)</code>, <code>(4.9, 52.37, 14)</code>, <code>(-74.0060, 40.7128, 10)</code>, <code>(139.6917, 35.6895, 16)</code>, <code>(0, 0, 1)</code>, <code>(180, 90, 3)</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>tile_bounds_*</code></td>\n<td>tile <code>12/2103/1346</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>tiles_for_bbox</code></td>\n<td>bbox <code>(4.85, 52.34, 4.95, 52.40)</code> for <code>z = 10 … 14</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>tms_y</code></td>\n<td><code>z = 12</code>, <code>y = 1346</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div><p><strong>Expected deliverable.</strong> <code>tile_math.py</code> with <code>assert</code>-based self-tests, plus a short table of the results above.</p>\n<p><strong>Success criteria.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>All six <code>lonlat_to_tile</code> cases return in-range indices (including the clamped one).</li>\n<li>The point <code>(4.9, 52.37)</code> lies inside the bounds you compute for its own tile at z = 12.</li>\n<li><code>resolution(0)</code> equals 156 543.034 m/px to at least 6 significant digits.</li>\n<li><code>tms_y</code> applied twice returns the original <code>y</code>.</li>\n<li>You can predict, before running, that the bbox needs more tiles at each deeper zoom.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Hint.</strong> <code>asinh(tan φ)</code> is numerically friendlier than <code>ln(tan φ + sec φ)</code>; clamp the results to <code>0 … 2^z − 1</code>.</p>\n","sha256":"7d3a218eb2b5dd5f0c5939c926d3c14d3bdf35c643bda65a345d711fecdd438f"},{"key":"exercise-2","id":"exercise-2","title":"Exercise 2 — Intermediate: publish a polygon layer as vector tiles and OGC API Features","anchor":"exercise-2--intermediate-publish-a-polygon-layer-as-vector-tiles-and-ogc-api-features","depth":3,"start":64582,"topics":["styling"],"end":66623,"markdown":"### Exercise 2 — Intermediate: publish a polygon layer as vector tiles and OGC API Features\n\n**Task.** Using the tile math from Exercise 1 and the stack pattern of Section 5, publish a **polygon** dataset:\n1. Load a polygon layer with at least ~1 000 features into PostGIS as `city.areas` (EPSG:4326, GiST index, valid geometries, an `area_m2` column).\n2. Write `postgisftw.areas_mvt(z, x, y)` with ~1-pixel simplification and an area-based LOD rule.\n3. Serve it with pg_tileserv; publish the table through pg_featureserv.\n4. Build a MapLibre page that draws a **choropleth** (data-driven fill by `area_m2`), zoom-dependent outline width, and a click popup that fetches the full feature from the OGC API by id.\n\n**Prerequisites.** Exercise 1; Sections 4.3–4.7 and 5.\n\n**Sample input.** Any polygon dataset such as first-level administrative boundaries (for example the Natural Earth *admin-1* layer) or municipal boundaries from your region. Pick one landmark (lon/lat) inside the dataset.\n\n**Expected deliverable.** `docker-compose.yml`, SQL file(s), `index.html`, and a short `RESULTS.md` containing: tile size (bytes) of one low-zoom and one high-zoom tile, the `EXPLAIN` output of the tile query, and the tile address of your landmark at z = 10 computed with your Exercise 1 module.\n\n**Success criteria.**\n- `curl` on a tile URL returns `200` with a non-empty MVT; the decoded tile has the layer name used in your style.\n- The tile containing your landmark (computed by Exercise 1) includes the polygon that contains the landmark.\n- Low-zoom tiles are smaller than they would be without simplification/LOD (show both numbers).\n- `GET /collections/city.areas/items?limit=5` returns five GeoJSON features; the popup shows data fetched from `…/items/{id}`.\n- The tile query plan uses the GiST index on tiles that cover a small part of the dataset.\n\n**Hint.** Load with `ogr2ogr -f PostgreSQL … -lco SCHEMA=city -nln areas -lco GEOMETRY_NAME=geom -nlt PROMOTE_TO_MULTI`; run `ANALYZE`; start from the `parcels_mvt` function in Section 4.3.\n\n","lineStart":1168,"lineEnd":1190,"html":"<h2>Exercise 2 — Intermediate: publish a polygon layer as vector tiles and OGC API Features</h2>\n<p><strong>Task.</strong> Using the tile math from Exercise 1 and the stack pattern of Section 5, publish a <strong>polygon</strong> dataset:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Load a polygon layer with at least ~1 000 features into PostGIS as <code>city.areas</code> (EPSG:4326, GiST index, valid geometries, an <code>area_m2</code> column).</li>\n<li>Write <code>postgisftw.areas_mvt(z, x, y)</code> with ~1-pixel simplification and an area-based LOD rule.</li>\n<li>Serve it with pg_tileserv; publish the table through pg_featureserv.</li>\n<li>Build a MapLibre page that draws a <strong>choropleth</strong> (data-driven fill by <code>area_m2</code>), zoom-dependent outline width, and a click popup that fetches the full feature from the OGC API by id.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites.</strong> Exercise 1; Sections 4.3–4.7 and 5.</p>\n<p><strong>Sample input.</strong> Any polygon dataset such as first-level administrative boundaries (for example the Natural Earth <em>admin-1</em> layer) or municipal boundaries from your region. Pick one landmark (lon/lat) inside the dataset.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected deliverable.</strong> <code>docker-compose.yml</code>, SQL file(s), <code>index.html</code>, and a short <code>RESULTS.md</code> containing: tile size (bytes) of one low-zoom and one high-zoom tile, the <code>EXPLAIN</code> output of the tile query, and the tile address of your landmark at z = 10 computed with your Exercise 1 module.</p>\n<p><strong>Success criteria.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>curl</code> on a tile URL returns <code>200</code> with a non-empty MVT; the decoded tile has the layer name used in your style.</li>\n<li>The tile containing your landmark (computed by Exercise 1) includes the polygon that contains the landmark.</li>\n<li>Low-zoom tiles are smaller than they would be without simplification/LOD (show both numbers).</li>\n<li><code>GET /collections/city.areas/items?limit=5</code> returns five GeoJSON features; the popup shows data fetched from <code>…/items/{id}</code>.</li>\n<li>The tile query plan uses the GiST index on tiles that cover a small part of the dataset.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Hint.</strong> Load with <code>ogr2ogr -f PostgreSQL … -lco SCHEMA=city -nln areas -lco GEOMETRY_NAME=geom -nlt PROMOTE_TO_MULTI</code>; run <code>ANALYZE</code>; start from the <code>parcels_mvt</code> function in Section 4.3.</p>\n","sha256":"5e39459b966ebe72972994e291e7cf1394159c078fcbab43a3006ec24bb237f0"},{"key":"exercise-3","id":"exercise-3","title":"Exercise 3 — Advanced: scale to one million points and engineer performance","anchor":"exercise-3--advanced-scale-to-one-million-points-and-engineer-performance","depth":3,"start":66623,"topics":["performance"],"end":68698,"markdown":"### Exercise 3 — Advanced: scale to one million points and engineer performance\n\n**Task.** Extend Exercise 2's stack with a **1 000 000-point** layer and make it fast:\n1. Generate reproducible random POIs in the bbox `(4.70, 52.28, 5.10, 52.45)`.\n2. Write a dynamic tile function `postgisftw.poi_mvt` that returns **grid-aggregated counts below z = 13** and individual points from z = 13.\n3. Build a **pre-generated** alternative with Tippecanoe and serve it with Martin.\n4. Benchmark both (≥ 300 random tiles, z = 8…14) and report p50/p95 latency and maximum tile size per zoom.\n5. Write `tiles_to_purge(bbox, zmin, zmax)` that lists the tiles to invalidate after an edit, accounting for the tile buffer.\n6. Add a MapLibre **heatmap** at low zoom and individual points at high zoom, and decide where to hand over.\n\n**Prerequisites.** Exercises 1–2; Sections 4.5, 4.7.3 and 4.8.\n\n**Sample input.** The bbox above; zoom range 8–14; the changed-feature bbox `(4.9, 52.37, 4.9, 52.37)` for the purge function.\n\n**Expected deliverable.** SQL (data generation, indexes, tile function), the Tippecanoe/Martin commands, `bench.py`, `purge.py`, and a results table with a one-paragraph recommendation (dynamic vs pre-generated vs hybrid).\n\n**Success criteria.**\n- No tile exceeds 500 000 bytes at any benchmarked zoom (state whether you measured decoded or transferred size).\n- The inner tile query uses the GiST index (index or bitmap scan) for high-zoom tiles.\n- You can explain, with numbers, why un-aggregated points at z = 11 would be unacceptable (estimate points per tile from density).\n- `tiles_to_purge` returns exactly one tile for an interior point at z = 12 and includes a neighbouring tile when the change lies within the buffer distance of a tile edge.\n- Your recommendation justifies hybrid vs single approach using measured latency.\n\n**Hint.** `ST_SnapToGrid(geom_3857, res * 32)` makes ~32-pixel cells; `GROUP BY` the snapped point and `count(*)`. For tiles below z = 8 consider a pre-aggregated table or Tippecanoe rather than scanning a million rows per request.\n\n","lineStart":1191,"lineEnd":1215,"html":"<h2>Exercise 3 — Advanced: scale to one million points and engineer performance</h2>\n<p><strong>Task.</strong> Extend Exercise 2&#39;s stack with a <strong>1 000 000-point</strong> layer and make it fast:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Generate reproducible random POIs in the bbox <code>(4.70, 52.28, 5.10, 52.45)</code>.</li>\n<li>Write a dynamic tile function <code>postgisftw.poi_mvt</code> that returns <strong>grid-aggregated counts below z = 13</strong> and individual points from z = 13.</li>\n<li>Build a <strong>pre-generated</strong> alternative with Tippecanoe and serve it with Martin.</li>\n<li>Benchmark both (≥ 300 random tiles, z = 8…14) and report p50/p95 latency and maximum tile size per zoom.</li>\n<li>Write <code>tiles_to_purge(bbox, zmin, zmax)</code> that lists the tiles to invalidate after an edit, accounting for the tile buffer.</li>\n<li>Add a MapLibre <strong>heatmap</strong> at low zoom and individual points at high zoom, and decide where to hand over.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites.</strong> Exercises 1–2; Sections 4.5, 4.7.3 and 4.8.</p>\n<p><strong>Sample input.</strong> The bbox above; zoom range 8–14; the changed-feature bbox <code>(4.9, 52.37, 4.9, 52.37)</code> for the purge function.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected deliverable.</strong> SQL (data generation, indexes, tile function), the Tippecanoe/Martin commands, <code>bench.py</code>, <code>purge.py</code>, and a results table with a one-paragraph recommendation (dynamic vs pre-generated vs hybrid).</p>\n<p><strong>Success criteria.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>No tile exceeds 500 000 bytes at any benchmarked zoom (state whether you measured decoded or transferred size).</li>\n<li>The inner tile query uses the GiST index (index or bitmap scan) for high-zoom tiles.</li>\n<li>You can explain, with numbers, why un-aggregated points at z = 11 would be unacceptable (estimate points per tile from density).</li>\n<li><code>tiles_to_purge</code> returns exactly one tile for an interior point at z = 12 and includes a neighbouring tile when the change lies within the buffer distance of a tile edge.</li>\n<li>Your recommendation justifies hybrid vs single approach using measured latency.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Hint.</strong> <code>ST_SnapToGrid(geom_3857, res * 32)</code> makes ~32-pixel cells; <code>GROUP BY</code> the snapped point and <code>count(*)</code>. For tiles below z = 8 consider a pre-aggregated table or Tippecanoe rather than scanning a million rows per request.</p>\n","sha256":"139887339259ade6a9b4ac70572db2873e38583215ddcb7c863921f1e6a9495b"},{"key":"exercise-4","id":"exercise-4","title":"Exercise 4 — Applied challenge: a secure, editable inventory","anchor":"exercise-4--applied-challenge-a-secure-editable-inventory","depth":3,"start":68698,"topics":["capstone"],"end":71156,"markdown":"### Exercise 4 — Applied challenge: a secure, editable inventory\n\n**Task.** Combine everything into a small production-style system for the Exercise 3 POI data:\n1. **Public view:** vector tiles that never expose non-public rows (dynamic or pre-generated, behind a caching reverse proxy).\n2. **Editor API:** JWT authentication; `owner_org` from the token; PostgreSQL RLS; optimistic concurrency with `ETag`/`If-Match`; audit trail. Shape the routes like OGC API – Features Part 4: `POST /collections/poi/items`, `PUT|PATCH|DELETE /collections/poi/items/{id}` with GeoJSON bodies.\n3. **Editor UI:** add, move and delete points, and edit attributes, using either OpenLayers (`Draw`/`Modify`/`Snap`) or MapLibre with a drawing plugin of your choice (for example Terra Draw or Mapbox GL Draw).\n4. **Freshness:** after each successful edit, make affected tiles fresh using your `tiles_to_purge` (purge, versioned URL or short TTL — justify the choice).\n5. **Tests and threat model:** automated tests for the security rules and a one-page threat model.\n\n**Prerequisites.** Exercises 1–3; Sections 4.9, 5.6 and 6.4.\n\n**Sample input.** Two organisations (`parks`, `harbor`), two users each (JWTs with `sub`, `org`, `exp`), 1 000 000 POIs split roughly evenly between organisations with 10 % flagged non-public.\n\n**Expected deliverable.** A repository with `docker-compose.yml`, SQL migrations, the API, the web client, a test suite (`pytest`), and `README.md` containing the threat model and a results table.\n\n**Success criteria.**\n- Anonymous requests to every public endpoint (tiles, features) never return a non-public POI (test on at least three tiles that contain one).\n- A `parks` token can never read, update or delete a `harbor` row through any route; the response is `404`, identical to a missing id.\n- Two concurrent `PATCH` requests with the same `ETag`: exactly one succeeds, the other gets `412`/`409`.\n- With the caching proxy enabled, requesting an editor-only tile or feature response as user A and then as user B never returns A's data.\n- After an edit, the edited feature appears in a fresh tile within the freshness target you defined.\n- At least 8 automated tests pass in CI; the threat model lists at least five threats with mitigations.\n\n**Hint.** Reuse the `session()` pattern from Section 5.6 (transaction-local `set_config`), keep tile endpoints public-only, and serve everything user-specific from `Cache-Control: private` API routes.\n\n---\n\n","lineStart":1216,"lineEnd":1242,"html":"<h2>Exercise 4 — Applied challenge: a secure, editable inventory</h2>\n<p><strong>Task.</strong> Combine everything into a small production-style system for the Exercise 3 POI data:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Public view:</strong> vector tiles that never expose non-public rows (dynamic or pre-generated, behind a caching reverse proxy).</li>\n<li><strong>Editor API:</strong> JWT authentication; <code>owner_org</code> from the token; PostgreSQL RLS; optimistic concurrency with <code>ETag</code>/<code>If-Match</code>; audit trail. Shape the routes like OGC API – Features Part 4: <code>POST /collections/poi/items</code>, <code>PUT|PATCH|DELETE /collections/poi/items/{id}</code> with GeoJSON bodies.</li>\n<li><strong>Editor UI:</strong> add, move and delete points, and edit attributes, using either OpenLayers (<code>Draw</code>/<code>Modify</code>/<code>Snap</code>) or MapLibre with a drawing plugin of your choice (for example Terra Draw or Mapbox GL Draw).</li>\n<li><strong>Freshness:</strong> after each successful edit, make affected tiles fresh using your <code>tiles_to_purge</code> (purge, versioned URL or short TTL — justify the choice).</li>\n<li><strong>Tests and threat model:</strong> automated tests for the security rules and a one-page threat model.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites.</strong> Exercises 1–3; Sections 4.9, 5.6 and 6.4.</p>\n<p><strong>Sample input.</strong> Two organisations (<code>parks</code>, <code>harbor</code>), two users each (JWTs with <code>sub</code>, <code>org</code>, <code>exp</code>), 1 000 000 POIs split roughly evenly between organisations with 10 % flagged non-public.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected deliverable.</strong> A repository with <code>docker-compose.yml</code>, SQL migrations, the API, the web client, a test suite (<code>pytest</code>), and <code>README.md</code> containing the threat model and a results table.</p>\n<p><strong>Success criteria.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Anonymous requests to every public endpoint (tiles, features) never return a non-public POI (test on at least three tiles that contain one).</li>\n<li>A <code>parks</code> token can never read, update or delete a <code>harbor</code> row through any route; the response is <code>404</code>, identical to a missing id.</li>\n<li>Two concurrent <code>PATCH</code> requests with the same <code>ETag</code>: exactly one succeeds, the other gets <code>412</code>/<code>409</code>.</li>\n<li>With the caching proxy enabled, requesting an editor-only tile or feature response as user A and then as user B never returns A&#39;s data.</li>\n<li>After an edit, the edited feature appears in a fresh tile within the freshness target you defined.</li>\n<li>At least 8 automated tests pass in CI; the threat model lists at least five threats with mitigations.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Hint.</strong> Reuse the <code>session()</code> pattern from Section 5.6 (transaction-local <code>set_config</code>), keep tile endpoints public-only, and serve everything user-specific from <code>Cache-Control: private</code> API routes.</p>\n<hr>\n","sha256":"0ed4050e98d389f08d15b49de3e1b58f32c97b8bdcec50d8ce9034dacb0803a2"},{"key":"8","id":"8","title":"8. Solutions and explanations","anchor":"8-solutions-and-explanations","depth":2,"start":71156,"topics":["tile-math"],"end":71391,"markdown":"## 8. Solutions and explanations\n\n> Attempt each exercise first. Numbers below that come from the tile math were computed by running the code shown; benchmark numbers depend on your hardware and are therefore described qualitatively.\n\n","lineStart":1243,"lineEnd":1246,"html":"<h2>8. Solutions and explanations</h2>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Attempt each exercise first. Numbers below that come from the tile math were computed by running the code shown; benchmark numbers depend on your hardware and are therefore described qualitatively.</p>\n</blockquote>\n","sha256":"588bf1b2ed50b76a9963d7c3a2918e7fb0a0c2298dc2e4c1c3858acc5cc7fcaf"},{"key":"solution-1","id":"solution-1","title":"Solution 1 — tile-math toolkit","anchor":"solution-1--tile-math-toolkit","depth":3,"start":71391,"topics":["tile-math"],"end":76292,"markdown":"### Solution 1 — tile-math toolkit\n\n`tile_math.py`:\n\n```python\n\"\"\"Web Mercator tile helpers (Exercise 1). Pure standard library.\"\"\"\nimport math\n\nR = 6378137.0                         # spherical radius used by EPSG:3857 (metres)\nC = 2 * math.pi * R                   # world width: 40 075 016.685578... m\nHALF = C / 2                          # 20 037 508.342789... m\nMAX_LAT = math.degrees(math.atan(math.sinh(math.pi)))   # 85.0511287798066\n\ndef lonlat_to_tile(lon: float, lat: float, z: int) -> tuple[int, int]:\n    \"\"\"XYZ tile (x, y) containing (lon, lat) at zoom z. Latitude is clamped to the Mercator limit.\"\"\"\n    lat = max(min(lat, MAX_LAT), -MAX_LAT)\n    n = 2 ** z\n    x = (lon + 180.0) / 360.0 * n\n    y = (1.0 - math.asinh(math.tan(math.radians(lat))) / math.pi) / 2.0 * n\n    return min(max(int(math.floor(x)), 0), n - 1), min(max(int(math.floor(y)), 0), n - 1)\n\ndef tile_bounds_3857(z: int, x: int, y: int) -> tuple[float, float, float, float]:\n    \"\"\"(minX, minY, maxX, maxY) in EPSG:3857 metres.\"\"\"\n    size = C / 2 ** z\n    min_x = -HALF + x * size\n    max_y = HALF - y * size\n    return (min_x, max_y - size, min_x + size, max_y)\n\ndef tile_bounds_4326(z: int, x: int, y: int) -> tuple[float, float, float, float]:\n    \"\"\"(west, south, east, north) in degrees.\"\"\"\n    n = 2 ** z\n    def lat(row: float) -> float:\n        return math.degrees(math.atan(math.sinh(math.pi * (1 - 2 * row / n))))\n    return (x / n * 360 - 180, lat(y + 1), (x + 1) / n * 360 - 180, lat(y))\n\ndef resolution(z: int, lat: float = 0.0, tile_size: int = 256) -> float:\n    \"\"\"Ground metres per pixel at zoom z and latitude lat.\"\"\"\n    return C / (tile_size * 2 ** z) * math.cos(math.radians(lat))\n\ndef tms_y(z: int, y_xyz: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Flip between XYZ and TMS row numbering (the operation is its own inverse).\"\"\"\n    return 2 ** z - 1 - y_xyz\n\ndef tiles_for_bbox(west: float, south: float, east: float, north: float, z: int):\n    \"\"\"Yield every XYZ tile (z, x, y) intersecting the lon/lat bbox.\"\"\"\n    x0, y1 = lonlat_to_tile(west, south, z)      # south-west corner -> largest y\n    x1, y0 = lonlat_to_tile(east, north, z)      # north-east corner -> smallest y\n    for x in range(x0, x1 + 1):\n        for y in range(y0, y1 + 1):\n            yield (z, x, y)\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    assert lonlat_to_tile(4.9, 52.37, 12) == (2103, 1346)\n    assert lonlat_to_tile(4.9, 52.37, 14) == (8415, 5384)\n    assert lonlat_to_tile(-74.0060, 40.7128, 10) == (301, 385)\n    assert lonlat_to_tile(139.6917, 35.6895, 16) == (58198, 25804)\n    assert lonlat_to_tile(0, 0, 1) == (1, 1)\n    assert lonlat_to_tile(180, 90, 3) == (7, 0)            # clamped, never out of range\n    w, s, e, n = tile_bounds_4326(12, 2103, 1346)\n    assert w <= 4.9 < e and s <= 52.37 < n                 # the point lies inside its tile\n    assert abs(resolution(0) - 156543.03392804097) < 1e-6\n    assert tms_y(12, tms_y(12, 1346)) == 1346\n    counts = {z: len(list(tiles_for_bbox(4.85, 52.34, 4.95, 52.40, z))) for z in range(10, 15)}\n    assert counts == {10: 2, 11: 4, 12: 4, 13: 9, 14: 30}, counts\n    print(\"all checks passed\")\n    print(\"bounds 3857:\", [round(v, 2) for v in tile_bounds_3857(12, 2103, 1346)])\n    print(\"bounds 4326:\", [round(v, 6) for v in tile_bounds_4326(12, 2103, 1346)])\n    print(\"tms y:\", tms_y(12, 1346))\n    print(\"bbox tile counts:\", counts)\n```\n\n**Expected output**\n\n| Call | Result |\n|---|---|\n| `lonlat_to_tile(4.9, 52.37, 12)` | `(2103, 1346)` |\n| `lonlat_to_tile(4.9, 52.37, 14)` | `(8415, 5384)` |\n| `lonlat_to_tile(-74.0060, 40.7128, 10)` | `(301, 385)` |\n| `lonlat_to_tile(139.6917, 35.6895, 16)` | `(58198, 25804)` |\n| `lonlat_to_tile(0, 0, 1)` | `(1, 1)` |\n| `lonlat_to_tile(180, 90, 3)` | `(7, 0)` (clamped) |\n| `tile_bounds_3857(12, 2103, 1346)` | `(538116.68, 6858541.67, 547900.62, 6868325.61)` |\n| `tile_bounds_4326(12, 2103, 1346)` | `(4.833984, 52.321911, 4.921875, 52.375599)` |\n| `tms_y(12, 1346)` | `2749` |\n| `tiles_for_bbox(4.85, 52.34, 4.95, 52.40, z)` for z = 10…14 | 2, 4, 4, 9, 30 tiles (at z = 14: x 8412–8417, y 5382–5386) |\n\n**Reasoning.**\n- `(0, 0)` at z = 1 lies exactly on the corner shared by all four tiles; the `floor` rule assigns it to the south-east tile `(1, 1)` — a useful reminder that tile boundaries are half-open intervals.\n- Counts grow roughly ×4 per zoom for a *large* area, but for a small bbox they depend on how the bbox aligns with tile edges (4 → 4 → 9 → 30 here), which is why the number of tiles needed is not a clean power of four.\n- `lonlat_to_tile` clamps both latitude (Mercator limit) and the index (so `lon = 180` does not produce `x = 2^z`).\n\n**Verify against PostGIS.**\n\n```sql\nSELECT round(ST_XMin(g)::numeric, 2), round(ST_YMin(g)::numeric, 2),\n       round(ST_XMax(g)::numeric, 2), round(ST_YMax(g)::numeric, 2)\nFROM (SELECT ST_TileEnvelope(12, 2103, 1346) AS g) s;\n-- expected: 538116.68 | 6858541.67 | 547900.62 | 6868325.61\n```\n\n","lineStart":1247,"lineEnd":1346,"html":"<h2>Solution 1 — tile-math toolkit</h2>\n<p><code>tile_math.py</code>:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">&quot;&quot;&quot;Web Mercator tile helpers (Exercise 1). Pure standard library.&quot;&quot;&quot;\nimport math\n\nR = 6378137.0                         # spherical radius used by EPSG:3857 (metres)\nC = 2 * math.pi * R                   # world width: 40 075 016.685578... m\nHALF = C / 2                          # 20 037 508.342789... m\nMAX_LAT = math.degrees(math.atan(math.sinh(math.pi)))   # 85.0511287798066\n\ndef lonlat_to_tile(lon: float, lat: float, z: int) -&gt; tuple[int, int]:\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;XYZ tile (x, y) containing (lon, lat) at zoom z. Latitude is clamped to the Mercator limit.&quot;&quot;&quot;\n    lat = max(min(lat, MAX_LAT), -MAX_LAT)\n    n = 2 ** z\n    x = (lon + 180.0) / 360.0 * n\n    y = (1.0 - math.asinh(math.tan(math.radians(lat))) / math.pi) / 2.0 * n\n    return min(max(int(math.floor(x)), 0), n - 1), min(max(int(math.floor(y)), 0), n - 1)\n\ndef tile_bounds_3857(z: int, x: int, y: int) -&gt; tuple[float, float, float, float]:\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;(minX, minY, maxX, maxY) in EPSG:3857 metres.&quot;&quot;&quot;\n    size = C / 2 ** z\n    min_x = -HALF + x * size\n    max_y = HALF - y * size\n    return (min_x, max_y - size, min_x + size, max_y)\n\ndef tile_bounds_4326(z: int, x: int, y: int) -&gt; tuple[float, float, float, float]:\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;(west, south, east, north) in degrees.&quot;&quot;&quot;\n    n = 2 ** z\n    def lat(row: float) -&gt; float:\n        return math.degrees(math.atan(math.sinh(math.pi * (1 - 2 * row / n))))\n    return (x / n * 360 - 180, lat(y + 1), (x + 1) / n * 360 - 180, lat(y))\n\ndef resolution(z: int, lat: float = 0.0, tile_size: int = 256) -&gt; float:\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;Ground metres per pixel at zoom z and latitude lat.&quot;&quot;&quot;\n    return C / (tile_size * 2 ** z) * math.cos(math.radians(lat))\n\ndef tms_y(z: int, y_xyz: int) -&gt; int:\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;Flip between XYZ and TMS row numbering (the operation is its own inverse).&quot;&quot;&quot;\n    return 2 ** z - 1 - y_xyz\n\ndef tiles_for_bbox(west: float, south: float, east: float, north: float, z: int):\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;Yield every XYZ tile (z, x, y) intersecting the lon/lat bbox.&quot;&quot;&quot;\n    x0, y1 = lonlat_to_tile(west, south, z)      # south-west corner -&gt; largest y\n    x1, y0 = lonlat_to_tile(east, north, z)      # north-east corner -&gt; smallest y\n    for x in range(x0, x1 + 1):\n        for y in range(y0, y1 + 1):\n            yield (z, x, y)\n\nif __name__ == &quot;__main__&quot;:\n    assert lonlat_to_tile(4.9, 52.37, 12) == (2103, 1346)\n    assert lonlat_to_tile(4.9, 52.37, 14) == (8415, 5384)\n    assert lonlat_to_tile(-74.0060, 40.7128, 10) == (301, 385)\n    assert lonlat_to_tile(139.6917, 35.6895, 16) == (58198, 25804)\n    assert lonlat_to_tile(0, 0, 1) == (1, 1)\n    assert lonlat_to_tile(180, 90, 3) == (7, 0)            # clamped, never out of range\n    w, s, e, n = tile_bounds_4326(12, 2103, 1346)\n    assert w &lt;= 4.9 &lt; e and s &lt;= 52.37 &lt; n                 # the point lies inside its tile\n    assert abs(resolution(0) - 156543.03392804097) &lt; 1e-6\n    assert tms_y(12, tms_y(12, 1346)) == 1346\n    counts = {z: len(list(tiles_for_bbox(4.85, 52.34, 4.95, 52.40, z))) for z in range(10, 15)}\n    assert counts == {10: 2, 11: 4, 12: 4, 13: 9, 14: 30}, counts\n    print(&quot;all checks passed&quot;)\n    print(&quot;bounds 3857:&quot;, [round(v, 2) for v in tile_bounds_3857(12, 2103, 1346)])\n    print(&quot;bounds 4326:&quot;, [round(v, 6) for v in tile_bounds_4326(12, 2103, 1346)])\n    print(&quot;tms y:&quot;, tms_y(12, 1346))\n    print(&quot;bbox tile counts:&quot;, counts)</code></pre><p><strong>Expected output</strong></p>\n<div class=\"source-table\" role=\"region\" tabindex=\"0\" aria-label=\"Table in Solution 1 — tile-math toolkit\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Call</th>\n<th>Result</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>lonlat_to_tile(4.9, 52.37, 12)</code></td>\n<td><code>(2103, 1346)</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>lonlat_to_tile(4.9, 52.37, 14)</code></td>\n<td><code>(8415, 5384)</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>lonlat_to_tile(-74.0060, 40.7128, 10)</code></td>\n<td><code>(301, 385)</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>lonlat_to_tile(139.6917, 35.6895, 16)</code></td>\n<td><code>(58198, 25804)</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>lonlat_to_tile(0, 0, 1)</code></td>\n<td><code>(1, 1)</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>lonlat_to_tile(180, 90, 3)</code></td>\n<td><code>(7, 0)</code> (clamped)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>tile_bounds_3857(12, 2103, 1346)</code></td>\n<td><code>(538116.68, 6858541.67, 547900.62, 6868325.61)</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>tile_bounds_4326(12, 2103, 1346)</code></td>\n<td><code>(4.833984, 52.321911, 4.921875, 52.375599)</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>tms_y(12, 1346)</code></td>\n<td><code>2749</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>tiles_for_bbox(4.85, 52.34, 4.95, 52.40, z)</code> for z = 10…14</td>\n<td>2, 4, 4, 9, 30 tiles (at z = 14: x 8412–8417, y 5382–5386)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div><p><strong>Reasoning.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>(0, 0)</code> at z = 1 lies exactly on the corner shared by all four tiles; the <code>floor</code> rule assigns it to the south-east tile <code>(1, 1)</code> — a useful reminder that tile boundaries are half-open intervals.</li>\n<li>Counts grow roughly ×4 per zoom for a <em>large</em> area, but for a small bbox they depend on how the bbox aligns with tile edges (4 → 4 → 9 → 30 here), which is why the number of tiles needed is not a clean power of four.</li>\n<li><code>lonlat_to_tile</code> clamps both latitude (Mercator limit) and the index (so <code>lon = 180</code> does not produce <code>x = 2^z</code>).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Verify against PostGIS.</strong></p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-sql\">SELECT round(ST_XMin(g)::numeric, 2), round(ST_YMin(g)::numeric, 2),\n       round(ST_XMax(g)::numeric, 2), round(ST_YMax(g)::numeric, 2)\nFROM (SELECT ST_TileEnvelope(12, 2103, 1346) AS g) s;\n-- expected: 538116.68 | 6858541.67 | 547900.62 | 6868325.61</code></pre>","sha256":"f9cd9f6908a5a5e5e3bef6f0d7524181639c3a7af955e4856245305629d9b9b7"},{"key":"solution-2","id":"solution-2","title":"Solution 2 — polygon layer: vector tiles + OGC API","anchor":"solution-2--polygon-layer-vector-tiles--ogc-api","depth":3,"start":76292,"topics":["styling"],"end":80253,"markdown":"### Solution 2 — polygon layer: vector tiles + OGC API\n\n**1. Load and prepare**\n\n```bash\nogr2ogr -f PostgreSQL PG:\"host=localhost dbname=gis user=postgres password=postgres\" \\\n  ne_10m_admin_1_states_provinces.shp \\\n  -lco SCHEMA=city -nln areas -lco GEOMETRY_NAME=geom -lco FID=id \\\n  -nlt PROMOTE_TO_MULTI -t_srs EPSG:4326 -overwrite\n```\n\n```sql\nUPDATE city.areas SET geom = ST_Multi(ST_CollectionExtract(ST_MakeValid(geom), 3)) WHERE NOT ST_IsValid(geom);\nALTER TABLE city.areas ADD COLUMN area_m2 double precision;\nUPDATE city.areas SET area_m2 = ST_Area(geom::geography);      -- true area on the ellipsoid\nANALYZE city.areas;\nGRANT SELECT ON city.areas TO tile_reader;\n```\n(`ogr2ogr` creates a GiST index by default; adjust the attribute names — here `name` — to your dataset.)\n\n**2. Tile function** (same pattern as `parcels_mvt` in Section 4.3)\n\n```sql\nCREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION postgisftw.areas_mvt(z integer, x integer, y integer)\nRETURNS bytea LANGUAGE sql STABLE PARALLEL SAFE AS $$\n  WITH env AS (\n    SELECT ST_TileEnvelope(z, x, y) AS g3857, 156543.03392804097 / (2 ^ z) AS res\n  ),\n  mvtgeom AS (\n    SELECT a.id, a.name, a.area_m2,\n           ST_AsMVTGeom(ST_SimplifyPreserveTopology(ST_Transform(a.geom, 3857), env.res),\n                        env.g3857, 4096, 64, true) AS geom\n    FROM city.areas a, env\n    WHERE a.geom && ST_Transform(env.g3857, 4326)\n      AND (z >= 8 OR a.area_m2 >= env.res * env.res * 16)\n  )\n  SELECT COALESCE(ST_AsMVT(mvtgeom.*, 'areas', 4096, 'geom', 'id'), ''::bytea) FROM mvtgeom;\n$$;\nGRANT EXECUTE ON FUNCTION postgisftw.areas_mvt(integer, integer, integer) TO tile_reader;\n```\n\n**3. Client** (add to the MapLibre page from Section 5.7)\n\n```js\nmap.addSource('areas', { type: 'vector', tiles: ['http://localhost:7800/postgisftw.areas_mvt/{z}/{x}/{y}.pbf'], maxzoom: 12 });\nmap.addLayer({ id: 'areas-fill', type: 'fill', source: 'areas', 'source-layer': 'areas',\n  paint: { 'fill-opacity': 0.7,\n           'fill-color': ['step', ['get', 'area_m2'], '#edf8fb', 1e9, '#b3cde3', 1e10, '#8c96c6', 1e11, '#88419d'] } });\nmap.addLayer({ id: 'areas-line', type: 'line', source: 'areas', 'source-layer': 'areas',\n  paint: { 'line-color': '#333', 'line-width': ['interpolate', ['linear'], ['zoom'], 2, 0.3, 10, 1.5] } });\n\nmap.on('click', 'areas-fill', async (e) => {\n  const id = e.features[0].id;                                   // from ST_AsMVT's id column\n  const f = await (await fetch(`http://localhost:9000/collections/city.areas/items/${id}`)).json();\n  const div = document.createElement('div');\n  div.textContent = `${f.properties.name}: ${Math.round(f.properties.area_m2 / 1e6).toLocaleString()} km²`;\n  new maplibregl.Popup().setLngLat(e.lngLat).setDOMContent(div).addTo(map);     // textContent, not HTML: no XSS\n});\n```\n\n**4. Verify**\n\n```bash\n# tile for your landmark: compute (z, x, y) with Exercise 1, then\ncurl -s -o /dev/null -w \"%{http_code} %{size_download}\\n\" http://localhost:7800/postgisftw.areas_mvt/10/<x>/<y>.pbf\ncurl -s \"http://localhost:9000/collections/city.areas/items?limit=5\" | python3 -c \"import sys,json; print(len(json.load(sys.stdin)['features']))\"   # expected 5\n```\n\n```sql\nEXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)\nSELECT count(*) FROM city.areas a WHERE a.geom && ST_Transform(ST_TileEnvelope(10, <x>, <y>), 4326);\n-- expect a Bitmap/Index scan on the GiST index (its name varies) for a small tile;\n-- a very large tile covering most of the table may legitimately choose a sequential scan.\n```\n\n**Showing the LOD/simplification benefit.** Create a copy of the function without `ST_SimplifyPreserveTopology` and without the area test (`areas_mvt_raw`), request the same low-zoom tile from both, and record `size_download`. The reduction is dataset-dependent; the point is to measure it rather than assume it. Decode the tile with `mapbox_vector_tile` (Section 5.5) to confirm the layer name is `areas` and that the polygon containing your landmark is present in the tile Exercise 1 predicted.\n\n","lineStart":1347,"lineEnd":1423,"html":"<h2>Solution 2 — polygon layer: vector tiles + OGC API</h2>\n<p><strong>1. Load and prepare</strong></p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-bash\">ogr2ogr -f PostgreSQL PG:&quot;host=localhost dbname=gis user=postgres password=postgres&quot; \\\n  ne_10m_admin_1_states_provinces.shp \\\n  -lco SCHEMA=city -nln areas -lco GEOMETRY_NAME=geom -lco FID=id \\\n  -nlt PROMOTE_TO_MULTI -t_srs EPSG:4326 -overwrite</code></pre><pre><code class=\"language-sql\">UPDATE city.areas SET geom = ST_Multi(ST_CollectionExtract(ST_MakeValid(geom), 3)) WHERE NOT ST_IsValid(geom);\nALTER TABLE city.areas ADD COLUMN area_m2 double precision;\nUPDATE city.areas SET area_m2 = ST_Area(geom::geography);      -- true area on the ellipsoid\nANALYZE city.areas;\nGRANT SELECT ON city.areas TO tile_reader;</code></pre><p>(<code>ogr2ogr</code> creates a GiST index by default; adjust the attribute names — here <code>name</code> — to your dataset.)</p>\n<p><strong>2. Tile function</strong> (same pattern as <code>parcels_mvt</code> in Section 4.3)</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-sql\">CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION postgisftw.areas_mvt(z integer, x integer, y integer)\nRETURNS bytea LANGUAGE sql STABLE PARALLEL SAFE AS $$\n  WITH env AS (\n    SELECT ST_TileEnvelope(z, x, y) AS g3857, 156543.03392804097 / (2 ^ z) AS res\n  ),\n  mvtgeom AS (\n    SELECT a.id, a.name, a.area_m2,\n           ST_AsMVTGeom(ST_SimplifyPreserveTopology(ST_Transform(a.geom, 3857), env.res),\n                        env.g3857, 4096, 64, true) AS geom\n    FROM city.areas a, env\n    WHERE a.geom &amp;&amp; ST_Transform(env.g3857, 4326)\n      AND (z &gt;= 8 OR a.area_m2 &gt;= env.res * env.res * 16)\n  )\n  SELECT COALESCE(ST_AsMVT(mvtgeom.*, &#39;areas&#39;, 4096, &#39;geom&#39;, &#39;id&#39;), &#39;&#39;::bytea) FROM mvtgeom;\n$$;\nGRANT EXECUTE ON FUNCTION postgisftw.areas_mvt(integer, integer, integer) TO tile_reader;</code></pre><p><strong>3. Client</strong> (add to the MapLibre page from Section 5.7)</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-js\">map.addSource(&#39;areas&#39;, { type: &#39;vector&#39;, tiles: [&#39;http://localhost:7800/postgisftw.areas_mvt/{z}/{x}/{y}.pbf&#39;], maxzoom: 12 });\nmap.addLayer({ id: &#39;areas-fill&#39;, type: &#39;fill&#39;, source: &#39;areas&#39;, &#39;source-layer&#39;: &#39;areas&#39;,\n  paint: { &#39;fill-opacity&#39;: 0.7,\n           &#39;fill-color&#39;: [&#39;step&#39;, [&#39;get&#39;, &#39;area_m2&#39;], &#39;#edf8fb&#39;, 1e9, &#39;#b3cde3&#39;, 1e10, &#39;#8c96c6&#39;, 1e11, &#39;#88419d&#39;] } });\nmap.addLayer({ id: &#39;areas-line&#39;, type: &#39;line&#39;, source: &#39;areas&#39;, &#39;source-layer&#39;: &#39;areas&#39;,\n  paint: { &#39;line-color&#39;: &#39;#333&#39;, &#39;line-width&#39;: [&#39;interpolate&#39;, [&#39;linear&#39;], [&#39;zoom&#39;], 2, 0.3, 10, 1.5] } });\n\nmap.on(&#39;click&#39;, &#39;areas-fill&#39;, async (e) =&gt; {\n  const id = e.features[0].id;                                   // from ST_AsMVT&#39;s id column\n  const f = await (await fetch(`http://localhost:9000/collections/city.areas/items/${id}`)).json();\n  const div = document.createElement(&#39;div&#39;);\n  div.textContent = `${f.properties.name}: ${Math.round(f.properties.area_m2 / 1e6).toLocaleString()} km²`;\n  new maplibregl.Popup().setLngLat(e.lngLat).setDOMContent(div).addTo(map);     // textContent, not HTML: no XSS\n});</code></pre><p><strong>4. Verify</strong></p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-bash\"># tile for your landmark: compute (z, x, y) with Exercise 1, then\ncurl -s -o /dev/null -w &quot;%{http_code} %{size_download}\\n&quot; http://localhost:7800/postgisftw.areas_mvt/10/&lt;x&gt;/&lt;y&gt;.pbf\ncurl -s &quot;http://localhost:9000/collections/city.areas/items?limit=5&quot; | python3 -c &quot;import sys,json; print(len(json.load(sys.stdin)[&#39;features&#39;]))&quot;   # expected 5</code></pre><pre><code class=\"language-sql\">EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)\nSELECT count(*) FROM city.areas a WHERE a.geom &amp;&amp; ST_Transform(ST_TileEnvelope(10, &lt;x&gt;, &lt;y&gt;), 4326);\n-- expect a Bitmap/Index scan on the GiST index (its name varies) for a small tile;\n-- a very large tile covering most of the table may legitimately choose a sequential scan.</code></pre><p><strong>Showing the LOD/simplification benefit.</strong> Create a copy of the function without <code>ST_SimplifyPreserveTopology</code> and without the area test (<code>areas_mvt_raw</code>), request the same low-zoom tile from both, and record <code>size_download</code>. The reduction is dataset-dependent; the point is to measure it rather than assume it. Decode the tile with <code>mapbox_vector_tile</code> (Section 5.5) to confirm the layer name is <code>areas</code> and that the polygon containing your landmark is present in the tile Exercise 1 predicted.</p>\n","sha256":"486e2c4e74658a2df25733edf46cfc8296cde3e5a83a09332ba1186a982f3278"},{"key":"solution-3","id":"solution-3","title":"Solution 3 — one million points","anchor":"solution-3--one-million-points","depth":3,"start":80253,"topics":["performance"],"end":88200,"markdown":"### Solution 3 — one million points\n\n**1. Reproducible data**\n\n```sql\nSELECT setseed(0.42);\nCREATE TABLE city.poi AS\nSELECT g AS id,\n       ST_SetSRID(ST_MakePoint(4.70 + random() * 0.40, 52.28 + random() * 0.17), 4326) AS geom,\n       (ARRAY['cafe','shop','school','park'])[1 + floor(random() * 4)::int] AS kind\nFROM generate_series(1, 1000000) AS g;\nALTER TABLE city.poi ADD PRIMARY KEY (id);\nCREATE INDEX poi_geom_gix ON city.poi USING gist (geom);\nANALYZE city.poi;\nGRANT SELECT ON city.poi TO tile_reader;\n```\n\n**2. Why aggregate? Estimate points per tile.** The bbox is about 27.2 km × 18.8 km ≈ 511 km², so the density is ≈ 1 957 points/km². Ground tile width at this latitude is `tile width × cos φ`:\n\n| z | Ground tile width (km) | Points per tile (≈) |\n|---:|---:|---:|\n| 10 | 23.9 | 1 117 000 (the whole dataset) |\n| 11 | 11.95 | 279 000 |\n| 12 | 5.97 | 70 000 |\n| 13 | 2.99 | 17 500 |\n| 14 | 1.49 | 4 400 |\n\nEven at an optimistic ~10 bytes per point, a z = 11 tile would be ≈ 2.8 MB — far beyond the 500 KB budget (this is an order-of-magnitude estimate; measure real sizes). Hence: aggregate below z = 13, individual points from z = 13.\n\n**3. Hybrid tile function**\n\n```sql\nCREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION postgisftw.poi_mvt(z integer, x integer, y integer)\nRETURNS bytea LANGUAGE sql STABLE PARALLEL SAFE AS $$\n  WITH env AS (\n    SELECT ST_TileEnvelope(z, x, y) AS g3857, 156543.03392804097 / (2 ^ z) AS res\n  ),\n  agg AS (                                                   -- z < 13: ~32-pixel grid cells with counts\n    SELECT ST_SnapToGrid(ST_Transform(p.geom, 3857), env.res * 32) AS cell, count(*) AS n\n    FROM city.poi p, env\n    WHERE z < 13 AND p.geom && ST_Transform(env.g3857, 4326)\n    GROUP BY 1\n  ),\n  mvtgeom AS (\n    SELECT NULL::bigint AS id, agg.n AS point_count,\n           ST_AsMVTGeom(agg.cell, env.g3857, 4096, 64, true) AS geom\n    FROM agg, env\n    UNION ALL\n    SELECT p.id, 1 AS point_count,\n           ST_AsMVTGeom(ST_Transform(p.geom, 3857), env.g3857, 4096, 64, true)\n    FROM city.poi p, env\n    WHERE z >= 13 AND p.geom && ST_Transform(env.g3857, 4326)\n  )\n  SELECT COALESCE(ST_AsMVT(mvtgeom.*, 'poi', 4096, 'geom', 'id'), ''::bytea) FROM mvtgeom;\n$$;\nGRANT EXECUTE ON FUNCTION postgisftw.poi_mvt(integer, integer, integer) TO tile_reader;\n```\n\nThe predicates `z < 13` / `z >= 13` depend only on the function parameter, so PostgreSQL can skip the branch that does not apply (look for a *One-Time Filter* in the plan). Cells are snapped to a global grid, so a cell that straddles a tile edge may appear with partial counts in two tiles (pitfall 12).\n\n**4. Pre-generated alternative**\n\n```bash\nogr2ogr -f GeoJSONSeq poi.geojsonl PG:\"host=localhost dbname=gis user=postgres password=postgres\" city.poi\ntippecanoe -o poi.mbtiles -l poi -zg --drop-densest-as-needed --force poi.geojsonl\nmartin poi.mbtiles                         # http://localhost:3000/poi/{z}/{x}/{y}\n```\n\n**5. Benchmark and purge helpers** — `bench.py` (needs Exercise 1's module):\n\n```python\n\"\"\"Latency / size benchmark for a tile endpoint (Exercise 3). Needs: pip install requests\"\"\"\nimport random\nimport statistics\nimport time\n\nimport requests\nfrom tile_math_solution import lonlat_to_tile        # your Exercise 1 module\n\nBBOX = (4.70, 52.28, 5.10, 52.45)                     # west, south, east, north (lon/lat)\nDYNAMIC = \"http://localhost:7800/postgisftw.poi_mvt/{z}/{x}/{y}.pbf\"\nSTATIC = \"http://localhost:3000/poi/{z}/{x}/{y}\"      # Martin serving poi.mbtiles\nrandom.seed(7)\n\ndef sample(n, zooms):\n    for _ in range(n):\n        z = random.choice(zooms)\n        lon, lat = random.uniform(BBOX[0], BBOX[2]), random.uniform(BBOX[1], BBOX[3])\n        x, y = lonlat_to_tile(lon, lat, z)\n        yield z, x, y\n\ndef run(url_tpl, tiles):\n    lat_ms, max_bytes = {}, {}\n    with requests.Session() as s:\n        for z, x, y in tiles:\n            t0 = time.perf_counter()\n            r = s.get(url_tpl.format(z=z, x=x, y=y), timeout=30)\n            dt = (time.perf_counter() - t0) * 1000\n            r.raise_for_status()\n            lat_ms.setdefault(z, []).append(dt)\n            max_bytes[z] = max(max_bytes.get(z, 0), len(r.content))   # decoded size; see Content-Length for wire size\n    for z in sorted(lat_ms):\n        v = sorted(lat_ms[z])\n        p95 = v[min(len(v) - 1, int(0.95 * len(v)))]\n        print(f\"z{z:>2} n={len(v):>3} p50={statistics.median(v):7.1f} ms p95={p95:7.1f} ms max={max_bytes[z]:>8} B\")\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    tiles = list(sample(300, [8, 9, 10, 11, 12, 13, 14]))\n    print(\"dynamic:\"); run(DYNAMIC, tiles)\n    print(\"static:\");  run(STATIC, tiles)\n```\n\n`purge.py`:\n\n```python\n\"\"\"Which tiles must be invalidated after an edit? (Exercise 3 / 4)\"\"\"\nimport math\n\nPAD = 64 / 4096          # the MVT buffer (64 of 4096 units) as a fraction of a tile\n\ndef _frac(lon: float, lat: float, z: int) -> tuple[float, float]:\n    n = 2 ** z\n    x = (lon + 180.0) / 360.0 * n\n    y = (1.0 - math.asinh(math.tan(math.radians(lat))) / math.pi) / 2.0 * n\n    return x, y\n\ndef tiles_to_purge(west, south, east, north, zmin, zmax):\n    \"\"\"Tiles whose content (including the buffer) can change when something inside the bbox changes.\"\"\"\n    for z in range(zmin, zmax + 1):\n        n = 2 ** z\n        xw, yn = _frac(west, north, z)\n        xe, ys = _frac(east, south, z)\n        for x in range(max(0, math.floor(xw - PAD)), min(n - 1, math.floor(xe + PAD)) + 1):\n            for y in range(max(0, math.floor(yn - PAD)), min(n - 1, math.floor(ys + PAD)) + 1):\n                yield (z, x, y)\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    one = list(tiles_to_purge(4.9, 52.37, 4.9, 52.37, 12, 12))\n    assert one == [(12, 2103, 1346)], one                 # interior point: a single tile\n    edge_lon = 4.833984375 + 0.0001                       # just inside the west edge of tile 12/2103/1346\n    two = list(tiles_to_purge(edge_lon, 52.37, edge_lon, 52.37, 12, 12))\n    assert (12, 2102, 1346) in two and (12, 2103, 1346) in two, two     # buffer reaches the neighbour\n    print(len(list(tiles_to_purge(4.9, 52.37, 4.9, 52.37, 8, 16))), \"tiles for one point, z8-z16\")\n    print(\"ok\")\n```\n\nThe padding `64 / 4096` mirrors the tile buffer: an edit within that distance of a tile edge changes the *neighbouring* tile's buffered content, so the neighbour must be refreshed too. A single interior point at z = 8…16 touches 11 tiles (one per zoom plus two buffer neighbours — the exact number is what `purge.py` prints).\n\n**6. Heatmap → points hand-over**\n\n```js\nmap.addSource('poi', { type: 'vector', tiles: ['http://localhost:7800/postgisftw.poi_mvt/{z}/{x}/{y}.pbf'], maxzoom: 16 });\nmap.addLayer({ id: 'poi-heat', type: 'heatmap', source: 'poi', 'source-layer': 'poi', maxzoom: 13,\n  paint: { 'heatmap-weight': ['interpolate', ['linear'], ['get', 'point_count'], 1, 0.05, 200, 1],\n           'heatmap-radius': ['interpolate', ['linear'], ['zoom'], 8, 8, 12, 24] } });\nmap.addLayer({ id: 'poi-points', type: 'circle', source: 'poi', 'source-layer': 'poi', minzoom: 13,\n  paint: { 'circle-radius': 3, 'circle-color': '#1565c0' } });\n```\n\nBelow z = 13 the tiles contain aggregated cells (weight = `point_count`); from z = 13 they contain real points. Matching the heatmap's `maxzoom` with the function's switch zoom keeps the data and the symbology consistent.\n\n**Interpretation of results (what you should find).**\n- Static (Tippecanoe/Martin) tiles answer in a nearly constant, small time at every zoom, and Tippecanoe enforces the tile-size budget by construction.\n- Dynamic tiles are fast at high zoom (small index-assisted scans) but become slow at the lowest benchmarked zooms because a single tile touches a large share of the million rows. Exact numbers depend on your hardware.\n- Recommended pattern (justify with *your* measurements): **hybrid** — serve low zooms from pre-aggregated or pre-generated tiles (they change rarely and are expensive to compute) and high zooms dynamically (they are cheap and reflect edits immediately).\n\n","lineStart":1424,"lineEnd":1589,"html":"<h2>Solution 3 — one million points</h2>\n<p><strong>1. Reproducible data</strong></p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-sql\">SELECT setseed(0.42);\nCREATE TABLE city.poi AS\nSELECT g AS id,\n       ST_SetSRID(ST_MakePoint(4.70 + random() * 0.40, 52.28 + random() * 0.17), 4326) AS geom,\n       (ARRAY[&#39;cafe&#39;,&#39;shop&#39;,&#39;school&#39;,&#39;park&#39;])[1 + floor(random() * 4)::int] AS kind\nFROM generate_series(1, 1000000) AS g;\nALTER TABLE city.poi ADD PRIMARY KEY (id);\nCREATE INDEX poi_geom_gix ON city.poi USING gist (geom);\nANALYZE city.poi;\nGRANT SELECT ON city.poi TO tile_reader;</code></pre><p><strong>2. Why aggregate? Estimate points per tile.</strong> The bbox is about 27.2 km × 18.8 km ≈ 511 km², so the density is ≈ 1 957 points/km². Ground tile width at this latitude is <code>tile width × cos φ</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"source-table\" role=\"region\" tabindex=\"0\" aria-label=\"Table in Solution 3 — one million points\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th align=\"right\">z</th>\n<th align=\"right\">Ground tile width (km)</th>\n<th align=\"right\">Points per tile (≈)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"right\">10</td>\n<td align=\"right\">23.9</td>\n<td align=\"right\">1 117 000 (the whole dataset)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"right\">11</td>\n<td align=\"right\">11.95</td>\n<td align=\"right\">279 000</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"right\">12</td>\n<td align=\"right\">5.97</td>\n<td align=\"right\">70 000</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"right\">13</td>\n<td align=\"right\">2.99</td>\n<td align=\"right\">17 500</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"right\">14</td>\n<td align=\"right\">1.49</td>\n<td align=\"right\">4 400</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div><p>Even at an optimistic ~10 bytes per point, a z = 11 tile would be ≈ 2.8 MB — far beyond the 500 KB budget (this is an order-of-magnitude estimate; measure real sizes). Hence: aggregate below z = 13, individual points from z = 13.</p>\n<p><strong>3. Hybrid tile function</strong></p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-sql\">CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION postgisftw.poi_mvt(z integer, x integer, y integer)\nRETURNS bytea LANGUAGE sql STABLE PARALLEL SAFE AS $$\n  WITH env AS (\n    SELECT ST_TileEnvelope(z, x, y) AS g3857, 156543.03392804097 / (2 ^ z) AS res\n  ),\n  agg AS (                                                   -- z &lt; 13: ~32-pixel grid cells with counts\n    SELECT ST_SnapToGrid(ST_Transform(p.geom, 3857), env.res * 32) AS cell, count(*) AS n\n    FROM city.poi p, env\n    WHERE z &lt; 13 AND p.geom &amp;&amp; ST_Transform(env.g3857, 4326)\n    GROUP BY 1\n  ),\n  mvtgeom AS (\n    SELECT NULL::bigint AS id, agg.n AS point_count,\n           ST_AsMVTGeom(agg.cell, env.g3857, 4096, 64, true) AS geom\n    FROM agg, env\n    UNION ALL\n    SELECT p.id, 1 AS point_count,\n           ST_AsMVTGeom(ST_Transform(p.geom, 3857), env.g3857, 4096, 64, true)\n    FROM city.poi p, env\n    WHERE z &gt;= 13 AND p.geom &amp;&amp; ST_Transform(env.g3857, 4326)\n  )\n  SELECT COALESCE(ST_AsMVT(mvtgeom.*, &#39;poi&#39;, 4096, &#39;geom&#39;, &#39;id&#39;), &#39;&#39;::bytea) FROM mvtgeom;\n$$;\nGRANT EXECUTE ON FUNCTION postgisftw.poi_mvt(integer, integer, integer) TO tile_reader;</code></pre><p>The predicates <code>z &lt; 13</code> / <code>z &gt;= 13</code> depend only on the function parameter, so PostgreSQL can skip the branch that does not apply (look for a <em>One-Time Filter</em> in the plan). Cells are snapped to a global grid, so a cell that straddles a tile edge may appear with partial counts in two tiles (pitfall 12).</p>\n<p><strong>4. Pre-generated alternative</strong></p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-bash\">ogr2ogr -f GeoJSONSeq poi.geojsonl PG:&quot;host=localhost dbname=gis user=postgres password=postgres&quot; city.poi\ntippecanoe -o poi.mbtiles -l poi -zg --drop-densest-as-needed --force poi.geojsonl\nmartin poi.mbtiles                         # http://localhost:3000/poi/{z}/{x}/{y}</code></pre><p><strong>5. Benchmark and purge helpers</strong> — <code>bench.py</code> (needs Exercise 1&#39;s module):</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">&quot;&quot;&quot;Latency / size benchmark for a tile endpoint (Exercise 3). Needs: pip install requests&quot;&quot;&quot;\nimport random\nimport statistics\nimport time\n\nimport requests\nfrom tile_math_solution import lonlat_to_tile        # your Exercise 1 module\n\nBBOX = (4.70, 52.28, 5.10, 52.45)                     # west, south, east, north (lon/lat)\nDYNAMIC = &quot;http://localhost:7800/postgisftw.poi_mvt/{z}/{x}/{y}.pbf&quot;\nSTATIC = &quot;http://localhost:3000/poi/{z}/{x}/{y}&quot;      # Martin serving poi.mbtiles\nrandom.seed(7)\n\ndef sample(n, zooms):\n    for _ in range(n):\n        z = random.choice(zooms)\n        lon, lat = random.uniform(BBOX[0], BBOX[2]), random.uniform(BBOX[1], BBOX[3])\n        x, y = lonlat_to_tile(lon, lat, z)\n        yield z, x, y\n\ndef run(url_tpl, tiles):\n    lat_ms, max_bytes = {}, {}\n    with requests.Session() as s:\n        for z, x, y in tiles:\n            t0 = time.perf_counter()\n            r = s.get(url_tpl.format(z=z, x=x, y=y), timeout=30)\n            dt = (time.perf_counter() - t0) * 1000\n            r.raise_for_status()\n            lat_ms.setdefault(z, []).append(dt)\n            max_bytes[z] = max(max_bytes.get(z, 0), len(r.content))   # decoded size; see Content-Length for wire size\n    for z in sorted(lat_ms):\n        v = sorted(lat_ms[z])\n        p95 = v[min(len(v) - 1, int(0.95 * len(v)))]\n        print(f&quot;z{z:&gt;2} n={len(v):&gt;3} p50={statistics.median(v):7.1f} ms p95={p95:7.1f} ms max={max_bytes[z]:&gt;8} B&quot;)\n\nif __name__ == &quot;__main__&quot;:\n    tiles = list(sample(300, [8, 9, 10, 11, 12, 13, 14]))\n    print(&quot;dynamic:&quot;); run(DYNAMIC, tiles)\n    print(&quot;static:&quot;);  run(STATIC, tiles)</code></pre><p><code>purge.py</code>:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">&quot;&quot;&quot;Which tiles must be invalidated after an edit? (Exercise 3 / 4)&quot;&quot;&quot;\nimport math\n\nPAD = 64 / 4096          # the MVT buffer (64 of 4096 units) as a fraction of a tile\n\ndef _frac(lon: float, lat: float, z: int) -&gt; tuple[float, float]:\n    n = 2 ** z\n    x = (lon + 180.0) / 360.0 * n\n    y = (1.0 - math.asinh(math.tan(math.radians(lat))) / math.pi) / 2.0 * n\n    return x, y\n\ndef tiles_to_purge(west, south, east, north, zmin, zmax):\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;Tiles whose content (including the buffer) can change when something inside the bbox changes.&quot;&quot;&quot;\n    for z in range(zmin, zmax + 1):\n        n = 2 ** z\n        xw, yn = _frac(west, north, z)\n        xe, ys = _frac(east, south, z)\n        for x in range(max(0, math.floor(xw - PAD)), min(n - 1, math.floor(xe + PAD)) + 1):\n            for y in range(max(0, math.floor(yn - PAD)), min(n - 1, math.floor(ys + PAD)) + 1):\n                yield (z, x, y)\n\nif __name__ == &quot;__main__&quot;:\n    one = list(tiles_to_purge(4.9, 52.37, 4.9, 52.37, 12, 12))\n    assert one == [(12, 2103, 1346)], one                 # interior point: a single tile\n    edge_lon = 4.833984375 + 0.0001                       # just inside the west edge of tile 12/2103/1346\n    two = list(tiles_to_purge(edge_lon, 52.37, edge_lon, 52.37, 12, 12))\n    assert (12, 2102, 1346) in two and (12, 2103, 1346) in two, two     # buffer reaches the neighbour\n    print(len(list(tiles_to_purge(4.9, 52.37, 4.9, 52.37, 8, 16))), &quot;tiles for one point, z8-z16&quot;)\n    print(&quot;ok&quot;)</code></pre><p>The padding <code>64 / 4096</code> mirrors the tile buffer: an edit within that distance of a tile edge changes the <em>neighbouring</em> tile&#39;s buffered content, so the neighbour must be refreshed too. A single interior point at z = 8…16 touches 11 tiles (one per zoom plus two buffer neighbours — the exact number is what <code>purge.py</code> prints).</p>\n<p><strong>6. Heatmap → points hand-over</strong></p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-js\">map.addSource(&#39;poi&#39;, { type: &#39;vector&#39;, tiles: [&#39;http://localhost:7800/postgisftw.poi_mvt/{z}/{x}/{y}.pbf&#39;], maxzoom: 16 });\nmap.addLayer({ id: &#39;poi-heat&#39;, type: &#39;heatmap&#39;, source: &#39;poi&#39;, &#39;source-layer&#39;: &#39;poi&#39;, maxzoom: 13,\n  paint: { &#39;heatmap-weight&#39;: [&#39;interpolate&#39;, [&#39;linear&#39;], [&#39;get&#39;, &#39;point_count&#39;], 1, 0.05, 200, 1],\n           &#39;heatmap-radius&#39;: [&#39;interpolate&#39;, [&#39;linear&#39;], [&#39;zoom&#39;], 8, 8, 12, 24] } });\nmap.addLayer({ id: &#39;poi-points&#39;, type: &#39;circle&#39;, source: &#39;poi&#39;, &#39;source-layer&#39;: &#39;poi&#39;, minzoom: 13,\n  paint: { &#39;circle-radius&#39;: 3, &#39;circle-color&#39;: &#39;#1565c0&#39; } });</code></pre><p>Below z = 13 the tiles contain aggregated cells (weight = <code>point_count</code>); from z = 13 they contain real points. Matching the heatmap&#39;s <code>maxzoom</code> with the function&#39;s switch zoom keeps the data and the symbology consistent.</p>\n<p><strong>Interpretation of results (what you should find).</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Static (Tippecanoe/Martin) tiles answer in a nearly constant, small time at every zoom, and Tippecanoe enforces the tile-size budget by construction.</li>\n<li>Dynamic tiles are fast at high zoom (small index-assisted scans) but become slow at the lowest benchmarked zooms because a single tile touches a large share of the million rows. Exact numbers depend on your hardware.</li>\n<li>Recommended pattern (justify with <em>your</em> measurements): <strong>hybrid</strong> — serve low zooms from pre-aggregated or pre-generated tiles (they change rarely and are expensive to compute) and high zooms dynamically (they are cheap and reflect edits immediately).</li>\n</ul>\n","sha256":"be47b99bad6975933a6c2983fc13958e12482551c193ae9522ec2fcb0cb95cd6"},{"key":"solution-4","id":"solution-4","title":"Solution 4 — secure, editable inventory (reference design)","anchor":"solution-4--secure-editable-inventory-reference-design","depth":3,"start":88200,"topics":["capstone"],"end":97070,"markdown":"### Solution 4 — secure, editable inventory (reference design)\n\n**Architecture and rules**\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n    B[\"Browser\"] --> N[\"nginx gateway\"]\n    N -- \"/tiles/ public, cached 60 s\" --> T[\"pg_tileserv as tile_reader\"]\n    N -- \"/api/ private, never cached\" --> A[\"Edit API as edit_api\"]\n    T --> P[(\"PostGIS with RLS\")]\n    A --> P\n    A -. \"after commit: compute stale tiles\" .-> N\n```\n\n| Concern | Decision |\n|---|---|\n| Public data | Only `tile_reader` serves tiles; RLS policy `USING (is_public)`; tiles are public-only, so a shared cache is safe |\n| User-specific data | Only through `/api/`, with `Cache-Control: private, no-store` |\n| Identity | JWT verified in the API; `owner_org` and `sub` from claims, passed with transaction-local `set_config` |\n| Concurrency | `ETag` = row version; `If-Match` required on `PUT`/`PATCH`/`DELETE`; `412` on mismatch, `428` if missing |\n| Freshness | Short TTL (60 s) on public tiles + versioned tile URL (`?v=`) in the editing client; `tiles_to_purge` drives purging if you add a CDN/Varnish with a purge API (open-source nginx has no selective purge without extra modules) |\n\n**API routes shaped like OGC API – Features Part 4**\n\n| Method and path | Meaning | Success | Failure |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `POST /collections/poi/items` | Create from a GeoJSON Feature | `201`, `Location`, `ETag` | `400` invalid geometry, `401` |\n| `PUT /collections/poi/items/{id}` | Replace | `200`/`204`, new `ETag` | `404`, `412` |\n| `PATCH /collections/poi/items/{id}` | Partial update | `200`, new `ETag` | `404`, `412`, `428` |\n| `DELETE /collections/poi/items/{id}` | Delete | `204` | `404`, `412` |\n\nKey handler (extends the `session()` helper of Section 5.6; the table gets a `version` column and `CHECK (geometrytype(geom) = 'POINT' AND ST_IsValid(geom))`):\n\n```python\nimport json\nfrom fastapi import HTTPException, Request, Response\n\n@app.patch(\"/collections/poi/items/{item_id}\")\ndef patch_item(item_id: int, feature: dict, request: Request, response: Response,\n               c: Claims = Depends(claims)):\n    raw = request.headers.get(\"if-match\")\n    if raw is None:\n        raise HTTPException(428, \"If-Match header required\")\n    try:\n        version = int(raw.strip('\"'))\n    except ValueError:\n        raise HTTPException(400, \"malformed ETag\")\n    props, geom = feature.get(\"properties\", {}), feature.get(\"geometry\")\n    with session(c) as conn:\n        row = conn.execute(\"\"\"UPDATE city.poi SET\n                 kind    = COALESCE(%(kind)s, kind),\n                 geom    = COALESCE(ST_SetSRID(ST_GeomFromGeoJSON(%(geom)s::text), 4326), geom),\n                 version = version + 1\n               WHERE id = %(id)s AND version = %(version)s\n               RETURNING id, version\"\"\",\n            {\"kind\": props.get(\"kind\"), \"geom\": json.dumps(geom) if geom else None,\n             \"id\": item_id, \"version\": version}).fetchone()\n        exists = None if row else conn.execute(\"SELECT 1 FROM city.poi WHERE id = %s\", (item_id,)).fetchone()\n    if row is None:\n        raise HTTPException(412 if exists else 404, \"precondition failed\" if exists else \"not found\")\n    response.headers[\"ETag\"] = f'\"{row[\"version\"]}\"'\n    response.headers[\"Cache-Control\"] = \"private, no-store\"\n    return row\n```\n\nAdd `expose_headers=[\"ETag\"]` to the `CORSMiddleware` call — browsers hide non-safelisted response headers from cross-origin scripts, so the client could not read the new `ETag` otherwise.\n\n**Gateway (nginx excerpt)**\n\n```nginx\nproxy_cache_path /var/cache/nginx/tiles levels=1:2 keys_zone=tiles:50m max_size=2g inactive=1h use_temp_path=off;\n\nserver {\n  listen 8080;\n  location /tiles/ {                          # PUBLIC tiles only: a shared cache is safe here\n    proxy_pass http://tiles:7800/;\n    proxy_cache tiles;\n    proxy_cache_valid 200 60s;\n    add_header X-Cache $upstream_cache_status always;\n  }\n  location /api/ {                            # user-specific: never cached\n    proxy_pass http://edit:8000/;\n    proxy_no_cache 1;\n    proxy_cache_bypass 1;\n    add_header Cache-Control \"private, no-store\" always;\n  }\n}\n```\n\n**Editor UI with OpenLayers** (send EPSG:4326 to the API, keep the ETag per feature):\n\n```js\nimport GeoJSON from 'ol/format/GeoJSON.js';\nconst fmt = new GeoJSON();\nconst toApi = (f) => fmt.writeFeatureObject(f, { dataProjection: 'EPSG:4326', featureProjection: 'EPSG:3857' });\n\nmodify.on('modifyend', async (e) => {\n  for (const f of e.features.getArray()) {\n    const r = await fetch(`${API}/collections/poi/items/${f.getId()}`, {\n      method: 'PATCH',\n      headers: { ...auth, 'If-Match': f.get('etag') },\n      body: JSON.stringify({ geometry: toApi(f).geometry })\n    });\n    if (r.status === 412) { alert('Changed by someone else; reloading.'); await reloadFeatures(); }\n    else if (r.ok) f.set('etag', r.headers.get('ETag'));\n  }\n});\n```\n\n**Tests** (`pytest`, run against the compose stack):\n\n```python\nimport time, threading, jwt, requests\n\nAPI, SECRET = \"http://localhost:8080/api\", \"dev-only-change-me\"\n\ndef hdr(org, sub=\"tester\"):\n    tok = jwt.encode({\"sub\": sub, \"org\": org, \"exp\": int(time.time()) + 600}, SECRET, algorithm=\"HS256\")\n    return {\"Authorization\": f\"Bearer {tok}\", \"Content-Type\": \"application/json\"}\n\ndef feat(kind=\"cafe\", lon=4.9, lat=52.37):\n    return {\"type\": \"Feature\", \"geometry\": {\"type\": \"Point\", \"coordinates\": [lon, lat]}, \"properties\": {\"kind\": kind}}\n\ndef create(org):\n    r = requests.post(f\"{API}/collections/poi/items\", headers=hdr(org), json=feat())\n    assert r.status_code == 201, r.text\n    return r.json()[\"id\"], r.headers[\"ETag\"]\n\ndef test_missing_token_is_401():\n    assert requests.get(f\"{API}/collections/poi/items\").status_code == 401\n\ndef test_cross_org_patch_is_404():\n    pid, etag = create(\"parks\")\n    r = requests.patch(f\"{API}/collections/poi/items/{pid}\", headers={**hdr(\"harbor\"), \"If-Match\": etag},\n                       json={\"properties\": {\"kind\": \"x\"}})\n    assert r.status_code == 404                                  # same as a missing id\n\ndef test_stale_etag_is_412():\n    pid, etag = create(\"parks\")\n    url = f\"{API}/collections/poi/items/{pid}\"\n    assert requests.patch(url, headers={**hdr(\"parks\"), \"If-Match\": etag}, json={\"properties\": {\"kind\": \"a\"}}).status_code == 200\n    assert requests.patch(url, headers={**hdr(\"parks\"), \"If-Match\": etag}, json={\"properties\": {\"kind\": \"b\"}}).status_code == 412\n\ndef test_forged_owner_in_body_is_ignored():\n    f = feat(); f[\"properties\"][\"owner_org\"] = \"harbor\"\n    r = requests.post(f\"{API}/collections/poi/items\", headers=hdr(\"parks\"), json=f)\n    pid = r.json()[\"id\"]\n    assert requests.get(f\"{API}/collections/poi/items/{pid}\", headers=hdr(\"harbor\")).status_code == 404\n\ndef test_concurrent_patch_exactly_one_wins():\n    pid, etag = create(\"parks\")\n    url, codes = f\"{API}/collections/poi/items/{pid}\", []\n    def go(k): codes.append(requests.patch(url, headers={**hdr(\"parks\"), \"If-Match\": etag}, json={\"properties\": {\"kind\": k}}).status_code)\n    ts = [threading.Thread(target=go, args=(k,)) for k in (\"a\", \"b\")]\n    [t.start() for t in ts]; [t.join() for t in ts]\n    assert sorted(codes) == [200, 412]\n```\n\nWrite at least three more: *delete across organisations is `404`*; *expired token is `401`*; *a public tile that contains a known non-public POI decodes without it* (decode with `mapbox_vector_tile`, three tiles). A cache test: request the same `/api/…` URL as `parks` then `harbor` through the gateway and assert the second response contains no `parks` data and carries `Cache-Control: private, no-store`.\n\n**Threat model (excerpt)**\n\n| Threat | Impact | Mitigation | Test |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Forged or expired JWT | Unauthorised edits | Signature + `exp` verification; short lifetimes; asymmetric keys in production | `test_missing_token_is_401`, expired-token test |\n| Client sets `owner_org` | Cross-tenant writes | Owner from token; RLS `WITH CHECK` | `test_forged_owner…` |\n| Cross-tenant read/update via guessed ids | Data disclosure | RLS; `404` for hidden rows | `test_cross_org_patch_is_404` |\n| Shared cache serves private data | Disclosure | Public-only tiles in shared cache; private API uncached | cache test |\n| Lost updates | Data corruption | `If-Match`/version | concurrency test |\n| Superuser or `BYPASSRLS` role used by a service | Total RLS bypass | Dedicated least-privilege roles; `FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY` | role audit query on `pg_roles` |\n| SQL injection | Full compromise | Bound parameters only; typed function arguments | code review + fuzz test |\n\n**How to verify the whole system.** Run the stack, execute the test suite, run `bench.py` against `/tiles/` (cached) and directly against the tile server (uncached) to quantify the cache benefit, perform an edit in the UI, and watch the edited point appear within the freshness target (60 s TTL, or immediately with the versioned URL in the editing session).\n\n---\n\n","lineStart":1590,"lineEnd":1765,"html":"<h2>Solution 4 — secure, editable inventory (reference design)</h2>\n<p><strong>Architecture and rules</strong></p>\n<figure class=\"source-diagram\"><a href=\"assets/figures/phase5-source-solution-4.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/figures/phase5-source-solution-4.svg\" alt=\"Solution 4 — secure, editable inventory (reference design) — source diagram\" loading=\"lazy\"></a><figcaption>Source diagram · <a href=\"assets/figures/phase5-source-solution-4.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Enlarge ↗</a></figcaption></figure><details class=\"source-code\"><summary>Mermaid definition</summary><pre><code class=\"language-mermaid\">flowchart LR\n    B[&quot;Browser&quot;] --&gt; N[&quot;nginx gateway&quot;]\n    N -- &quot;/tiles/ public, cached 60 s&quot; --&gt; T[&quot;pg_tileserv as tile_reader&quot;]\n    N -- &quot;/api/ private, never cached&quot; --&gt; A[&quot;Edit API as edit_api&quot;]\n    T --&gt; P[(&quot;PostGIS with RLS&quot;)]\n    A --&gt; P\n    A -. &quot;after commit: compute stale tiles&quot; .-&gt; N</code></pre></details><div class=\"source-table\" role=\"region\" tabindex=\"0\" aria-label=\"Table in Solution 4 — secure, editable inventory (reference design)\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Concern</th>\n<th>Decision</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Public data</td>\n<td>Only <code>tile_reader</code> serves tiles; RLS policy <code>USING (is_public)</code>; tiles are public-only, so a shared cache is safe</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>User-specific data</td>\n<td>Only through <code>/api/</code>, with <code>Cache-Control: private, no-store</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Identity</td>\n<td>JWT verified in the API; <code>owner_org</code> and <code>sub</code> from claims, passed with transaction-local <code>set_config</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Concurrency</td>\n<td><code>ETag</code> = row version; <code>If-Match</code> required on <code>PUT</code>/<code>PATCH</code>/<code>DELETE</code>; <code>412</code> on mismatch, <code>428</code> if missing</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Freshness</td>\n<td>Short TTL (60 s) on public tiles + versioned tile URL (<code>?v=</code>) in the editing client; <code>tiles_to_purge</code> drives purging if you add a CDN/Varnish with a purge API (open-source nginx has no selective purge without extra modules)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div><p><strong>API routes shaped like OGC API – Features Part 4</strong></p>\n<div class=\"source-table\" role=\"region\" tabindex=\"0\" aria-label=\"Table in Solution 4 — secure, editable inventory (reference design)\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Method and path</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n<th>Success</th>\n<th>Failure</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>POST /collections/poi/items</code></td>\n<td>Create from a GeoJSON Feature</td>\n<td><code>201</code>, <code>Location</code>, <code>ETag</code></td>\n<td><code>400</code> invalid geometry, <code>401</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>PUT /collections/poi/items/{id}</code></td>\n<td>Replace</td>\n<td><code>200</code>/<code>204</code>, new <code>ETag</code></td>\n<td><code>404</code>, <code>412</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>PATCH /collections/poi/items/{id}</code></td>\n<td>Partial update</td>\n<td><code>200</code>, new <code>ETag</code></td>\n<td><code>404</code>, <code>412</code>, <code>428</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>DELETE /collections/poi/items/{id}</code></td>\n<td>Delete</td>\n<td><code>204</code></td>\n<td><code>404</code>, <code>412</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div><p>Key handler (extends the <code>session()</code> helper of Section 5.6; the table gets a <code>version</code> column and <code>CHECK (geometrytype(geom) = &#39;POINT&#39; AND ST_IsValid(geom))</code>):</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">import json\nfrom fastapi import HTTPException, Request, Response\n\n@app.patch(&quot;/collections/poi/items/{item_id}&quot;)\ndef patch_item(item_id: int, feature: dict, request: Request, response: Response,\n               c: Claims = Depends(claims)):\n    raw = request.headers.get(&quot;if-match&quot;)\n    if raw is None:\n        raise HTTPException(428, &quot;If-Match header required&quot;)\n    try:\n        version = int(raw.strip(&#39;&quot;&#39;))\n    except ValueError:\n        raise HTTPException(400, &quot;malformed ETag&quot;)\n    props, geom = feature.get(&quot;properties&quot;, {}), feature.get(&quot;geometry&quot;)\n    with session(c) as conn:\n        row = conn.execute(&quot;&quot;&quot;UPDATE city.poi SET\n                 kind    = COALESCE(%(kind)s, kind),\n                 geom    = COALESCE(ST_SetSRID(ST_GeomFromGeoJSON(%(geom)s::text), 4326), geom),\n                 version = version + 1\n               WHERE id = %(id)s AND version = %(version)s\n               RETURNING id, version&quot;&quot;&quot;,\n            {&quot;kind&quot;: props.get(&quot;kind&quot;), &quot;geom&quot;: json.dumps(geom) if geom else None,\n             &quot;id&quot;: item_id, &quot;version&quot;: version}).fetchone()\n        exists = None if row else conn.execute(&quot;SELECT 1 FROM city.poi WHERE id = %s&quot;, (item_id,)).fetchone()\n    if row is None:\n        raise HTTPException(412 if exists else 404, &quot;precondition failed&quot; if exists else &quot;not found&quot;)\n    response.headers[&quot;ETag&quot;] = f&#39;&quot;{row[&quot;version&quot;]}&quot;&#39;\n    response.headers[&quot;Cache-Control&quot;] = &quot;private, no-store&quot;\n    return row</code></pre><p>Add <code>expose_headers=[&quot;ETag&quot;]</code> to the <code>CORSMiddleware</code> call — browsers hide non-safelisted response headers from cross-origin scripts, so the client could not read the new <code>ETag</code> otherwise.</p>\n<p><strong>Gateway (nginx excerpt)</strong></p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-nginx\">proxy_cache_path /var/cache/nginx/tiles levels=1:2 keys_zone=tiles:50m max_size=2g inactive=1h use_temp_path=off;\n\nserver {\n  listen 8080;\n  location /tiles/ {                          # PUBLIC tiles only: a shared cache is safe here\n    proxy_pass http://tiles:7800/;\n    proxy_cache tiles;\n    proxy_cache_valid 200 60s;\n    add_header X-Cache $upstream_cache_status always;\n  }\n  location /api/ {                            # user-specific: never cached\n    proxy_pass http://edit:8000/;\n    proxy_no_cache 1;\n    proxy_cache_bypass 1;\n    add_header Cache-Control &quot;private, no-store&quot; always;\n  }\n}</code></pre><p><strong>Editor UI with OpenLayers</strong> (send EPSG:4326 to the API, keep the ETag per feature):</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-js\">import GeoJSON from &#39;ol/format/GeoJSON.js&#39;;\nconst fmt = new GeoJSON();\nconst toApi = (f) =&gt; fmt.writeFeatureObject(f, { dataProjection: &#39;EPSG:4326&#39;, featureProjection: &#39;EPSG:3857&#39; });\n\nmodify.on(&#39;modifyend&#39;, async (e) =&gt; {\n  for (const f of e.features.getArray()) {\n    const r = await fetch(`${API}/collections/poi/items/${f.getId()}`, {\n      method: &#39;PATCH&#39;,\n      headers: { ...auth, &#39;If-Match&#39;: f.get(&#39;etag&#39;) },\n      body: JSON.stringify({ geometry: toApi(f).geometry })\n    });\n    if (r.status === 412) { alert(&#39;Changed by someone else; reloading.&#39;); await reloadFeatures(); }\n    else if (r.ok) f.set(&#39;etag&#39;, r.headers.get(&#39;ETag&#39;));\n  }\n});</code></pre><p><strong>Tests</strong> (<code>pytest</code>, run against the compose stack):</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">import time, threading, jwt, requests\n\nAPI, SECRET = &quot;http://localhost:8080/api&quot;, &quot;dev-only-change-me&quot;\n\ndef hdr(org, sub=&quot;tester&quot;):\n    tok = jwt.encode({&quot;sub&quot;: sub, &quot;org&quot;: org, &quot;exp&quot;: int(time.time()) + 600}, SECRET, algorithm=&quot;HS256&quot;)\n    return {&quot;Authorization&quot;: f&quot;Bearer {tok}&quot;, &quot;Content-Type&quot;: &quot;application/json&quot;}\n\ndef feat(kind=&quot;cafe&quot;, lon=4.9, lat=52.37):\n    return {&quot;type&quot;: &quot;Feature&quot;, &quot;geometry&quot;: {&quot;type&quot;: &quot;Point&quot;, &quot;coordinates&quot;: [lon, lat]}, &quot;properties&quot;: {&quot;kind&quot;: kind}}\n\ndef create(org):\n    r = requests.post(f&quot;{API}/collections/poi/items&quot;, headers=hdr(org), json=feat())\n    assert r.status_code == 201, r.text\n    return r.json()[&quot;id&quot;], r.headers[&quot;ETag&quot;]\n\ndef test_missing_token_is_401():\n    assert requests.get(f&quot;{API}/collections/poi/items&quot;).status_code == 401\n\ndef test_cross_org_patch_is_404():\n    pid, etag = create(&quot;parks&quot;)\n    r = requests.patch(f&quot;{API}/collections/poi/items/{pid}&quot;, headers={**hdr(&quot;harbor&quot;), &quot;If-Match&quot;: etag},\n                       json={&quot;properties&quot;: {&quot;kind&quot;: &quot;x&quot;}})\n    assert r.status_code == 404                                  # same as a missing id\n\ndef test_stale_etag_is_412():\n    pid, etag = create(&quot;parks&quot;)\n    url = f&quot;{API}/collections/poi/items/{pid}&quot;\n    assert requests.patch(url, headers={**hdr(&quot;parks&quot;), &quot;If-Match&quot;: etag}, json={&quot;properties&quot;: {&quot;kind&quot;: &quot;a&quot;}}).status_code == 200\n    assert requests.patch(url, headers={**hdr(&quot;parks&quot;), &quot;If-Match&quot;: etag}, json={&quot;properties&quot;: {&quot;kind&quot;: &quot;b&quot;}}).status_code == 412\n\ndef test_forged_owner_in_body_is_ignored():\n    f = feat(); f[&quot;properties&quot;][&quot;owner_org&quot;] = &quot;harbor&quot;\n    r = requests.post(f&quot;{API}/collections/poi/items&quot;, headers=hdr(&quot;parks&quot;), json=f)\n    pid = r.json()[&quot;id&quot;]\n    assert requests.get(f&quot;{API}/collections/poi/items/{pid}&quot;, headers=hdr(&quot;harbor&quot;)).status_code == 404\n\ndef test_concurrent_patch_exactly_one_wins():\n    pid, etag = create(&quot;parks&quot;)\n    url, codes = f&quot;{API}/collections/poi/items/{pid}&quot;, []\n    def go(k): codes.append(requests.patch(url, headers={**hdr(&quot;parks&quot;), &quot;If-Match&quot;: etag}, json={&quot;properties&quot;: {&quot;kind&quot;: k}}).status_code)\n    ts = [threading.Thread(target=go, args=(k,)) for k in (&quot;a&quot;, &quot;b&quot;)]\n    [t.start() for t in ts]; [t.join() for t in ts]\n    assert sorted(codes) == [200, 412]</code></pre><p>Write at least three more: <em>delete across organisations is <code>404</code></em>; <em>expired token is <code>401</code></em>; <em>a public tile that contains a known non-public POI decodes without it</em> (decode with <code>mapbox_vector_tile</code>, three tiles). A cache test: request the same <code>/api/…</code> URL as <code>parks</code> then <code>harbor</code> through the gateway and assert the second response contains no <code>parks</code> data and carries <code>Cache-Control: private, no-store</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Threat model (excerpt)</strong></p>\n<div class=\"source-table\" role=\"region\" tabindex=\"0\" aria-label=\"Table in Solution 4 — secure, editable inventory (reference design)\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Threat</th>\n<th>Impact</th>\n<th>Mitigation</th>\n<th>Test</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Forged or expired JWT</td>\n<td>Unauthorised edits</td>\n<td>Signature + <code>exp</code> verification; short lifetimes; asymmetric keys in production</td>\n<td><code>test_missing_token_is_401</code>, expired-token test</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Client sets <code>owner_org</code></td>\n<td>Cross-tenant writes</td>\n<td>Owner from token; RLS <code>WITH CHECK</code></td>\n<td><code>test_forged_owner…</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cross-tenant read/update via guessed ids</td>\n<td>Data disclosure</td>\n<td>RLS; <code>404</code> for hidden rows</td>\n<td><code>test_cross_org_patch_is_404</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Shared cache serves private data</td>\n<td>Disclosure</td>\n<td>Public-only tiles in shared cache; private API uncached</td>\n<td>cache test</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Lost updates</td>\n<td>Data corruption</td>\n<td><code>If-Match</code>/version</td>\n<td>concurrency test</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Superuser or <code>BYPASSRLS</code> role used by a service</td>\n<td>Total RLS bypass</td>\n<td>Dedicated least-privilege roles; <code>FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY</code></td>\n<td>role audit query on <code>pg_roles</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>SQL injection</td>\n<td>Full compromise</td>\n<td>Bound parameters only; typed function arguments</td>\n<td>code review + fuzz test</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div><p><strong>How to verify the whole system.</strong> Run the stack, execute the test suite, run <code>bench.py</code> against <code>/tiles/</code> (cached) and directly against the tile server (uncached) to quantify the cache benefit, perform an edit in the UI, and watch the edited point appear within the freshness target (60 s TTL, or immediately with the versioned URL in the editing session).</p>\n<hr>\n","sha256":"bb3ca8ceb2b7c41eb7544540a585a0216b6f6397eed9835f1dd326dcf7b074dd"},{"key":"9","id":"9","title":"9. Summary, glossary, and further reading","anchor":"9-summary-glossary-and-further-reading","depth":2,"start":97070,"topics":["capstone"],"end":97116,"markdown":"## 9. Summary, glossary, and further reading\n\n","lineStart":1766,"lineEnd":1767,"html":"<h2>9. Summary, glossary, and further reading</h2>\n","sha256":"08231d14abf10cfe59854dd298a926412973c0368a48fc152cd0f7923aede4a0"},{"key":"9.1","id":"9-1","title":"9.1 Key takeaways","anchor":"91-key-takeaways","depth":3,"start":97116,"topics":["capstone"],"end":98730,"markdown":"### 9.1 Key takeaways\n\n1. **Tiles for display, features for query and edit.** Vector tiles are small, fast and client-styled but quantised and simplified; the authoritative geometry stays in PostGIS and is reached through a feature API.\n2. **Web Mercator tile math is simple and worth knowing cold:** `n = 2^z`, `x = ⌊(λ+180)/360·n⌋`, `y = ⌊(1 − asinh(tan φ)/π)/2·n⌋`, resolution `156 543.034 / 2^z` m/px at the equator, scaled by `cos φ` elsewhere.\n3. **PostGIS generates MVT directly** (`ST_TileEnvelope` → `ST_AsMVTGeom` → `ST_AsMVT`); filter in the column's SRID so the GiST index is used.\n4. **Performance is a budget problem:** bytes and features per tile, simplification at ≈ 1 pixel, LOD rules, aggregation, pre-generation (Tippecanoe/tilemaker, MBTiles/PMTiles) and HTTP/CDN caching.\n5. **Pick servers by role:** pg_tileserv/Martin for tiles, pg_featureserv for read-only OGC API – Features, GeoServer/MapServer/QGIS Server for classic OGC services and desktop-styled maps, GeoWebCache for raster tile caching.\n6. **Write paths need their own service** (or WFS-T): validation, authorization, optimistic concurrency and audit belong there. OGC API – Features Part 4 describes the REST shape, but check its status and your server's support first.\n7. **Security is layered:** gateway for authentication and layer rules, PostgreSQL RLS for rows, constraints for validity — and every cache is a security boundary.\n8. **Choose the library by need:** MapLibre for vector tiles and WebGL smoothness, OpenLayers for standards and editing, Leaflet for simplicity, Cesium for 3D, deck.gl for massive data overlays.\n\n","lineStart":1768,"lineEnd":1778,"html":"<h2>9.1 Key takeaways</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Tiles for display, features for query and edit.</strong> Vector tiles are small, fast and client-styled but quantised and simplified; the authoritative geometry stays in PostGIS and is reached through a feature API.</li>\n<li><strong>Web Mercator tile math is simple and worth knowing cold:</strong> <code>n = 2^z</code>, <code>x = ⌊(λ+180)/360·n⌋</code>, <code>y = ⌊(1 − asinh(tan φ)/π)/2·n⌋</code>, resolution <code>156 543.034 / 2^z</code> m/px at the equator, scaled by <code>cos φ</code> elsewhere.</li>\n<li><strong>PostGIS generates MVT directly</strong> (<code>ST_TileEnvelope</code> → <code>ST_AsMVTGeom</code> → <code>ST_AsMVT</code>); filter in the column&#39;s SRID so the GiST index is used.</li>\n<li><strong>Performance is a budget problem:</strong> bytes and features per tile, simplification at ≈ 1 pixel, LOD rules, aggregation, pre-generation (Tippecanoe/tilemaker, MBTiles/PMTiles) and HTTP/CDN caching.</li>\n<li><strong>Pick servers by role:</strong> pg_tileserv/Martin for tiles, pg_featureserv for read-only OGC API – Features, GeoServer/MapServer/QGIS Server for classic OGC services and desktop-styled maps, GeoWebCache for raster tile caching.</li>\n<li><strong>Write paths need their own service</strong> (or WFS-T): validation, authorization, optimistic concurrency and audit belong there. OGC API – Features Part 4 describes the REST shape, but check its status and your server&#39;s support first.</li>\n<li><strong>Security is layered:</strong> gateway for authentication and layer rules, PostgreSQL RLS for rows, constraints for validity — and every cache is a security boundary.</li>\n<li><strong>Choose the library by need:</strong> MapLibre for vector tiles and WebGL smoothness, OpenLayers for standards and editing, Leaflet for simplicity, Cesium for 3D, deck.gl for massive data overlays.</li>\n</ol>\n","sha256":"09aa107f3a68625a21bd8b54683049ce11fb7673621e256bdb90fb2c07d68f9b"},{"key":"9.2","id":"9-2","title":"9.2 Glossary","anchor":"92-glossary","depth":3,"start":98730,"topics":["capstone"],"end":100254,"markdown":"### 9.2 Glossary\n\n| Term | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| **Buffer (tile)** | Extra margin of geometry kept beyond the tile edge to avoid seams |\n| **Cluster** | Aggregate marker representing several nearby points |\n| **CQL2** | Common Query Language 2, the filter language used by OGC API Part 3 |\n| **CRS84** | WGS 84 with lon,lat axis order (the default CRS of OGC API – Features) |\n| **Extent (MVT)** | Size of the tile-local integer grid (default 4096) |\n| **GeoWebCache** | Tile cache/proxy for WMS layers, embedded in GeoServer |\n| **GiST** | Generalised Search Tree, PostgreSQL index type used for geometries |\n| **LOD** | Level of detail: less detail at smaller scales |\n| **Martin** | Rust tile server for PostGIS, MBTiles and PMTiles |\n| **MBTiles** | SQLite container format for tiles |\n| **MVT** | Mapbox Vector Tile, a Protobuf-encoded vector tile format |\n| **Optimistic locking** | Detecting conflicting edits by comparing a version/ETag instead of holding locks |\n| **PMTiles** | Single-file tile archive designed for static hosting with range requests |\n| **RLS** | PostgreSQL row-level security |\n| **Slippy map** | Tiled web map addressed by z/x/y |\n| **Tile matrix set** | Definition of a tile pyramid (origin, scales, tile size) |\n| **TMS** | Tile scheme with y increasing northwards (flipped vs XYZ) |\n| **WFS-T** | WFS with transactional operations |\n| **Web Mercator** | EPSG:3857, the spherical Mercator used by web maps |\n| **XYZ** | Tile addressing with origin at the top-left, y increasing southwards |\n\n","lineStart":1779,"lineEnd":1803,"html":"<h2>9.2 Glossary</h2>\n<div class=\"source-table\" role=\"region\" tabindex=\"0\" aria-label=\"Table in 9.2 Glossary\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Term</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Buffer (tile)</strong></td>\n<td>Extra margin of geometry kept beyond the tile edge to avoid seams</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Cluster</strong></td>\n<td>Aggregate marker representing several nearby points</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>CQL2</strong></td>\n<td>Common Query Language 2, the filter language used by OGC API Part 3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>CRS84</strong></td>\n<td>WGS 84 with lon,lat axis order (the default CRS of OGC API – Features)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Extent (MVT)</strong></td>\n<td>Size of the tile-local integer grid (default 4096)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>GeoWebCache</strong></td>\n<td>Tile cache/proxy for WMS layers, embedded in GeoServer</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>GiST</strong></td>\n<td>Generalised Search Tree, PostgreSQL index type used for geometries</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>LOD</strong></td>\n<td>Level of detail: less detail at smaller scales</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Martin</strong></td>\n<td>Rust tile server for PostGIS, MBTiles and PMTiles</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>MBTiles</strong></td>\n<td>SQLite container format for tiles</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>MVT</strong></td>\n<td>Mapbox Vector Tile, a Protobuf-encoded vector tile format</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Optimistic locking</strong></td>\n<td>Detecting conflicting edits by comparing a version/ETag instead of holding locks</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>PMTiles</strong></td>\n<td>Single-file tile archive designed for static hosting with range requests</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>RLS</strong></td>\n<td>PostgreSQL row-level security</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Slippy map</strong></td>\n<td>Tiled web map addressed by z/x/y</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Tile matrix set</strong></td>\n<td>Definition of a tile pyramid (origin, scales, tile size)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>TMS</strong></td>\n<td>Tile scheme with y increasing northwards (flipped vs XYZ)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>WFS-T</strong></td>\n<td>WFS with transactional operations</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Web Mercator</strong></td>\n<td>EPSG:3857, the spherical Mercator used by web maps</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>XYZ</strong></td>\n<td>Tile addressing with origin at the top-left, y increasing southwards</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>","sha256":"1f012e86d9f70b7b2656dbb7cda3a3399fb322b154bbb990c82ff25d3d001114"},{"key":"9.3","id":"9-3","title":"9.3 Further reading (authoritative sources)","anchor":"93-further-reading-authoritative-sources","depth":3,"start":100254,"topics":["capstone"],"end":101552,"markdown":"### 9.3 Further reading (authoritative sources)\n\nDocumentation changes quickly; always check the version you deploy.\n\n- MapLibre GL JS documentation: https://maplibre.org/maplibre-gl-js/docs/\n- MapLibre Style Specification: https://maplibre.org/maplibre-style-spec/\n- OpenLayers: https://openlayers.org/ · Leaflet: https://leafletjs.com/ · deck.gl: https://deck.gl/ · CesiumJS: https://cesium.com/platform/cesiumjs/\n- Mapbox Vector Tile specification: https://github.com/mapbox/vector-tile-spec\n- PostGIS reference: https://postgis.net/docs/ (see `ST_AsMVT`, `ST_AsMVTGeom`, `ST_TileEnvelope`)\n- pg_tileserv: https://github.com/CrunchyData/pg_tileserv · pg_featureserv: https://github.com/CrunchyData/pg_featureserv · Martin: https://github.com/maplibre/martin\n- Tippecanoe: https://github.com/felt/tippecanoe · tilemaker: https://github.com/systemed/tilemaker · PMTiles: https://docs.protomaps.com/pmtiles/\n- GeoServer: https://docs.geoserver.org/ · GeoWebCache: https://www.geowebcache.org/ · MapServer: https://mapserver.org/ · QGIS Server: https://docs.qgis.org/latest/en/docs/server_manual/\n- OGC API – Features: https://ogcapi.ogc.org/features/ · OGC API – Tiles: https://ogcapi.ogc.org/tiles/\n- PostgreSQL row security policies: https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/ddl-rowsecurity.html\n\n","lineStart":1804,"lineEnd":1818,"html":"<h2>9.3 Further reading (authoritative sources)</h2>\n<p>Documentation changes quickly; always check the version you deploy.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>MapLibre GL JS documentation: <a href=\"https://maplibre.org/maplibre-gl-js/docs/\">https://maplibre.org/maplibre-gl-js/docs/</a></li>\n<li>MapLibre Style Specification: <a href=\"https://maplibre.org/maplibre-style-spec/\">https://maplibre.org/maplibre-style-spec/</a></li>\n<li>OpenLayers: <a href=\"https://openlayers.org/\">https://openlayers.org/</a> · Leaflet: <a href=\"https://leafletjs.com/\">https://leafletjs.com/</a> · deck.gl: <a href=\"https://deck.gl/\">https://deck.gl/</a> · CesiumJS: <a href=\"https://cesium.com/platform/cesiumjs/\">https://cesium.com/platform/cesiumjs/</a></li>\n<li>Mapbox Vector Tile specification: <a href=\"https://github.com/mapbox/vector-tile-spec\">https://github.com/mapbox/vector-tile-spec</a></li>\n<li>PostGIS reference: <a href=\"https://postgis.net/docs/\">https://postgis.net/docs/</a> (see <code>ST_AsMVT</code>, <code>ST_AsMVTGeom</code>, <code>ST_TileEnvelope</code>)</li>\n<li>pg_tileserv: <a href=\"https://github.com/CrunchyData/pg_tileserv\">https://github.com/CrunchyData/pg_tileserv</a> · pg_featureserv: <a href=\"https://github.com/CrunchyData/pg_featureserv\">https://github.com/CrunchyData/pg_featureserv</a> · Martin: <a href=\"https://github.com/maplibre/martin\">https://github.com/maplibre/martin</a></li>\n<li>Tippecanoe: <a href=\"https://github.com/felt/tippecanoe\">https://github.com/felt/tippecanoe</a> · tilemaker: <a href=\"https://github.com/systemed/tilemaker\">https://github.com/systemed/tilemaker</a> · PMTiles: <a href=\"https://docs.protomaps.com/pmtiles/\">https://docs.protomaps.com/pmtiles/</a></li>\n<li>GeoServer: <a href=\"https://docs.geoserver.org/\">https://docs.geoserver.org/</a> · GeoWebCache: <a href=\"https://www.geowebcache.org/\">https://www.geowebcache.org/</a> · MapServer: <a href=\"https://mapserver.org/\">https://mapserver.org/</a> · QGIS Server: <a href=\"https://docs.qgis.org/latest/en/docs/server_manual/\">https://docs.qgis.org/latest/en/docs/server_manual/</a></li>\n<li>OGC API – Features: <a href=\"https://ogcapi.ogc.org/features/\">https://ogcapi.ogc.org/features/</a> · OGC API – Tiles: <a href=\"https://ogcapi.ogc.org/tiles/\">https://ogcapi.ogc.org/tiles/</a></li>\n<li>PostgreSQL row security policies: <a href=\"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/ddl-rowsecurity.html\">https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/ddl-rowsecurity.html</a></li>\n</ul>\n","sha256":"0722b6f9925444e1f7fed73132f29a7c9eb47011f4d116360e44eb4a7eb7d6c0"},{"key":"9.4","id":"9-4","title":"9.4 Verification status of this note","anchor":"94-verification-status-of-this-note","depth":3,"start":101552,"topics":["capstone"],"end":102379,"markdown":"### 9.4 Verification status of this note\n\n- The tile-math values, the Exercise 1 module, `bench.py` (syntax) and `purge.py` were executed when this note was written; the numbers in the tables come from those runs.\n- The SQL, server configuration, FastAPI code and browser code follow the documented behaviour of PostGIS, pg_tileserv, pg_featureserv, Martin, MapLibre GL JS and OpenLayers, but were **not** run against live servers while writing. Run the checklist in Section 5.8 first; if something differs, check the release notes of the versions you installed (default ports, schema rules such as `postgisftw`, and `setTiles` availability are the likeliest differences).\n- OGC API – Features Part 4 status and per-server support were checked against current OGC and project pages at the time of writing and may have changed.\n","lineStart":1819,"lineEnd":1823,"html":"<h2>9.4 Verification status of this note</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>The tile-math values, the Exercise 1 module, <code>bench.py</code> (syntax) and <code>purge.py</code> were executed when this note was written; the numbers in the tables come from those runs.</li>\n<li>The SQL, server configuration, FastAPI code and browser code follow the documented behaviour of PostGIS, pg_tileserv, pg_featureserv, Martin, MapLibre GL JS and OpenLayers, but were <strong>not</strong> run against live servers while writing. Run the checklist in Section 5.8 first; if something differs, check the release notes of the versions you installed (default ports, schema rules such as <code>postgisftw</code>, and <code>setTiles</code> availability are the likeliest differences).</li>\n<li>OGC API – Features Part 4 status and per-server support were checked against current OGC and project pages at the time of writing and may have changed.</li>\n</ul>\n","sha256":"4910f07314eadb1fcae44e9e28b76b001b2810561397a2a9121cef27b30c83e3"}]};
